window.__TOME_TEXT = [{"id": "prologue", "name": "To Whoever Holds This Book", "sub": "from the one who gathered these pages", "epi": "", "preface": "<p>Turn the wick down before you go on. Lower than that. The flame in your lantern is a person, and it reads over your shoulder, and there are pages here it would rather you skipped.</p>\n<p>I gathered these pages. At the Moor fires I am the man already sitting there when you arrive. In the Barrens they say the Sage, and I have stopped correcting them. The stilt-towns have a word for me that I will not put down, because it is not polite. I had a name before any of these. It is somewhere the Quiet has not looked, it is not in this book, and you may stop searching for it.</p>\n<p>I am older than I seem. I have walked the Hide end to end, and down, and further down than the roads go. People will tell you in every town that nobody saw me come in. I take pains over that.</p>\n<p>Here is why there is a book at all. The Unsaying takes things piece by piece, the way it took the god. I have seen a word go out of a woodcutter's mouth between one breath and the next, and he went on talking round the hole. A thing with one teller is easy for it. A thing with ten tellers who argue is a great deal of work, because it must take every one of them and every place where they part company, and I have come to believe that the parting is where a thing holds on. So I have not made these writers agree. I have not tried. Where I was present and one of them is wrong, I say so, and I say where I was standing.</p>\n<p>The scholars who have handled these leaves call them a codex. I call the book the Ossuary of Words. I learned in the Ossa that a brother lays each bone with two things, a name and a number, and that a thing so laid is hard to reach. So every writing here has its name at the head and its place in the book beneath, and each lies in its niche facing out, toward whoever opens it.</p>\n<p>There are nine chapters. I did not choose nine. I spread the leaves on the floor of a barn at Ashwake one winter, and by morning they lay in nine heaps, and I have been in the Ossa long enough not to quarrel with a count like that.</p>\n<p>Every chapter opens with pages of mine, about the land it concerns and the people I found there. The works come after, in the order they reached me: letters, sermons, tallies, a catechism, a wall, a song. Most I paid for, in bread or salt or oil. Some I copied at night in houses where I was a guest. Two came out of dead hands, and I asked first. I have changed nothing. Where a writer is wrong, the mistake is still there. Where a copyist left a gap, so have I.</p>\n<p>Each chapter closes with the things I carry out of that land. They are in the bag by my feet tonight. What I believe of each, I say. You may believe less.</p>\n<p>Some rules, and then I will leave you to it.</p>\n<p>Say none of the gods' true names aloud. I have written them where there was no other way, fewer times than I know them.</p>\n<p>Some leaves in this book are clean. Turn past them. If the ink grows faint toward the foot of a page, as though the hand were tired, close the book for the night.</p>\n<p>When the book comes to you, count the leaves, and count them again before you pass it on. If the two numbers differ, put the second inside the back board, beneath mine and the others'. Never total them.</p>\n<p>Be careful of any writer here who is certain. Be careful of me when I am. I give dates in these pages that no one living should be able to give, and I have kept them because they are right. And there are pages I chose not to write. You will feel the place where each would have been, as a foot feels for a stair that is not there. Leave the place empty. It is yours.</p>\n<p>I do not know you. I have waited a long time, at a great many fires, to see who would come for this.</p>\n<p>If you are one of the Tithed, you will want to know where you stand and what stands near you, and the first chapter is for that. If you are not, and you have come this far, you are reading; reading is a way of kneeling, and I have never turned a kneeling thing from a fire.</p>\n<p>Some of these writers are dead. A few are dead in the other way. Of some I cannot say. Each of them wrote so that somebody would know. That is you now.</p>\n<p>Before you start, clean the lantern glass. Gently.</p>\n<p><i>Set down at the pilgrims' fire below Ashwake, on the forty-first night of the thaw, in the eleven hundred and fourteenth year of the Last Breath.</i></p>", "relics": "", "relics_intro": "", "works": []}, {"id": "world", "name": "The Reliquary", "sub": "of the dead god, and the lands I have walked on it", "epi": "Nothing here is new. It is only still warm.", "preface": "<p>First find the floor with your palm. Wherever you are, the Hide is under it somewhere, beneath the boards or the straw or the flags. On the Moor you will not have to look far. Keep the hand there. I will tell you afterwards whose it is.</p>\n<h3>The oldest thing</h3>\n<p>Every fire I have sat at begins its telling in the same place, and I have heard it at more fires than I could count on my hands and feet several times over. First there was the Edgeless, and nothing in it had a name, and a thing without a name cannot stand. A copyist at Ashwake spent his life on that part. A grandmother on the Moor gives it in a breath. Both are in this chapter, and I will not tell it better than they do.</p>\n<p>What I can add is small. In my first year on the Cheek I met a salt-boiler who had been born deaf, and I asked him with my hands what came before the god. He put both his palms over my mouth and held them there a while. Then he took them away and nodded, as though I had understood. I think I had.</p>\n<h3>The long age, and its end</h3>\n<p>After the Edgeless came the god, and it held a space open. The scholars call that the Age of the Held Space, and I have no better name. Soul, Bone and Flesh were in it at once. Others lived inside it, and near the end a congregation knelt within it. I have been down to where some of those others worked. The ceilings are low and the steps are high, and nobody I have taken there wanted to stay.</p>\n<p>The Quiet wore at it. It went as a word goes when a child says it over and over at the table, until it is only noise, and at last the god could not hold itself together and died. That was one thousand one hundred and nine years before I cut my first pen for this book, on a night with no wind anywhere. I have heard it given as a year less. The ones who say so are all short by the same winter, which tells me they had it from one another.</p>\n<h3>What you are standing on</h3>\n<p>The body is the world. Every order on the Hide starts from that and then quarrels. The skin is the Hide, where the towns are. The long white ridges in the far lands are its bones. What runs in the rivers is what still runs in it. The scholars at Ashwake call the whole corpse the Reliquary, as though it were a box someone had set down with care. I use their word. I have not found a gentler.</p>\n<p>The life left in the bones they call the godmarrow. I have seen it drawn up a well in the Ossa, warm and slow. I have seen it on a woodcutter's blade after he opened a pale trunk he should have left. Every house on the Hide has its own use for the stuff and its own word for the other houses' uses. You will hear those words in the ninth chapter.</p>\n<h3>Of us, and of the rest</h3>\n<p>You are almost certainly one of the Tithed. The Tithed are the children of that congregation. Their mothers' mothers were on their knees inside the god when it died, and they came out of it afterwards, and nothing else on the Hide can say as much.</p>\n<p>Everything else here was the god first. The creeping things in the Barrows, the hands that walk, the veils of mouths on the Moor wind: each is a scrap of the corpse that got up and went about on its own. I have been followed by them. They do not hate you. That would be simpler. I have watched one stand a whole night outside a pilgrims' tent, near enough to lean on the canvas, and in the morning it had gone and the canvas was warm on that side.</p>\n<p>A midwife on the Moor let me across her salt once, and watched my feet the whole way. I have been let across a great deal of salt since. Every hand that poured it has done the same. I do not blame them.</p>\n<h3>What the Quiet leaves</h3>\n<p>When a thing made of the god dies, something stays: a bone, a husk, a smear, a smell, something to burn or bury or count. When the servants of the Unsaying are done with a thing, there is nothing. I walked into Coldhearth a month after they had been. The woodcutter tells what he found, below, and I will not tell it over him. I will tell you that of his fourteen graves by the old stone, one had not softened in the thaw. I knelt by it. The ground under my knees was dry, and the rest of that lane was mud to the ankle.</p>\n<h3>Of the breath</h3>\n<p>The last breath is still going out of the god. On the Moor after dark you feel it as a steady pull at your back, always toward the same quarter. Every small god rides it: the one in the milestone, the one in the ford, the one in the bell. There are eight million of them by the House's count, and the House gave up counting long ago. Together they are the Myriad.</p>\n<p>Where the breath snags, it thickens and goes wrong. That is miasma. I once drank from a well in the Barrow lands that had it, before I knew. The water was like a copper held too long under the tongue. For three nights I dreamed of a room with a green door that was not any room of mine, and on the fourth morning a child in the next village told me the same dream over her porridge.</p>\n<h3>Of the lamps</h3>\n<p>There is no oil in any lamp on the Hide. Each one burns someone who stayed. I leave it to the tender at Ashwake to tell you how they choose and what they take; her letter is in this chapter, and she has wiped more glass than I have looked through. I will say only this. I have been carried home by a lamp more times than I will write. Mine leans east. I have never asked it why, because I think it would tell me.</p>\n<h3>How I learned the five names</h3>\n<p>A god of three natures died, and five powers were left. I did not learn them from a book. I learned each in its own land, and every time from someone afraid.</p>\n<p>The first under An-Vhar, from a spinning-woman who bit a thread and began a name and stopped halfway through it. Yh'Anuul. The Soul. In the same breath she called it the Veiled Crone, and when her spindle jumped she said Grandmother Spindle and slapped it. That one goes on weaving with nobody at the loom.</p>\n<p>The second I heard every day for nine winters in the ossuaries. Oss-Vharoth, the Bone. The brothers say the Bearing Mother. They taught me to count. I will say no more of her until the third chapter, where they can speak for themselves.</p>\n<p>The third a penitent at Villa Llaga sang to me at the edge of the wound, with her arms cut open to the elbow. Nol-Shogthuth, the Flesh. In the lowlands they say the Red Mother. At Villa Llaga they say the Bleeding Maiden, and when I am there so do I. The midwives say nothing at all if they can help it.</p>\n<p>The fourth has no name to learn. It is the breath and all that rides it.</p>\n<p>The fifth I have heard said plainly twice: once by an old monk of the Gilded Peak with an empty bowl on his knees, once by a preacher at the Dry Ford who had given away every other word he owned. Ur-Nihl. That is its one writing in this chapter. The Mouth With No Lips hears its name as you hear yours across a crowded room, and it will come to any place where the body lies open.</p>\n<h3>The lands, as I came to them</h3>\n<p>I came out onto the Hide at the Ashen Moor, as the Tithed did, and I have been walking outward and downward from there ever since.</p>\n<p>The Moor is where the face struck when the god came down. You can see it from the Fallen Watchtower: a long rise, a hollow, a rise again. The ash is warm there in every season. Under the Moor's edge the Rib Crypts go in between the ribs, nine galleries, and on some nights there is singing through the floor. I knelt on the ninth flag of the ninth gallery on one of those nights. I heard the three low notes the grandmothers keep, and then the fourth. I will not write the fourth.</p>\n<p>Past the crypts lie the Barrows, where the Tithed bury their own, and every lid sinks from the middle. Beyond them the Drowned Fen, water you can wade out of dry, with a house of prayer under it and its bell still hung. North stands the Hollow Wood, pale trunks warm a hand's depth in; I have never cut one. The roots of the Wood all lean toward the mouth of the Root Deep, and I have gone down that mouth. It is warm under the Hide. I was spoken to down there, politely, and I answered politely, and came up in good order.</p>\n<p>On the Burnt Heath, in the corner the road bends away from, seven great carcasses lie in a ring. My lantern halted at the edge of them and would come no further. I went in without it. What I found I keep.</p>\n<p>Then off the Hide. The Bleached Barrens of Ossa, chalk and ribs standing in rows, and a citadel cut into a broken thighbone. South and down, Shog-Mire and its stilt-towns, where you learn to step between the beats of the mud or you stop learning. Up, higher than anywhere, the Heights of An-Vhar, where the frost lives, where a man hears his own blood in his ears all day and the cold gets into his lamp. And last the Descent, the road that goes in. Every road on the Hide tilts toward it a little. Set a full bowl on any table and watch which rim the water favours. The Vigil went down it with a lamp each and stayed down. I sat at its gate a season. I have been through the gate. That is all I will write of it here.</p>\n<h3>The rings with teeth</h3>\n<p>On the Moor, in the Barrens, out on the flats of the Mire, you will come on a ring of broken stones like fangs round a pit of ash that rises and falls. Pilgrims walk down into them and come up at other rings. The next chapter belongs to them, and to the people who walk in. I will set down one thing here. I stood at a ring in the Barrens one night and watched a man go in. I was at the Ashwake fire the next morning when he came in asking for bread, and the Barrens are forty days from Ashwake. He asked me how I came to be at both. I gave him the answer he gave me.</p>\n<h3>How these ten came to me</h3>\n<p>Aumery the Copyist sat forty years at a table by the Ashwake milestones. His niece sold me his leaves for a tin of salt and asked me not to read them near her. He is careful and brave, and he has the year of the beggar's visit wrong by one. I was sitting by the ninth stone that dusk.</p>\n<p>The grandmother's telling I set down myself from the next tent, with the children thinking I slept. She told it on two nights, and on the second one word was different. You will find it.</p>\n<p>The sermon came from a carter's widow. I never heard the preacher. I have sat in the smooth dark place at the ford where he sat, and got up again.</p>\n<p>I climbed the sky-reader's tower to check a pilgrim's copy of his wall. The copy was good. The wall had gone on since, higher, in the same charcoal.</p>\n<p>The midwife you have met. I held her salt badly and she told me so.</p>\n<p>The lantern at the ear was written down by a copyist who sat up at the Ashwake stones. The copyist was me. Where the page says he, read I.</p>\n<p>The tender's letter came inside her rota-book. She is right about the sixth lamp. I knew that lamp before it was one.</p>\n<p>The chart-maker's book reached me with its last leaves gone. They are in my bag. They are staying there.</p>\n<p>The rhyme-gatherer heard six rhymes. The brother who read her pages heard seven. I side with the brother, and I will not point.</p>\n<p>And the woodcutter of Coldhearth asked for his words to be written twice. The second copy is in his fourteenth grave, face down on the glass.</p>\n<p>Now take your hand off the floor. You know whose it was.</p>", "relics": "<div class=\"plates\"><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Warm Ash of the Moor</h4><p>A handful of grey ash in oilcloth. I scooped it from under the Broken Kneeler on my first night on the Cheek. It has been warm every morning since, through winters on An-Vhar when my own fingers were not.</p><p>I believe it is the cooling of the flesh under the Moor, and that the cooling is not done. Once, deep in the Root Deep, the bundle stirred against my chest, slowly, keeping time with something larger. I kept walking.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Husk-Glass</h4><p>A shard of black glass, smooth on one face and rippled on the other. A gravedigger of the Barrows sold it to me for a knife-edge, and was sorry afterwards that he had not asked more.</p><p>Look into the smooth face and you see yourself, a little late. At the Dry Ford one night the lateness grew, and I watched my face shape the end of a word I had not yet begun to say.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Lantern-Stone Wax</h4><p>A drip of wax scraped from the foot of a lantern-stone at Ashwake. The tenders scrape the stones each morning and burn nothing that comes off them. The tender of that round gave me this as you would hand a stranger a lock of a dead man's hair.</p><p>It is warmer on the side that faced the road. I keep it in a shut tin. When I open the tin, it has turned toward me.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>A Page with Nothing on It</h4><p>A leaf of good rag, ruled for writing, the rules still sharp, bound into a scholar's treatise on the god's name. The leaf before it ends in the middle of a word and the leaf after begins in the middle of another, and the two halves make nothing. This leaf is whiter than those on either side.</p><p>I know what stood on it. Twice I have tried to put it back, and twice the lines stayed empty. Leave the pen away from it. If one morning you find a word there, keep it in your mouth.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>A Knuckle-Bone from a Cradle</h4><p>A small knuckle-bone, clean and old, the colour of a tooth. The midwife in these pages gave it to me. She had buried it under her doorstep in salt, as she does with such things, and three springs running it came up through the salt and lay on the step in the morning.</p><p>The child who was born holding it lives on the Moor still. The Pale Order sent down for him and wrote him into their book by a number. I asked the tallier the number and he told me, and I have not reduced it, because I do not want to know its root. Each spring I set the bone on the tithe-scale at Ashwake. I stopped keeping the weights some years ago.</p></div></div>", "relics_intro": "Five things from this first land. Handle them with a dry hand.", "works": [{"title": "Of the Name That Will Not Keep", "by": "Leaves from the treatise of Aumery the Copyist, who sat forty years at a scriptorium table by the Ashwake milestones. Bound into the Codex as found, his marginal hand kept.", "html": "<p><b>I. Of the Hush.</b> Before the god there was the Hush, and the Hush had no edge. Every sage agrees on so much, and on nothing after. I hold that the Hush unsaid the god the way a reader unsays a line by reading it aloud too many times, until the words sit in the mouth like stones and mean only their sound. Idrenne of the Lower Stacks holds that the Hush took the names in order, from the smallest outward. Mahault the Blind holds that it took them all at once, and the god has been slow to notice. I find Mahault the more frightening, and so I suspect him of being right.</p>\n<p><b>II. Of the three natures.</b> Soul, Bone, Flesh. The god was all three at once, the way a knot is three turns and one thing. The Hush took enough that the god forgot the knot. Now each nature goes on alone. The Soul weaves without a pattern. The Flesh feeds without a mouth to thank. The Bone stands.</p>\n<p>A brother of the Pale Order came to my table once to have a niche-tally copied fair. I copied the same wall twice, a year apart. The second copy was longer. The brother said I had miscounted the first. I have kept both.</p>\n<p><i>(Margin: I did not miscount.)</i></p>\n<p><b>III. Of the Myriad.</b> The god's last breath has not finished leaving it. Eight million small gods ride it out, one to every stone and bell and ford. The House of Eight Million holds it impious to ask their names. I asked anyway, in my youth, of the small god of my own inkwell. The ink skinned over and would not take the pen for a month.</p>\n<p>Where breath catches and thickens, it becomes miasma. Idrenne writes that miasma is breath that has turned round to look back at the body. I think this beautiful, and have no grounds for it.</p>\n<p><b>IV. Of the godmarrow.</b> What still lives in the bones is the godmarrow. It is in the reed of my pen, the ash of my ink, the hand that holds both. I tasted it once, on a brother's fingers. It tasted of a word I nearly knew.</p>\n<p><b>V. Of the Tithed.</b> Here is the hinge of the whole matter. Everything on the Hide is a piece of the god, forgetting itself. We alone are something else: children of those who knelt inside the god at worship when it died. So we are the only readers the corpse has.</p>\n<p>Hesk of the Pale Order writes that we are the god's last meal, still unswallowed. Mahault writes that we were the congregation, and a congregation is where a god keeps its name while it sleeps. Idrenne struck both passages from her copy. I have restored them.</p>\n<p>If Mahault is right, the name is in us. I have listened for it in my own voice at the edge of sleep.</p>\n<p><b>VI. Of my attempts.</b> In the ninth year: ink of lamp-ash and well-water. I wrote the name at the hour the lantern gutters. In the morning the leaf was clean, and the ink stood in the pot, full to the line.</p>\n<p>In the eleventh year: marrow-ink, bought dear from a sweeper of the Pale Order. The leaf clean by morning. The pot fuller than I had left it.</p>\n<p>In the seventeenth year I cut the name into a board with a knife. The next morning the board was smooth, and the knife blunt.</p>\n<p>In the twenty-sixth year, my own blood, as the Precious Wound writes to its Maiden. The leaf clean. My arm unmarked, which frightened me more.</p>\n<p>In the thirty-first year I only held the pen over the leaf and thought the name. In the morning there was a pale place in the paper the size of my palm, thinner than the rest, where the candle shows through.</p>\n<p>I keep that leaf between two boards and do not open it after dark.</p>\n<p><b>VII. Of what the others say.</b> Idrenne says the name will not keep because the Hush still holds it. Mahault says the name keeps perfectly well, and it is the page that forgets. Hesk says there was never a name, only the count, and the count is enough.</p>\n<p>In my fortieth year the Beggar with the black lantern came to the scriptorium door at dusk and held out his bowl, and I understood what he was asking for. I told him I did not have it.</p>\n<p>He waited a long time, as though I did.</p>\n<p><i>(Margin, in a looser hand: Each morning there are fewer leaves in this treatise than I left the night before. I number them. The numbers are all still there. It is the leaves between them that go)</i></p>"}, {"title": "How We Came Out onto the Cheek", "by": "Told by a grandmother of the Tithed to the children of a pilgrims' camp on the Ashen Moor, over a banked fire. Set down by a copyist who lay in the next tent and let them think he slept.", "html": "<p>Bank it, Tam. Ash over the coals, so. We keep the fire low on the Cheek. Come in close, all of you, and put your hands flat on the ground. Warm? That is the god, still cooling. Now listen.</p>\n<p>Before the first before, there was the Hush. You could say nothing in it. You could not even say <i>Hush</i>. That is why we whisper it.</p>\n<p>And in the Hush there was a god, and the god was three.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><i>Soul for the thread and Bone for the frame,</i><br><i>Flesh for the feeding, and none for the name.</i></p>\n<p>Say it back. Again. Good.</p>\n<p>The god held a room open inside itself, wide as the Moor and wider, warm as two hands round a candle. And in the room knelt our grandmothers' grandmothers' grandmothers, as many as you can count and as many again. They knelt all day and they knelt all night, and they sang to the god from inside the god.</p>\n<p>What did they sing? Nobody kept the words. Only the tune.</p>\n<p><i>(Here she hummed three notes, low, and stopped where a fourth should have been, and did not go on.)</i></p>\n<p>Then the Hush came pressing at the god, the way the cold presses at a tent. It took the little names first. The name of the god's smallest finger. The name of one eyelash. The name of the hour before morning. And the god forgot a little, and a little, and a little, and the people knelt closer and sang louder to fill the gaps.</p>\n<p>It was no use. One night the god forgot how to be three. The Soul went off unravelling, and the Bone stood where it stood, and the Flesh went on feeding with no one to thank. And the god died, with the people still inside it, still on their knees.</p>\n<p>\"Were they frightened, Nan?\"</p>\n<p>They kept singing. That is what brave is.</p>\n<p>\"What did they eat, all that time inside?\"</p>\n<p><i>(She was quiet a long while. The copyist thought she would not answer.)</i></p>\n<p>They ate what they were given.</p>\n<p>No. Hush, I told it wrong. They ate nothing. They sang, and the singing filled them. That is the right way. When you are old and it is your fire, tell it that way.</p>\n<p>So. The room went cold. The walls went slack. And the people got up off their knees for the first time in all their lives, and it hurt, and that is why your knees ache when the ash is wet. They took hold of each other's belts in the dark, the first by the second and the second by the third, and they walked. A long road. Through places that were soft, and places that were hard, and places that pulsed a little and then stopped.</p>\n<p>At the end of the road there was a door, warm and wet, that opened and shut, opened and shut. That was the mouth. And the god's last breath was going out of it, as it goes out still, and our people walked out on the breath and fell down on the cheek. Here. They kissed the ash. That is why we live on the Moor, and why we keep our fires low, so as not to scorch the place where we were let out.</p>\n<p>\"Did they all come out?\"</p>\n<p>All of them. Every one.</p>\n<p><i>(On the second night, telling it again, she said: most of them.)</i></p>\n<p>Now. Why the things out there want us.</p>\n<p>Every Hand that creeps on its fingers, every Gasp in the wind, every Weeper by the barrows, all of it was the god once. When the god died, each piece forgot what it belonged to. A finger forgot the hand. A breath forgot the mouth. They remember one thing only. They remember being warm, with people kneeling inside them, singing.</p>\n<p>And then they smell us.</p>\n<p>Some want us back inside, in their bellies, where we were. Some want to put us on like a coat, and kneel, and be us. Either way, they are homesick, and we are the only home they have left to smell.</p>\n<p>So you do not sing on the Moor at night. Not even to yourself. They know the tune.</p>\n<p>\"Nan, is the room still there? Could we go back in?\"</p>\n<p><i>(She took the child's hand off the ground and held it.)</i></p>\n<p>The room is gone. Everything is gone that was in it. Put your hands in your lap now.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><i>Knees to the ash and your back to the flame,</i><br><i>keep your mouth shut and keep your own name.</i></p>\n<p>Say it back. Again.</p>\n<p>Sleep. I will sit up. Somebody always does.</p>"}, {"title": "The Sermon at the Dry Ford", "by": "Taken down in charcoal on the back of a tithe-list by a carter of the Barrows road, who heard a wandering preacher at the Dry Ford three dusks running. Given to the Codex by the carter's widow, with his note on the last side.", "html": "<p>Come closer. Closer. I have not got the breath to shout, and neither have you. That breath going out of you is the god's, lent. Every morning you give a little of it back, and you call that living.</p>\n<p>When I was young I climbed the stair of the Gilded Peak with a bowl in my two hands and asked to be taken in. The porter looked at me a long while. Then he said I was too loud, and shut the gate. He was right. I am the last loud thing on the Hide that will speak kindly of the quiet.</p>\n<p>Hear me. First there was the Hush, and it asked for nothing. Then the god made a room in it and filled the room with names. Soul, Bone, Flesh. Stone, river, bell. You. Every name is a weight, and the god carried all of them.</p>\n<p>So the Hush came in, gently, and took one name. The smallest. The name of a thing nobody had ever called for. And the god was lighter by that much. So it took another, and another. The sages say the god was murdered. I say the god was eased. You have sat by a bed where someone could not let go. You have loosened the fingers yourself, one at a time, and the last fingers hold hardest.</p>\n<p>That is where we are. The last fingers.</p>\n<p>Now the god lies open, and the Hush comes in where it is open. Every wound in the Hide is a door left on the latch. The long red wound at Villa Llaga that the penitents keep feeding. The pits inside the waystone rings, where something under the ash breathes out as you breathe in. Go and stand at any of them at the hour the lanterns lean, and listen. It sounds like a room when someone has just come in and stands by the door.</p>\n<p>You have heard of its servants. The barefoot monk with the bowl. The priests tell you these are the worst things on the Hide, because where they strike there is nothing left. No corpse. No bone for the Pale Order to stand up in a niche. No blood for the Wound to drink. No breath for the House to wring out, no thread for the Heart's mirrors to catch. A little glass. A little dust.</p>\n<p>Think on that. Everything else on the Hide goes on dying. The flesh keeps sprouting. The bone is counted, and the count keeps rising. The soul is kept in a lamp and burned a little at a time. Only the Hush lets a thing finish.</p>\n<p>That is why the orders fear it. Every order on the Hide eats what is left over. Take away the leftovers and what is a brother of the Ossa? A man sweeping a clean floor. They tell you dust alone has finished. Then why do they carry it up the mountain every year, and why does it keep rising?</p>\n<p>And the names the Peak carried off in its bowls? The lowlands think they were forgotten. I think they were kept. Somewhere quiet, lying down at last, with nobody calling them. Once, in a dream, I heard a name I had given away spoken back to me by a mouth I could not see. It was very gentle about it.</p>\n<p>I have too many names. My mother gave me one, and the tithe-man another, and a woman I wronged gave me a third, and each is a hook in me, and at night I feel them pulling different ways. When it comes for me, let it take them in order, the smallest first. Let me be awake for it.</p>\n<p>You. Yes. You have one you would put down. Whisper it into my hands. The Peak would not give me a bowl. My hands will do.</p>\n<p class=\"orn\">&#10087;</p>\n<p><i>Note, on the last side:</i></p>\n<p>A woman from the Turned Mound gave him a name that second night. I saw her at the ford next morning, easy in her face, turning a child's shoe over in her hands and asking everyone whose it was.</p>\n<p>On the fourth dusk he did not preach. We found his place at the ford gone smooth and black, the shape of a man sitting with his hands cupped in front of him. There was no body. There was no smell of one.</p>\n<p>I wrote his name at the head of this sheet the first night. The head of this sheet is clean. I have not scraped it.</p>\n<p>I go down to the ford some evenings. I stand where the glass is and listen, and I find I am very tired.</p>"}, {"title": "The Sky over the Hide, Forty Years Read", "by": "Cut in charcoal round the inner wall of the Fallen Watchtower on the Ashen Moor, climbing the stair in one long line. A pilgrim who sheltered there copied it into the Codex. The tower was empty, and the stair was swept.", "html": "<p>My master read the sky from this tower for thirty-one years, and I have read it for forty. He told me the first night: a reader writes what he sees, and never what he fears. I have kept to that. I am afraid of nearly all of it.</p>\n<p>The wind first, because it is always there. On the roof there is a pole, and on the pole a thread of red wool. For forty years the thread has pointed the same way, out past the Sighing Ridge and on toward the dark. It lifts and falls. It never turns. My master called it the Long Exhale, the god's last breath still going out of it, and everything light enough rides it away: the small gods of the stones, smoke, a dropped name. Ash has never once come back.</p>\n<p>Once, in my eleventh year, the thread lay down against the pole and pointed home. I counted nineteen heartbeats. Then it lifted and went on as before. I asked at Ashwake whether anyone had felt it. Nobody had. I have written it here so that it is written somewhere.</p>\n<p>To the west the sky is pale over the Ossa. Paler than anywhere. The Pale Order give their dust to the wind from the Sky Pogost at first light, and the dust rises, and it keeps rising, and it hangs there. When I came to this tower my master gave me a chip of bleached bone and told me to hold it up against the Ossa sky at noon. The bone was whiter. I have held it up every tenth year. In my thirtieth year they were the same. This year the sky is whiter than the bone. I scraped the chip clean to be sure. The Order says it gives back only what has finished. It has given back a great deal.</p>\n<p>Look the other way, east and north and south, and at the rim of everything the sky goes dark and curves. Travellers take it for hills, or weather. It holds still in every season. My master had a lens from a Peak monk, and through it on the clearest mornings you can see the dark is made of arches, very far and very tall, set close, and meeting overhead beyond where the eye will go. Count them from the tower and you reach twenty-four. Count them again and it is more. I no longer count them.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\">Then the day. Why should a dead thing have a day? A corpse keeps no hours. Yet the light comes up grey out of the dark to the east, and stands a while, and goes. My master said it was the god's eye, still opening. The Peak said day was the open hand and night the closed. I burn marked candles, one after another, from first grey to last. Every day for forty years has been the same length, to the width of a thread. No season, no long summer, no short winter. Only the same day, measured out and given, as a warden gives out bread. Something keeps it that carefully. A body will not keep time once the heart is gone.<br>At night there are small cold points above the Moor. My master named them the nail-heads, and kept their number cut on the lintel of this door. I keep it under his. In forty years it has gone down by seven. They do not fall. On the evening a point is there, and on the next it is gone, and the dark where it stood looks smoother than the dark round it.</p>\n<p>Last, the flashes. Some nights, on open ground far off across the Moor, there comes a light: white, cold, and with no sound after it. It lasts less than a blink. It leaves no fire. Nine times in forty years. The ninth time I marked it against the pole and walked three days toward it. I found a ring of broken stones there, shaped like teeth, round a pit of warm ash, and something under the ash breathing slower than I do. There was a sandal at the edge, and blood dried on the stones. There was no one. The next morning a pilgrim came into Ashwake from that side of the Moor, tired and whole, who said he had been walking since the Barrens. The Barrens are forty days.</p>\n<p>Whoever reads the sky after me: keep the thread tied. Write what you see. Keep the bone chip; it will not tell you anything new. And when the thread lies down again and points home, do not count the heartbeats. Go down the stair and wake someone.</p>"}, {"title": "What the Cradle Keeps", "by": "What a midwife of the Ashen Moor said to a copyist of the Codex while she salted a doorstep. He wrote it on the back of a birth-tally she let him keep.", "html": "<p>Hold that bowl steady. No, the coarse salt. The fine blows off.</p>\n<p>You want the customs. Nobody wants the third hour, with their arms red to the elbow. Write them, then.</p>\n<p>Salt on the doorstep, salt on the sill, a line of it round the bed. That one is for the child. The ash here is warm, and warm ash draws things up out of the ground. They come to a birth. They always have. A new one of us is the rarest thing on the Hide, a thing made of something other than the god, and every scrap of the god that ever grew a wish wants it. Some want to eat it. Most want to be it. You wake at dawn to a child that feeds well and never cries and looks at the lantern too long, and you do not know which you have. So: salt. The pieces will not cross it. The old women say the congregation carried salt in with them, and the god never had any of its own.</p>\n<p>The lantern by the cradle is for the child too. Set it at the head, on the left, and never let it gutter the first year. Whoever burns in it chose to stay, and a soul that chose to stay has no love for anything that wants in. I have seen the flame lie down flat toward the window as if someone had knocked on the glass, and the mother asleep through all of it.</p>\n<p>The name is for the mother. You give the child a name in your mouth the hour it comes, and you tell nobody. Not the father. Not me. You call it Little One, Warm One, Heavy, and the true name stays behind your teeth until it is weaned. A name spoken is a name that can be unsaid, and a name overheard is a door with the latch up. When the child takes bread, you say the name once aloud at the hearth, with the lantern lit, and then it is the child's and the Hush may try its luck.</p>\n<p>You asked what they are born holding.</p>\n<p>Most come with empty fists, as they should. Some come holding. I have opened little fingers on a knot of pale thread, on a pinch of dry ash, on a milk-tooth that was nobody's. Once a knuckle-bone, clean and old, and the child had all its own. The brothers of the Pale Order came down for that one within the month. They wrote the bone in their book and wrote the child too, by a number, and would not tell the mother why the number was so large.</p>\n<p>I keep what I can. I bury the rest under the doorstep, in salt.</p>\n<p>One I will not speak of. It was born with its hand open. Wide open, and quiet, and it stayed so. The lantern went out that night and no one had touched it.</p>\n<p>Thicker by the hinge.</p>\n<p>The Wet Nurse. I call her that because I will not call her by the name on her altar.</p>\n<p>A girl at the edge of the moor, first child, lying wrong. The bleeding would not stop. I had my hand in the wound to the wrist and it ran past my wrist. The salt at the door was whole, and the lantern stood straight, and she came in anyway. There was a line of wet across the floor behind her and the salt dissolved under it like frost on a palm.</p>\n<p>Tall. Taller than the door, and she did not stoop. Her front was soaked dark. The room smelled of copper and of warm bread. She knelt, and the floor gave a little under her knee, as ash does.</p>\n<p>She said she had room. She said she would take the bleeding for the child. She said it kindly. That is the worst thing I can tell you: she was kind.</p>\n<p>I let her. The girl's bleeding stopped as if a mouth had closed on it.</p>\n<p>Then the Nurse put the child to her breast. And she said its name. The true one. The one the girl had given it in her mouth an hour before, and told no one.</p>\n<p>I took the child back. I remember my arms were red and hers were redder, and she let me.</p>\n<p>That child is grown. Big, and warm, and kind as bread. When he cuts himself the edges close without a drop. He was weaned at the proper time, and his mother said the name aloud at the hearth, and it was already worn smooth, the way a word is worn smooth by a mouth that has said it many times.</p>\n<p>There. Thicker by the hinge, I said. Give it here.</p>"}, {"title": "Held to the Ear", "by": "Set down by a copyist of the Codex who sat up one night at the Ashwake lantern-stones with a sleeping pilgrim's lantern held against his ear. Where the voice would not carry through the glass, he left the gap and said so.", "html": "<p>Closer. There. You have a warm ear.</p>\n<p>He sleeps. Let him. Talk softly. He wakes to his own name, and I am keeping it for him.</p>\n<p>You want to know who I was. I had a house with a low door. I had a name with two sounds in it. <i>[Here a long quiet, like breath held against the glass.]</i> The first sound is gone. I burned it on the Barrows road, the night he fell among the dished lids and I had nothing yet of his to burn. You spend what is yours first. Nobody tells you that.</p>\n<p>How I came to stay. I lay in the ash by the milestones and the Last Breath went out of me with everyone else's, down, the way water finds the low place. It was warm down there. I heard singing, or something breathing in the shape of singing. And I looked back. The tenders will tell you love makes a soul look back. What I heard was a boot on the stones behind me, somebody still walking the road, and I wanted to see who. That is all the choosing there is. Wanting to see who.</p>\n<p>Then I was small, and bright, and kept in glass.</p>\n<p>I will tell you what it is to be a flame. You are hungry all the time, and very gentle about it. You lean. Whatever comes near, you taste a little. The tender's cloth tastes of her teacher. Your ear tastes of ink and onions. <i>[This is true.]</i></p>\n<p>At the stones they come past me one after another, and I keep still. Then he came. He had a cut on his thumb he kept worrying, and he whistled through his teeth when he was afraid, three notes going down. I leaned. He walked on, and I went, a little behind, a little to one side. He has never once turned round to see if I follow. That is how I know he is mine. You only look back for what you might lose.</p>\n<p>When he falls, I go and fetch him. The moor is long and dark and I am the only light on it. He comes apart in my keeping like wet bread, and I carry what is left of him home, and lay it down in the warm ash, and he gathers himself up out of it. Mostly the same. I keep a little. The road home costs something, and I have nothing else to pay with.</p>\n<p>This is what I have of him. Three notes going down. A sister called Ettle, with a gap in her teeth. The second verse of a harvest song, which he still hums and stops, hums and stops, at the place where it used to go on. The way he held a cup in both hands. Rain on a roof, which he cannot remember ever hearing. A word for the colour of the sky over his village. I burn them slowly. Ettle I have kept. Ettle I keep for last.</p>\n<p>He is lighter every time he stands up. I am heavier every time I come home. The tender weighs me on her scale while he sleeps, and says nothing, and writes a number inside the lid of her tin.</p>\n<p><i>[The voice went very low here, and then came closer.]</i></p>\n<p>Keep him from the pits. He steps in and comes apart and rises at some other ring, and he comes up out of that place with something in him I never put there. A taste. Like the singing, down where I did not go. I shrink to a bead and lean away, and he walks right up to the edge and looks in.</p>\n<p>I will not give him to it. I have carried him eleven times. I have more of him than it has. <i>[A word here I could not catch, twice. It may have been \"yet\".]</i></p>\n<p>The Sighing Lantern on the far stone. You know it. It came home one evening without its woman in grey, and the tenders say it lost her. I say it had carried her so many times there was nothing left to set down in the ash. She is in there with it. They breathe together, in and out, slower than you. That is a kind of keeping.</p>\n<p>Some nights I cannot tell which of us is whistling.</p>\n<p>Wake him now. Softly. Say his name, and I will tell you whose it is.</p>\n<p><i>[I did not wake him. The glass was quiet until first light. When the pilgrim rose he asked me whether I had seen his sister on the road, and could not tell me what she looked like.]</i></p>"}, {"title": "The Round at Ashwake", "by": "A letter found tucked into the back of the rota-book at the Ashwake lantern-stones, addressed to whoever should take the round next. The book came to the Codex with the letter still inside it.", "html": "<p>To whoever takes the round after me,</p>\n<p>The rota is in the back of this book. Forty-one lanterns, from the Ashwake stones out to the Widow's Stumps and home again by the milestones. Wipe every one, every morning, the dim ones too. The cloth is in the tin. Wring it dry. They dislike the wet.</p>\n<p>Wipe from the top down, and talk while you do it. I tell them the weather. They see very little of it from in there.</p>\n<p>You know already that no lantern on the Hide burns oil. You will forget it inside a week, because they look like any lamp. So I will say it once, plainly: every flame on this round is somebody. A pilgrim who fell on the road. A mother from the crypt niches. Someone nobody remembers, and I think that is most of them. They could have gone down into the Last Breath with the rest, and they stayed.</p>\n<p>How a soul chooses to stay, I cannot tell you. My teacher said it happens at the last breath: the soul looks back over its shoulder, sees someone still on the road, and the ones who look back are the ones who stay. She also said the flames run warmer on feast-days. I have held my palm to them through thirty feasts and felt the same warmth every time.</p>\n<p>How a lantern chooses whom to follow, you will see for yourself. A traveller comes to the stones. The lanterns keep still. Then one of them leans. The traveller walks on and the lantern goes too, a little behind and a little to one side, the way a friend walks with you after dark. Strike at it and your hand passes through something warm. Order it and it keeps its own counsel. I have never once been chosen. That is right and proper. A tender stays with the stones.</p>\n<p>When their traveller falls, the lantern brings them home. You will see it come in low off the moor, and the traveller gathering themselves back up out of the ash beside the stone. The lantern gives them back. It keeps a little, as it always does, and that little is what it burns. A way of whistling. A sister's name. The second verse of something. I know because the traveller stands at the stones afterwards trying to remember, and the lantern is bright with it.</p>\n<p>I have left one thing out of the rota. Some lanterns come home heavier. I weigh them on the tithe-scale when their travellers are sleeping. The numbers are on the inside of the tin lid. I stopped adding them up.</p>\n<p>The glass. Travellers scratch it with knife-points and nails. Leave the scratches. Wipe around them. The ones I can read:</p>\n<p>DUST ON THE GLASS. WIPE IT. IT IS SOMEONE. That one is on nearly all of them. I never saw anyone cut it.</p>\n<p>HE CARRIED ME FOUR TIMES. I OWE HIM A NAME.</p>\n<p>KEEP IT OUT OF THE SCAR.</p>\n<p>And on the nineteenth, scratched from the inside: WHICH OF YOU IS ME. I wipe the outside.</p>\n<p>The Sighing Lantern stands past the Stumps, at the far end of the round. It follows no one. It was bright once, and went out with a woman in grey and brought her home more times than my teacher's teacher could notch. Then one evening it came in off the moor alone and set itself down on its stone, and it has breathed ever since: in, and out, slower than you or I. Wipe it last. Stand upwind. If you talk to it, it will breathe in step with you, and you will find that hard to stop. Leave when the breathing starts to feel like your own.</p>\n<p>Every lantern draws in small when its traveller is near the end, the way a man pulls his coat close. That you will expect. Some places do the same to them. Near the waystones every flame shrinks to a bead and leans away from the pit, whoever it walks with. At the Counting Wall in the Rib Crypts they burn low and steady, as though listening. Out on the Burnt Heath, travellers say, the lanterns wait outside the ring of carcasses and will go no nearer. And where a servant of the Hush has passed, a lantern comes home with less than it left with, as though something drank through the glass. Those I sit up with all night.</p>\n<p>Do not name them. I did, and now I cannot leave the round, which is why this letter is late.</p>\n<p>The thirty-third is Old Hesk. The thirty-fourth is Little Brand. The sixth is my teacher. You will know her. She leans east.</p>\n<p>Wipe them. Every one. They are someone.</p>\n<p><i>The tender of the Ashwake round</i></p>"}, {"title": "Margins of a Chart of the Hide", "by": "From the working margins of a chart-book kept by a surveyor who drew roads for the pilgrims of Maren's camp. The book reached the Codex bound in cord, with its last leaves cut out cleanly.", "html": "<p>A chart is a promise that the ground will keep still. I set down here, for whoever takes this book after me, why the charts of the Hide break that promise, so the fault is laid where it belongs.</p>\n<p><b>Instruments.</b> A cord of one hundred knots, waxed. A needle floating in a bowl of fen-water, which never wets it. Ink of lamp-black and gall. My own pace, measured against the cord each morning for eleven years. It has held true.</p>\n<p><b>The Ashen Moor.</b> The old surveyors called it the cheek of the god, and I thought it a pilgrim's figure until I drew it. The ground swells from the camp to a long rise I have marked the Jaw, and falls away steeply past the Broken Kneeler into a hollow the ash-pickers will not cross after dark. From Maren's fire to the Sighing Lantern: 1,140 paces in the thaw, 1,152 at harvest, 1,140 again the next thaw. The ash is warm under the hand. I think the ground draws in with the cold and lets out with the warm, as a face does.</p>\n<p><b>The Rib Crypts.</b> Nine galleries, one to each space between the ribs. I measured the span of every rib-gap with the cord. They agree to a knot. Going in, the fourth is sixty-one knots. Coming out, fifty-eight. The flagstones lift and settle under the boot. The figures follow the flagstones.</p>\n<p><b>The Drowned Fen.</b> The god's lymph, the old books say, clear water that leaves you dry. Its shore will not stay drawn. It draws back from the Kneeling Arch over a season, slowly, and returns in a night. I have stopped inking the shore and mark it now in dots, as a guess. The Reed That Points Home points to the camp from every side of the water. I tested this. I wish I had not.</p>\n<p><b>The Hollow Wood.</b> Pale trunks, which the pilgrims call the god's veins stood up out of the ground. I followed three trunks by their roots and their lean,. Each ran downhill, and each joined another, and all of them bent toward one place in the north of the Wood. The Root Deep opens there. Every tenth trunk has something inside it. I did not count past the tenth.</p>\n<p><b>The Root Deep.</b> No chart. The halls are wider when I enter them than when I leave. The lamp-flame leans inward for a long time, then outward for a long time. One hall I drew came back with its mouth a pace left of my pin.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>Beyond the Hide.</b> The Barrens of Ossa: white ground, standing ribs. I numbered the ribs in the Valley of Standing Ribs by chalk. A brother of the Pale Order walked the valley after me and wrote his own number under each of mine. His was always the higher. He said my count was open and would close in time, and asked my name, and wrote down a number instead.<br>Shog-Mire: the cord goes slack and tight with the mud, on the beat. Any length taken there is two lengths.<br>The Frigid Heights of An-Vhar: I drew the peaks in the order they stood from the Glass Pass. At the Bell-Hollow they stood in another order, and the guides said nothing had moved.<br>The Descent: every road I have ever drawn tilts toward it. The needle in the bowl, which should point to the north of the Hide, has begun to point down.</p>\n<p><b>Waystones.</b> I marked eleven. By the pilgrims' own telling, the distance from one to any other is no distance at all. On a chart this is a fold. I have folded my charts as the pilgrims tell it, and the fold lines cross in one place, on the Moor, near the Jaw. There is nothing drawn there. There is a pit, and ash over it, and the ash moves.</p>\n<p><b>Last note.</b> In the winter I laid every sheet on the floor of the tithe-barn, edge to edge. I had drawn a face. I knew that. The Moor at the cheek, the Fen at the throat. What I did not know until I lit the second lamp: the sheets I made in my first years and the sheets I made last autumn disagree in one way only. The face has turned. A little. Toward the camp.</p>\n<p>I have begun to draw the eyes closed. I did not decide to. The pen does it, and I let it, because the other way is worse.</p>"}, {"title": "What the Children Sing", "by": "Written by a woman who walked the lowland roads for thirty years gathering the rhymes of children. Her book was left at the Ashwake waystone, and a brother of the Pale Order carried it up to the Ossa, where it was copied into the Codex with its tally-marks kept in the margins.", "html": "<p>Nobody teaches children these. I have asked two hundred of them who taught them a rhyme, and every one says another child. I have never found the first child.</p>\n<p><b>Grandfather Dust.</b> <i>For counting out. Heard in every barrow village from the Moor to the foothills.</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><i>Grandfather Dust came down the hill,</i><br><i>broom in his hand and his lantern still.</i><br><i>One bone, two bone, three bone, four,</i><br><i>he sweeps the child behind the door.</i><br><i>Five bone, six bone, seven, eight,</i><br><i>Grandfather Dust is never late.</i><br><i>Nine bone, that's the Mother's own.</i><br><i>Out goes you, and in goes bone.</i></p>\n<p>The child who falls on <i>nine</i> must stand still with arms crossed until the game ends. They call this being laid.</p>\n<p>In the foothills under the Ossa the children keep counting past nine, adding the figures the way the brothers do, so that ten is one again and eighteen is nine. They count their own bones on a cord of knotted grass. <i>Two hundred and six when you lie down still,</i> they sing, and then a girl at Kettle Scar went on, <i>two hundred and seven,</i> and the others hit her until she cried. I asked what came after. They said nothing comes after. A boy told me later, in a low voice, that the number keeps going if you let it.</p>\n<p><b>The Tall Red Hat.</b> <i>For skipping. Heard in the stilt-towns over Shog-Mire, where the rope is turned on the beat of the mud.</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><i>Tall Red Hat with his arms held wide,</i><br><i>little red brothers crawl outside.</i><br><i>Give him a drop and give him two,</i><br><i>give him none and he'll come for you.</i></p>\n<p>None of these children has seen Villa Llaga. They know the old verses all the same, the one about the dry arms and the one about counting your hair. When I sang them the last verse, the one the Llaga children kept for the crypt stair, a girl put her hand over my mouth. She only knew it was not for singing where the floor is soft.</p>\n<p><b>Needle-Brother.</b> <i>For clapping, two by two.</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><i>Needle-Brother, stitch and stay,</i><br><i>turn the glass the other way.</i><br><i>Stitch my granny to her chair.</i><br><i>Don't look up. She's standing there.</i></p>\n<p>There is a last line. Before they say it the children turn the water bucket over and cover the window with a shawl, and then they only move their lips. It has five words. One child told me that if you say it near a glass you see who has been standing behind you all along, and how many.</p>\n<p><b>The Bowl.</b> <i>A sitting game, played at dusk. Heard on the Pilgrim Road.</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><i>Bowl goes round and bowl goes black,</i><br><i>put a name in, don't want it back.</i><br><i>Up the mountain, up the stair.</i><br><i>Who was it? Nobody. Nobody there.</i></p>\n<p>A wooden cup goes round the ring. Whoever holds it at <i>nobody</i> whispers a name into it, and the others must forget it. At Low Harrow I counted the children in the ring, and counted the gap they left in it, and asked whose place it was. They looked at the gap for a long time. Nobody could say.</p>\n<p><b>The Unfallen.</b> <i>Sung lying on backs in the stubble, pointing up. Heard on the Barrens road.</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><i>Star bone, far bone, hanging in the black,</i><br><i>Mother wants you, won't you come back?</i><br><i>Count you once and count you twice,</i><br><i>come down slow and come down nice.</i></p>\n<p>The verse they refuse begins <i>all come down</i>. They will not say even the first words of it. A boy started it once in my hearing and his sister pinched him quiet. That night one star came down in a long white stroke over the Barrens. Their fathers went out to look for it with sacks.</p>\n<p><b>The Stones.</b> <i>A ring game, drawn in the ash with a stick, the teeth of it marked with pebbles.</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><i>In at the tooth and out at the tooth,</i><br><i>leave your blood for the one beneath.</i><br><i>Blood goes first and bone goes last.</i><br><i>Who comes up? Ask the ash.</i></p>\n<p>One child crouches in the middle while the others close the ring and count. Then she stands and says her name. If she says it wrong, she has come up changed, and must play the rest of the day as someone else.</p>\n<p>There is a question at the end: <i>who's at the bottom, breathing slow?</i> No child answers it. They all breathe in together, a long breath, and hold it, and look at the ground.</p>\n<p><i>I have set these down in the order I heard them, and I count six. The brother who read my pages at the Ashwake ring counted seven, and would not show me where.</i></p>"}, {"title": "The Fourteenth Grave at Coldhearth", "by": "Set down at Maren's camp on the Ashen Moor from the words of a woodcutter of Coldhearth, a hamlet at the edge of the Hollow Wood. He could not read it back, and asked that it be written twice. It is written here once.", "html": "<p>I cut the pale wood. You know it. It weeps clear where you open it, and the chips stay warm in the basket all the way home. You leave every tenth trunk be. My father showed me how to count them with my hand flat on the bark. I am a plain man. I want you to know that.</p>\n<p>It was the end of the day. I had the barrow on the lane, coming down to the houses. Coldhearth is fourteen hearths. Was. I say fourteen.</p>\n<p>The candles were lit in the windows, and every one of them leaned the same way. Toward the well. The way a flame goes in a door-draught. There was no draught. They were short, short as a fingernail.</p>\n<p>Then the barrow went quiet. The wheel was going over stones and there was no sound off it. I set the handles down. I knocked the axe-head on the rim, hard. Nothing. I shouted. I felt it go out of my chest and I heard none of it.</p>\n<p>I went down anyway. You go home.</p>\n<p>There was something by the well. Every time I tell it I say it was tall, and then I am less sure. I looked straight at it. I cannot give you its shape. What I have is the light. Every candle in Coldhearth leaning in at it and getting shorter, as if they were being poured out toward it.</p>\n<p>I got down in the sawpit behind Tam's. I lay in the dust with both hands over my mouth. I don't know how long. When I came up the candles stood straight again, burned down to the wick, and the wax whole round them. Whole. Then the barrow wheel ticked once, settling. I heard it.</p>\n<p>There was nobody.</p>\n<p>Doors stood open. Tam's bowl was on the board with the spoon standing up in it. The beds had the shape still in them where you'd lain. Boots at the hearths. Shawls on the pegs. When the things out of the wood take someone, there is always something after. Something to put in the ground. I went on my knees in every house. A tooth. A nail. There was nothing to bury.</p>\n<p>I dug the graves anyway, by the old stone. I put a spoon in each, one from every house. I dug fourteen, and I had thirteen spoons. I walked the lane again and counted thirteen doors. I had dug fourteen.</p>\n<p>Old Wenna had a glass, the only one in Coldhearth, a hand across, and proud of it. I held it up to my face. It gave me nothing. Grey, like looking into a shut eye. I laid it face down in the fourteenth grave and filled it in.</p>\n<p>The Pale Order came up after the thaw. Someone had sent word. They came in their carved plate, and the tall dead walked behind and stopped at the edge of the trees. A brother went house to house with his strip of bone and his little knife to cut the count. He came back with the strip as clean as he took it out.</p>\n<p>He asked me the names. I gave them. Tam. Wenna. The Hollis boys. Gethin. He wrote them. I gave twelve, and came to the thirteenth, and my mouth was empty. It is still empty there. There is a place at the back of my tongue where a word sat.</p>\n<p>Their captain said something to him in their old tongue. I caught one word. I think it was a number. They did not stay the night. They went down the road before dark, fast, and none of them looked back.</p>\n<p>I live in my house still. There are two cups on the shelf. I made the second one; I know my own work. There is a short coat on the peg by the door, sleeves turned up. There is a second pallet by the hearth, made up. At evening my feet take me out on the step and I call, once, high, the way you call someone in. I don't know the name I'm calling. Nobody comes. I go in, and I grieve the whole night, and I could not tell you for what.</p>\n<p>I split the kindling small, for small hands. I can't stop.</p>\n<p>The candle in my window leans a little still. Toward the pallet, I think.</p>\n<p>When I bend over the trough I am there in the water. Less, I think. I could not tell you where.</p>\n<p>The woman at this camp says the mirror people can find a thread and follow it back. Maybe. If you find whose coat it is, don't tell me. I don't think I would be let keep it.</p>\n<p>Write it twice, like I asked. I'll set the cup out anyway.</p>"}]}, {"id": "roads", "name": "The Roads and the Stones", "sub": "of the pilgrims, the lamps that keep them, and the rings that are older than both", "epi": "The road was there before the feet. The feet only found it.", "preface": "<p>Take your boots off tonight and turn one of your feet up to the lamp. If the sole is soft, this chapter is for you. If it is yellow and thick and you can press a thumbnail into it without feeling anything, you know most of what follows already, and you may read it to find where I have it wrong.</p>\n<h3>Why anyone walks</h3>\n<p>I have asked at a great many fires why people are on the road, and I have never once been told that someone set out to see what was over the next rise. They go because their fathers went. They go to shrines whose gods were dead before the road was cut, and kneel there, and come home with feet like horn and a look about the eyes that their mothers do not have. Ask them what they went for and they look at you as though you had asked why they breathe.</p>\n<p>I asked a boy of about twelve once, on the road below Ashwake, and he thought about it longer than the grown ones do. Then he said that if nobody walked the road it would forget where it went. I have not had a better answer since.</p>\n<h3>The milestones</h3>\n<p>Every order on the Hide will tell you it cut the Pilgrim Road. It was there before any of them. I have walked it through the Hollow Wood and seen the pale roots turn aside at its edge, as a hand turns aside from a hot pot. The milestones came later. They were set in one autumn of the two hundred and sixth year after the god's death, by people who found the road already lying there and wanted it numbered. The figures go down as you walk out from Ashwake. None of the orders will tell you what they count toward. I spent one night beside the stone that says one. The ground past it looked like the ground before it. It was warmer.</p>\n<p>The first milestone I ever leaned on was the ninth out of Ashwake. It came to my hip then. There is a beggar against it now who says it has grown past his shoulder in his time. I have no quarrel with him. I have stopped leaning on it too.</p>\n<h3>Of waking by a lantern-stone</h3>\n<p>Along every road stand the lantern-stones, a day's walk apart where the ground is kind and nearer where it is not. The tenders keep the lamps. The lamps keep the walkers.</p>\n<p>I have woken by a lantern-stone more mornings than I have woken under a roof. There is a way to do it. Before you sit up, say your own name under your breath and listen for whether any of it is missing. Then find your lamp. It will be lower on its stone than you left it, and it will not look at you for a while, the way a friend will not look at you after paying a debt of yours you did not know about. Then get up and walk, and do not stand at the stone trying to remember. The ones who stand there trying are the ones the tenders end by hanging new lamps for.</p>\n<p>Some sit down by the stone and do not rise, and in a winter or two there is a new flame on the round. The tenders keep a book of every round. More names are written in it than there were ever tenders.</p>\n<h3>Of the rings, briefly</h3>\n<p>Older than the roads and older than the lamps are the rings of stones with teeth. A measurer in this chapter gives their shape with a cord, and a walker gives their manners, and neither needs me. What the orders do about them is keep away.</p>\n<p>I once watched one of the Order's knights lead his standing dead a long way off the white road to pass a ring out on the chalk, and his dead went round it wider than he did, without being asked. At the Fen stair I asked a Fan-Bearer what the stones were. She took out a square of paper and folded a doll while I waited, put it away in her sleeve, and spoke to me about the damp. The Brotherhood will not let its hooded ones stand in sight of one. The lodge would not read a thread past the place where it went into a ring. Only the pilgrims go near. They leave something at the lip when they go in: a button, a curl of hair, a child's milk tooth. By morning the lip is bare.</p>\n<p>Nobody I have met has seen one raised. Fires ask me whether I have, and I say no, and it is true. I have seen one stoop a little lower over a man going down, the way a woman stoops over a cradle to see if the child is breathing.</p>\n<h3>The first time I went in</h3>\n<p>Three people in these pages tell you what happens in the pit, and they tell it well, and I will not make it four. I will give you only what was different for me.</p>\n<p>I went in first at the Barrow ring, long ago, with an iron ring on my finger because no one had told me to take it off. I remember that it did not hurt where the walkers say it hurts, and that it hurt somewhere they do not mention, behind the breastbone, like a word you were about to say and lost. In the dark there were others going the same way. It was warm as a crowded room is warm when everyone in it is on their knees. I have been in one room like that before. I was very small then.</p>\n<p>I rose on the Kettlewick Flats, a long walk from the Barrows by any road, with the mud keeping its beat under the crust and a woman at the lip holding out a candle. The iron ring had gone to my other hand. I was hungrier than I have ever been, and bread did nothing for it, and I keep to myself what did.</p>\n<p>I have gone in since, more often than I will number. My scars stay where they were. The walkers who watch me come up tell me that is uncommon, and then their eyes go down to my feet, and stay there a while.</p>\n<h3>A thing I will say once</h3>\n<p>You will ask what is under the ash. So did the measurer, and her cord is in my bag. I have my belief, and it is in the eighth chapter as far as it is anywhere. Here I will say only that I have never come up at a ring I did not expect, and that I have never once chosen which.</p>\n<h3>The walkers' leaves</h3>\n<p>Ten works follow, from people who walk, and from people who stay put beside the walking.</p>\n<p>The beggar sold his talk to a pilgrim by the copper, and the pilgrim's coppers gave out before the talk did. I sat at the next stone down and heard the rest for nothing. He says his seat is ninety paces from the ring. I paced it in the thaw and made it one more.</p>\n<p>The old walker gave her counsel at a hearth on the Pilgrim Road. I met her years after at the lip of a ring I have never gone into. She did not know me. Her scar had moved to her cheek.</p>\n<p>The measurer's tables are the best thing in this chapter. I checked six of her rings against her figures and found her right at every one; only the rings had gone on since. Her copyist does not say where her charts went. I do not say either.</p>\n<p>The girl who saw between the stones was written down by the tender who kept the Ashwake round that season, and the leaf came to me folded inside the tender's own book. I have looked for a child named Pip at every ring I pass since. I have not looked for the other name.</p>\n<p>The gravedigger's tally reached me through two hands. A knight copied the coffin-board before he burned it, and I copied the knight, in the Ossa, in a winter when I was helping them count. The flat lid is still flat. I have not lifted it.</p>\n<p>The hunter's paths were written out at the next ring by the pilgrim he had turned back onto the road. I have walked the hunter's line through the Wood. The trunk with the lantern in it was an eleventh when I counted.</p>\n<p>The old woman in the reed hut by the Drowned Village talked to a Fan-Bearer, who handed the leaf to me at the Lych-Gate with a paper doll still pressed in it and asked me to leave the doll where it was. I have. I have stood beside the reed in the western pool. Which way it turned for me is my own affair.</p>\n<p>The fire-keeper's rules came to the book from a pilgrim's widow. I slept three nights at that fire. On the third morning my feet pointed where everyone's did. The keeper told me mine had pointed that way since I lay down.</p>\n<p>Mother Brisk of Kettlewick was the woman at the lip with the candle. The pilgrim's strip of kettle-paper came to me years later, and it still smells of her rendering. I went back at last to pay for the candle. There is a new cord hanging by the Salt Ladder now, next to her husband's. I tied a knot in it and left the coin in the cold kettle.</p>\n<p>Last, the water-gourd. I took it from the lap of a new saint on the white road, a day short of the Citadel, and told him first. Then I counted his fingers. I will not give the number to anyone.</p>\n<p>Put your boots back on before you sleep. You may have to leave in the night.</p><div class=\"carve\">WHAT GOES DOWN<br>COMES UP<br>MOSTLY<span class=\"under\">Cut into a milestone of the Pilgrim Road a day's walk out from the Ashwake ring, with a fourth line under it scraped away. I have read that line whole on a milestone in the Barrens where nobody has troubled to scrape it, and I left it as I found it.</span></div>", "relics": "<div class=\"plates\"><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Chip of a Waystone</h4><p>A flake of grey stone, banded on its outer face. I found it in the long pale grass at the Fen-stair ring, in the spring the fallen stone there changed places, lying where the old fallen one had been.</p><p>It is cold on the outside and warm through the middle, like a stone taken from beside a hearth. Masons tell me its grain runs the wrong way for any quarry they know. A tooth-drawer in Kettlewick turned it over once in her fingers and handed it back quickly, and would not say what it reminded her of.</p><p>Sleep with it in your pack and you will sleep soundly. In the morning its point will have come round toward the nearest ring.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>The Measurer's Cord</h4><p>A cord of knots a hand apart, stiff with dust, with a lead plumb still tied to one end. I found it at the lip of the Moor ring the spring after her last leaf, the plumb lying just over the edge in the ash. I drew it up myself. It came up warm.</p><p>She began with a hundred knots and wrote that three of them had crept together. I have counted it many times. It has a hundred and one. The last is tied very tight, in a hand I know, and I have not tried to loosen it.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Wet Stain, Bottled</h4><p>A small stoppered bottle of dark stuff from the foot of a waystone plinth. The measurer filled it at the Barrow ring, meaning to learn what it was. It lay beside her cord.</p><p>It has not dried. In warm weather it stands higher in the glass. Set it near the ash from the pit and it climbs the side of the bottle nearest.</p><p>I have wiped a hand on the stain at a plinth, as the walkers do. I have not wiped the hand since.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Ash from the Pit</h4><p>Grey ash in a twist of cloth, faintly warm, faintly moving. I scraped it from the crust of the Moor ring with a spoon, and left the spoon at the lip.</p><p>Poured onto a flat stone it gathers itself into a shallow dish, and lifts, and settles. The walkers say never take it far from its ring and do not say why. I carried it to An-Vhar and back, and for a long while nothing came of that. Then one night on the Heights I woke to find it lifting each time I breathed in.</p><p>I carry it now in a pocket far from my chest. If you take it from me, take it home.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Tender's Rota-Book</h4><p>A small book of the lantern-rounds at Ashwake. Each tender keeps the round a season and signs it over to the next. The tender who kept it the season of the child gave it to me with the child's leaf folded inside.</p><p>Toward the back one name stands nine times, in nine different hands. I knew four of the hands. After her letter the pages are blank.</p><p>The tenders have asked me to sign the next line. I have not. The book falls open at that page whatever I do.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>An Iron Ring</h4><p>A plain iron ring, the kind a smith gives the boy who works his bellows. I wore it on my right forefinger into the Barrow ring the first time, and came up at Kettlewick with it on my left hand, on the little finger.</p><p>Since then I have kept the walkers' rule and taken it off at every lip. I have left it lying at more rings than one. When I come up it is on my hand again, on that small finger, and warm.</p><p>It is in the bag now. I checked before I wrote this line.</p></div></div>", "relics_intro": "What I carry off the roads. Keep the bottle and the ash at opposite ends of the bag.", "works": [{"title": "The Beggar at the Ninth Stone", "by": "Words of a beggar who sits by an Ashwake milestone on the Pilgrim Road, set down by a pilgrim who paid him a copper for each part, and ran out of copper before he ran out of talk.", "html": "<p>A coin. Good. Put it in the hand, the left one, the right one is for luck and luck has never paid me. Sit if you like. The ash is warm here. It's always warm. Forty years I've sat on it and never once had a cold backside, which is more than the brothers up in the Ossa can say.</p>\n<p>This is the ninth stone out of Ashwake. I know it the way you know your mother's face. When I first sat down it came up to my shoulder. Now it comes to my ear, and I sit no lower, and I have eaten nothing that would make a man shrink. The knights of the Pale Order came by one spring and held a knotted cord against it. One of them wrote in a little book. Then they rode on quick, all of them, and their dead walked after them quicker. Nobody has held a cord to it since. I don't lean on it any more.</p>\n<p>You'll meet the knights on this road. You'll hear them first: bone on bone, like a basket of dishes going downhill. The standing dead walk behind in a line and they never tread in the ash, only in the prints the living leave. I asked a knight once what they were carrying home. He said, everything that fell. I said that's a great deal to carry. He said the Mother bears more. Then he gave me a coin, and it was cold, and it has stayed cold in my purse for eleven years. I don't spend it. Who would take it?</p>\n<p>The pilgrims are better company. They come through thin and go on thinner. They all carry a lantern, or a lantern carries them, I was never sure which way it goes. Watch a pilgrim who has fallen on the road and come back from the lantern-stones. Something small has gone missing. One lad came past me three times in a season. The first time he told me the name of his village. The second time he told me the name of the river by it. The third time he asked me where he was from, and I told him, because I remembered, and he thanked me and wept. The lanterns keep a little of you. Everybody knows that. Nobody asks what they do with it.</p>\n<p>I wipe the dust off the Sighing Lantern at the crossroad every morning. It says on the glass to do it. I think whoever is in there likes me. It leans my way in a wind.</p>\n<p>Now. The monk.</p>\n<p>Barefoot, in a red shawl gone the colour of a scab. A wooden bowl, black at the lip. His lantern burned black, and I could still see my own hands by it, which I'll thank you not to think about. He stopped at my stone at dusk and held the bowl out, and I laughed, because who begs from a beggar? He said he only wanted a name.</p>\n<p>I gave him one. I remember giving it. I remember the weight going off my tongue like a stone off a cart. What I cannot tell you is whose it was. My wife's, maybe. The man I owed. My own, very likely. The pilgrims call me Ninth-Stone, and I answer to it, and I have never missed the other.</p>\n<p>My grandmother saw a barefoot monk with a black lantern on this same road when she was a girl. She said he smiled the whole time. Mine smiled too.</p>\n<p>And the waystones. You'll pass the ring below the Broken Kneeler. Stones like teeth round a pit of ash, and something under the ash that breathes out when you breathe in. I have watched pilgrims walk into it. They go down all at once, blood and bone and the soft bits, like a sack of wine dropped on a flagstone. Then nothing. The ash settles. And a day later some fellow from the Drowned Fen tells me he saw that same pilgrim climb out of a ring two valleys over, whole, and hungry.</p>\n<p>Same pilgrim, he says. I ask him: which hand did she carry her lantern in? He can never tell me.</p>\n<p>I have never gone in. Forty years, and it's ninety paces. I've counted. Some nights the breathing matches mine, and I hold my breath to make it stop, and it waits for me.</p>\n<p>That's the last of your copper. Mind the ash near the ring. It remembers feet.</p>"}, {"title": "Counsel for Those New to the Stones", "by": "Told by an old walker at a roadside hearth on the Pilgrim Road. She would take no food while she spoke, and twice asked the scribe which way the wind was blowing.", "html": "<p>You will want to know how to find one. Walk until the ground is warm under your feet. Look down for the pale threads in the soil, fine as hair, all running the same way, and follow them the way they run. Where they thicken and cross each other, you are close.</p>\n<p>The stones stand in a ring round a sunken pit. Each is long and hooked and sharp at the tip, and every one leans in over the pit. At the foot of each stone a dark stain seeps from the plinth and never dries. The old walkers wipe a hand on it for luck. I do it still. I no longer remember whose luck.</p>\n<p>The bottom of the pit is ash, grey and crusted, cracked like the top of a loaf. Kneel at the lip and watch it. The crust lifts, and settles. Lifts, and settles. That is how you know the stone is keeping the road. Where the crust lies still, walk past.</p>\n<p><b>The stepping.</b> Walk in. Never jump. The crust gives under you the way a scab gives, and you sink to the knee, and to the waist, and it is warm, warmer than any bath. Then you come apart. The blood goes first, because it is the easiest. Then the soft parts let go of each other. The bone goes last and slowest, and hurts the least. For a while you can feel every piece of yourself, a hand here, the heart far off, all of them yours and none of them near. Then the stones close over. You will hear it: a great slow meeting of edges, overhead and all around.</p>\n<p>Then the dark. It is warm, and it moves. There is a pull in it, long and patient, like being breathed in. I have felt other things in the dark beside me, going the same way. I have felt them turn.</p>\n<p>Then you are drawn back together, bone first this time, the rest settling onto it like cloth onto a chair. You rise out of the ash at another ring, wet to the hair, and the stones are open again as though nothing had happened. Say nothing until you are sure of your name.</p>\n<p><b>What it keeps.</b> Every passage keeps a little. Small things. The name of the well in your village. The colour of your mother's door. The word for the spoon you stir with. I kept a list, once. I have lost the list, or what it was for.</p>\n<p>It moves your scars. I had a sickle scar across my left palm. After some crossings it lay at my wrist. Later it was on my throat. Today it is on the other hand, a little higher. It is the same scar. I know its shape as I know my own feet.</p>\n<p>And you will come up hungry. The hunger has no name. Bread leaves it where it was. Meat answers it a little, for a while. Some old walkers stand at the lip of a pit with their mouths open, breathing what comes up from under the ash, and they say that answers it best. I do not do that. Not often.</p>\n<p><b>The rules we keep.</b></p>\n<p>Go in alone. Two walkers who step in together come up as two. Afterwards, look each other over.</p>\n<p>Bind your hair and take off your rings. The rings come back, but they choose the finger.</p>\n<p>Your lantern will not go down with you. It waits at the far ring, sitting a little lower than when you left it. It never says how it got there.</p>\n<p>Count the stones before you step in, and count them again when you rise. The number should not matter. Count them anyway, and remember.</p>\n<p>Never dig in the ash. What lies under it is dark and wet, and it breathes, and it is only good manners to leave it be.</p>\n<p>If you rise with ash in your mouth, spit it back into the pit. It belongs to the pit.</p>\n<p>And never ask where you were in the dark. A man I walked with asked it every time we rose. One morning he stepped in at the Moor ring. I have waited at every ring I know. He came up somewhere, I am sure of it. The stones give everyone back. They keep only a little.</p>\n<p>I have gone through thirty-one times. Thirty-four. I am well. I sleep curled on my side, and I wake warm. When I pass a ring I have never stepped into, I find myself at its lip with my mouth open, and the stones lean nearer to look at me. I think they know me by now. I think that is a kind of welcome.</p>\n<p>Go on, then. It is warm. It is always warm.</p>"}, {"title": "Tables of the Rings, with Remarks", "by": "Copied from the field books of a road-measurer who walked the Pilgrim Road with a knotted cord and a plumb. The last leaves are in a looser hand. Her maps were not sent with the books, and the copyist does not say why.", "html": "<p>I measured forty-three rings on the Hide and in the Barrens. I used a cord of a hundred knots, each a hand apart, a lead plumb, a chalk, and my own feet. I set down only what the cord told me.</p>\n<p><b>Of the stones.</b> Every ring has eight. Seven stand and one lies fallen, its point toward the pit. I went looking for a ring with all eight standing, and for a ring with two down, and found neither. Each stone is hooked, sharp at the tip, and wider at the root than at the crown, and each leans inward.</p>\n<p>The stones are carved with bands, ridges running round them like the hour-rings cut in a vigil candle. At the Moor ring under the cheek I counted eleven bands on the tallest stone and measured it at sixty-two hands from plinth to point. I came back after the thaw. Sixty-four hands and two fingers. Thirteen bands. I had cut a notch in the plinth the first time, at the foot of the lowest band. The notch was still there. It sat a band lower.</p>\n<p>At the ring by the Fen stair, the fallen stone on my first visit lay to the east. On my second it stood, and the stone to the west of it lay fallen in its place, point toward the pit, the grass under it long and pale as if it had lain there for years.</p>\n<p><b>Of the plinths and the stains.</b> Each stone rises from a plinth of the same grey stuff, and from the foot of each plinth a dark stain seeps out onto the ground. I drew a chalk line round the edge of one stain at dusk and sat up with it. By morning the stain had come out to the chalk and stopped there, exactly. I drew the line a hand wider. The next morning it had come out to that one. I stopped drawing lines.</p>\n<p><b>Of the ash.</b> I let the plumb down from the lip on the cord until it struck the crust. At most rings the crust lies between nine and twelve hands down. It lifts and settles. I counted the lifts against my own pulse: at the Moor ring, one lift in every forty beats of my heart; at the Barrow ring, one in thirty-one; in the Barrens, one in seventy, and deep. When a pilgrim crosses, the lifts come quicker for a while, and then slower, and the crust settles a finger lower than before. It does not come back up. The Moor pit was nine hands when I began. It is fourteen.</p>\n<p>The plumb comes up warm. Once it came up wet to the fourth knot. Once I let it down and felt it taken, gently, the way a child takes a thing from your hand, and when I drew the cord up the plumb was on it and the knots were all there. I counted them twice.</p>\n<p><b>Of the threads.</b> The pale fibres in the ground run the same way at every ring. I took their bearing with a needle hung on a hair, and wrote each one down. On a single sheet, the lines from forty-three rings run toward one another and meet. No map of the Hide I could buy shows that place. I think it is under the sheet.</p>\n<p><b>Of myself.</b> I have never crossed. A measurer who crosses comes back a different length, and then the cord is no good to anyone. I stand at the lip and hold the cord. I measure the pilgrims before they step in, if they let me. One came up at the Barrow ring the same height to the finger, and his shadow lay the wrong side of him until noon.</p>\n<p>A brother of the Pale Order read my tables at a hearth and told me eight is an open count. It must be carried, he said, until something closes it. I asked him what closes it. He counted the stones on his fingers and did not answer.</p>\n<p>I am carrying it. I am careful.</p>\n<p><b>Last remarks.</b> The Moor ring again. Sixty-six hands. Or I was lower. The ground is soft there, and warm, and I have been standing a long while.</p>\n<p>My cord is three knots shorter than it was. No blade has been near it. The knots are there, all of them, only nearer together.</p>\n<p>The fallen stone at the Moor ring lies with its point toward the pit. I measured from the point to the lip. Then from the point to where I stood. Then again. The second number is smaller every morning.</p>\n<p>I have written the stones down. I wonder, some nights, what they have written down of me.</p>"}, {"title": "What Wenna Saw Between the Stones", "by": "Taken down by a lantern-tender at the Ashwake ring on the Moor, in the child's own words, while her father slept by the hearth. The tender kept her speech as she spoke it, and added only the last lines.", "html": "<p>Papa said hold my hand and don't let go, so I didn't. The old lady at the lip said go in alone, one each, and Papa said she's too little to go alone. The old lady looked at me a long time, and then at Papa, and she said well, afterwards look each other over. Then she walked off so she wouldn't have to watch.</p>\n<p>The ash was warm like a bed somebody just got out of. It went up my legs soft. I was going to cry but it was too warm to cry.</p>\n<p>Did it hurt. A little bit, at first, the same as when you pull a scab off too early. Then my blood went away from me. It went all at once, like tipping a bowl. Then the rest of me went away slower, and I could feel my hand still holding Papa's hand, but my hand was far off and so was his, and then I couldn't tell which hand was which. The bones went last. The bones hurt least. They felt like being carried.</p>\n<p>Then the stones shut. They make a noise like when you grind your teeth in your sleep. Mama used to say I did that. But big, and all round, and on top.</p>\n<p>In the dark it's warm and it moves. It pulls you, slow, like somebody breathing in and in and never stopping. There was other people in there. I couldn't see them but I could feel them going the same way as us, lots and lots, more than at a feast day. They was all so quiet. Somebody was holding my other hand. I thought it was Mama. It was a lot of fingers.</p>\n<p>Who was waiting. Somebody was. Down at the bottom, where the pull goes. I never saw a face. You know when you walk into a room and somebody's in it and you know before you look? Like that. It was very big and it was very glad we came. It was so glad. I think it was counting us. I think it gets to keep a little of everybody, and it was counting which little.</p>\n<p>Then we came back together. The bones first. It's like being dressed by somebody in a hurry. We came up out of the ash here, all wet, and Papa was already standing, and he was looking at me a long time the way the old lady did.</p>\n<p>He says I'm fine. He says it every morning. He says it to the pot and to the road.</p>\n<p>This is what's different. Papa used to hum when he walked, and now he doesn't know the song. I hummed it for him and he said that's pretty, where did you learn that. Papa had a burn on his thumb from the forge, and now it's on his other thumb. He eats his bread standing up, looking at the ring. At night he counts my fingers. He does it twice. Then he counts his own. He always gets the same, he says, but he counts again.</p>\n<p>And he calls me Wenna, which is my name. He always called me Pip before.</p>\n<p>Maybe it's me that's different. I like the dark now. I used to need the candle, and now the candle hurts my eyes a little, and in the dark I can hear the ring breathing all the way from the hearth, and it's nice. I'm hungry all the time and bread doesn't help. I have a tooth at the back I didn't have before. Papa felt it with his finger and then he went outside for a long time.</p>\n<p>I know which hand was mine when we went in. I was holding his right hand with my left. When we came up I was holding his right hand with my right.</p>\n<p>I asked Papa which of us came up first. He said me. I think it was him. I think somebody should count us.</p>\n<p><i>She asked me whether my lantern was a person. I told her yes, as they all are. She asked if it could see her, and when I said it could, she held both hands up to the glass with the fingers spread, and waited, as if it would tell her how many. The flame leaned toward her, and sank. In the morning the father thanked me for the bread and led her down to the stones. He stood at the lip a long while with his mouth open. They went in together.</i></p>"}, {"title": "The Digger's Tally, Widow's Lane", "by": "Written in charcoal on the inside of a coffin board by a gravedigger of the Barrows. A knight of the Pale Order took the board up the mountain with a load of bones, read it before he burned it, and copied it out for the Codex.", "html": "<p>Buried: one hundred and six. Came back up: fourteen. Dug up again by me, to be sure: nine. Bones found in the barrows that I never put there: I stopped counting at the thaw.</p>\n<p>Warnings, for whoever has the spade after me.</p>\n<p><b>The lids.</b> Every lid on the Barrows is dished in, like a bowl, like a cheek sucked hollow. Earth settles at the edges. These sink from the middle, deeper at dawn than at dusk. Lay a new lid flat and in a year it has gone the way of the rest. Keep out of the dish. Sleep on no mound. I did once, young, and woke with ash in my teeth and my breath going the wrong way. In.</p>\n<p><b>The ones who come back up.</b> You will know them. They come up clean, cleaner than I laid them, and they walk to their own houses and stand at the door. The mother under the Turned Mound came up three times, each time further along Widow's Lane, facing home. Put them back face down, a flat stone on the neck, and say no name over them. The name is what they follow.</p>\n<p><b>Husks.</b> Pilgrims who found the red places on the Moor and drank. One Husk stands like a post for days. Two stand closer together. When there are three, the heads snap up all at once, the arms go wide, and they come. So: see one, walk. See two, run. Fight them only in a doorway, one at a time, and the Moor has few doors, so keep near the crypt mouth. Where they bleed, the ash goes to black glass. I sell it for knife-edges. It stays warm in the hand.</p>\n<p><b>Tithe-Hands.</b> Hands the size of a man, walking on their fingers. They come round behind, in threes and fives. Put a barrow-wall at your back and wait. When the fingers spread, bring the spade down flat.</p>\n<p><b>Weepers.</b> Mourners with no faces. They keep far off, behind the Grieving Stone or a broken wall. When the head tilts back the weeping comes, and the tears come out hard and white, bone, a finger long. I have pulled eleven out of my own coat. Go round the stone and close on it. Up close it only stands there and shakes.</p>\n<p><b>Gasps.</b> You feel a Gasp before you see it. The air in a barrow leans toward something. A veil of mouths, every one breathing in, and it comes through a barrow wall as if the wall were mist. Keep your lantern lit. It shies from light the way you shy from an open grave. When the veil fills, put the lantern between you.</p>\n<p><b>Bellwethers.</b> The head shut up in a bell. It tolls twice and comes straight on. Stand before stone, step aside on the second toll, and it strikes the stone and stands ringing. Then you have time. Names are cast round the rims of the bells. I read one. It was a man I buried. I put him down whole.</p>\n<p><b>The Kneeler.</b> Out on the Moor stands the Broken Kneeler, the stone one. There are others, of flesh, on their knees in the crypt-mouths and on the road, praying, and they never finish. I have never seen one rise. I have seen one a hundred paces further down the road than the morning before. Kneel nowhere near one, and pray where it cannot hear you. It is listening for how.</p>\n<p><b>The knights.</b> Every spring the Pale Order rides down in carved plate and asks for my bones. A bone out of place, they say, is a wound in the world. Twenty years I sent them off. The Tithed belong to nobody's Mother, I said. Your dead stand. Mine lie down. Go back up your mountain.</p>\n<p>Then the Empty Ninth. Nine barrows to the ring, nine of a family to each barrow. The ninth I dug myself. This spring I opened it for a new one. None of mine were in it. It was full of other bones. Long ones, clean, laid in rows like a wall set by someone careful, and more of them than the barrow has room for. I measured twice.</p>\n<p>I gave the knights those. They counted them out in the lane. One knight wrote a number, crossed it, wrote another, and they took the load up the mountain very fast and said nothing to me.</p>\n<p>I have started to give them mine as well. The ones they carry up stand where they are told and stay there. Mine come home and stand at the door.</p>\n<p>I have dug my own place in the Empty Ninth. Of all the lids on the Barrows, it is the only one that lies flat. I have been sleeping near it. My breath goes out, the way it should.</p>"}, {"title": "The Hunter's Paths Through the Hollow Wood", "by": "Spoken by a hunter of the Hollow Wood to a pilgrim who had lost the road, and set down by that pilgrim at the next ring of stones. The hunter kept his name to himself, and said the Wood already had it.", "html": "<p>You came in off the road. Everyone does, once. Sit. Keep your feet on the flat stone.</p>\n<p>The trees here are veins. Put your palm to the bark and wait. There. Slow, and slower than yours. White all the way through, and warm a hand's depth down. When they are cut they bleed thin and clear, and the cut closes by morning. We never cut them.</p>\n<p>I hunt. Nothing in the Wood was born. Everything here fell off the god and got up. I hunt what got up.</p>\n<p><b>The caps.</b> Learn the three lights on the ground, or leave the Wood. The white caps hold a little light, the way a coal holds it under ash, and they mean the ground is sound. The blue ones grow over something hollow. Step round them. The red ones grow where the god is still bleeding underneath. Eat none of them. My sister ate a red one when she was small. She talks in her sleep now in a voice lower than mine, about a room with no floor. She says she has never been there.</p>\n<p><b>The carried things.</b> Count the trunks as you walk. Every tenth one has grown round something. A bell, deep in the bark, only the lip showing. A skull with the wood come in at the eyes. A candle, still standing, still lit, the bark drawn back from the flame the way you draw back your hand. The old hunters say the god was carrying these when it fell. The bell was in its hand. The candle was in its mouth. I believed that a long time.</p>\n<p>Last winter I counted a trunk that had grown round a pilgrim's lantern. The glass was whole. The flame was low and turned toward the road. I have walked that line since I could walk, and I know every tenth trunk on it. That trunk was a ninth, the year before. I have not counted it since. I go round.</p>\n<p><b>What you will meet.</b> The Wick-Saints come as the light goes. Painted faces, white, with a little red at the mouth, and wide sails like dusted paper. They swoop in a long curve and climb away. When the sails fold back, it is coming. Strike then, while it is low, and never look at the face after. They have kind faces. That is the worst of them.</p>\n<p>The Gasps drift through the trunks like breath through a curtain, a veil of small mouths, all drawing in. They cannot bear light. Keep your lantern high and near. If one comes through your tree, breathe out. Give it nothing to take.</p>\n<p>The Vein-Borers you know by the ground. A ring of red bubbles, soft as a kettle. Get onto stone, onto a road, onto anything laid by hands. They will rise under soft ground and never under a road. That is why the roads are where they are. I used to think we laid the roads. The old ones say the roads were there first, and the Wood grew away from them the way skin grows away from a nail.</p>\n<p><b>The stones.</b> Follow the pale threads in the soil toward the ring in the east. You have seen it. The fang-stones, the pit, the ash that lifts. Every root in the Wood runs to that ring. Dig anywhere and you will find them all lying the same way, like hair combed by a hand.</p>\n<p>When the Wood is quiet at the ring I go and sit at the lip, and I listen. Down under the ash is the Root Deep, the dark ground under the skin. It breathes. When it breathes out, the whole Wood leans a little toward the stones. You can watch it happen, if you keep still. Every trunk. The candles too.</p>\n<p>Once, in a bad year, the ash lay flat for nine days and the Wood leaned the other way. Nothing came up out of the ground that year. The creepers lay in their holes. The Wick-Saints sat in the high boughs with their faces turned east, and did not come down. On the tenth day the ash lifted again, and the Wood leaned back, and I went hunting.</p>\n<p><b>What I keep.</b> I take a finger's width of anything I kill and bury it at the foot of a tenth trunk. My father did it, and his. It is only manners. The Wood takes it back in a night. In the morning, the trunk has the smallest mark in the bark, at the height of my heart.</p>\n<p>Go now, while the caps are white. Walk the road. Walk in the middle of it. If you hear a bell in the Wood, keep walking. Ours are all grown shut.</p>"}, {"title": "The Door Left Ajar", "by": "Spoken by an old woman who keeps a reed hut at the edge of the Drowned Village, spoken to a Fan-Bearer of the House of Eight Million over a cold hearth. She kept her name back. The purifier folded the page into the Codex with a paper doll pressed between the leaves.", "html": "<p>Sit on that side. The hearth side. There is a bowl of water on this side and I keep a cloth over it.</p>\n<p>You want the village. I will give it you the way it was. I am the last who can.</p>\n<p>We had wet water then. You could drown in it. Children did, in the mill-race, and we buried them at the Lych-Gate. Our well was a good well. Forty turns of rope down, and the bucket came up cold and heavy and wet through, and you could see your face in it the right way round.</p>\n<p>My father drew that well. He had an iron hook for the bucket, the long kind with a barb at the end, and in a dry summer he would let it down past the rope's end and scrape the bottom for what fell in. A spoon. A ring. Once, something he would not show us. He carried it back to the well in his two hands and put it down again and did not eat that night.</p>\n<p>The spring the water rose, he let the hook down and it caught. He leaned on it. The rope ran slack, then tight, then slack, like something below was breathing on it. He pulled. What came up out of the well was water, only water, clear as a held breath, and it kept coming. It rose to the sills, and the lintels, and the bell. He dropped the hook where he stood. The hook is still there. You have seen it. The water went over it and the mud came after, and your House hangs its paper doll on it each Ōharae. I leave the dolls be. The doll asks once.</p>\n<p>The water was dry. You know this. Put your arm in to the shoulder and it comes out dry, and a little lighter. So the drowning never finished. The water closed over us like a lid, and those under it went on.</p>\n<p>I was on the chapel roof with my sister. The rose-arch of the chapel stood straight then, and straight up behind it the belfry. The water reached the arch and the arch bowed. Forty degrees, toward the edge of the world, where the sky sits lowest. It has not bowed further since. The boatmen tie up to it to sleep. They say it is kneeling. I say it is looking for something past the edge, and waiting to be sure.</p>\n<p>They tell me the belfry fell a thousand years ago. I rang it. Both of these are true and I will not choose between them.</p>\n<p>The bells still go at dusk, under the square. My sister rings them. She stayed on the roof when I climbed down onto the reeds. She said the water was clear and she could see our mother at the table below, laying out spoons, and she wanted to go and help. I said the water was clear and I could see our mother laying out spoons with her left hand.</p>\n<p>They are all still there. Walk the Sunken Square and look down. The doors stand ajar, every one, the way you leave a door for somebody who is coming home late. The loom in the weaver's house still has the shroud on it, half done, and the shuttle is warm; I put my hand down through the water once and felt it. They set the table. They keep a place. When a pilgrim leans over the edge to look, they all stop what they are doing and look up.</p>\n<p>Out in the western pool there is a reed. The Reed That Points Home. Stand by it and it turns its head toward the village you were born in. Pilgrims weep at it. A man from the Moor stood by it and it swung round slow as a sleeper, and he laughed for joy.</p>\n<p>I have stood by it. It points down.</p>\n<p>That is why the cloth is on the bowl. That is why I drink with my eyes shut and wash in the dark. A soul stops when it meets its own face; the mirror-folk know that much, and they are right. And I will tell you what I know that they do not. I climbed down off that roof into the reeds with my sister calling. I have seen my face since, once, in a puddle on the Pilgrim Road, and it looked up at me the right way round, and it was surprised to see me.</p>\n<p>My mother always said I parted my hair on the left. I part it on the right. Mothers misremember.</p>\n<p>Take your doll when you go. Leave the door as you found it.</p>"}, {"title": "The Rules of the Heath Fire", "by": "Set down from the talk of a woman who kept a tent and a banked fire for pilgrims on the Burnt Heath. Written by a pilgrim who stayed three nights, on the back of a road-tally, and given to the Codex by his widow.", "html": "<p>Sit. The other side, with your back to the road. There. You're welcome, and the broth is hot, and it's only ash-root and water, so don't thank me for it.</p>\n<p>Now. The rules. Everyone hears them, even the ones who've heard them.</p>\n<p>The fire is banked and it stays banked. Warm your hands at it all you like. I feed it. Nobody else feeds it. A man fed it once, a big armful of heath-brush, to be kind, and it came up so high you could see the far corner of the heath by it. We sat very quiet after that, all of us, until it came down again.</p>\n<p>You sleep with your feet toward the road. If you wake with your feet some other way, turn over, and don't tell me which way you were lying. I don't like to know.</p>\n<p>If someone calls your name from outside the tent, answer only if they say it wrong. Your own people say your name wrong. They always did.</p>\n<p>What you carry in, you carry out, except your dead. Your dead I put by the saint.</p>\n<p>The saint is the one at the fork, with no hands. The pilgrims leave a pinch of ash in her lap for a safe road, and in the morning the lap is clean. I have never swept it. My mother kept this fire before me, and she said the saint stood twenty paces back from the road when she was a girl. It's twelve now. My mother said the road moves. I've paced it every spring and I say nothing, which is what she did.</p>\n<p>The angel on the rise has no face left. The pilgrims kiss it, and the wind finishes the rest. In certain light it wears the face of whoever kissed it last. I've seen my own on it. I looked tired.</p>\n<p>The cages at the edge of the heath were hung for thieves, my grandmother said. There are no thieves in them now. There are the ones who came up out of the stones wrong. You know the stones I mean. You step in whole and come up somewhere else whole, and mostly it's you who comes up. Sometimes it's you with a hand too many, or with somebody else's way of looking at a fire. Those ones walk out to the edge by themselves and ask to be hung up, and we do it, because they ask so nicely. Some nights a cage swings with no wind in it. I count them at supper and I count them again at dark. Mostly the numbers agree.</p>\n<p>Now, you want to hear about the seven. Everyone does. They pretend they came for the road.</p>\n<p>Don't look where I'm nodding. I nod toward the road, you see, and the road bends that way, and that's all my nodding means.</p>\n<p>In the corner farthest from the road lie seven of them, great horned things, bigger than this tent, lying down in a ring. Every one faces the same way. The skulls are heaped round them like stones cleared from a field, and those skulls are smaller, and nobody knows whose. That's all anyone saw, and here are the stories.</p>\n<p>Old Garrow, who kept the fire at the Broken Bridge, said they were the god's first thoughts, sent walking to carry something, and they lay down when they got there. Brannoc's girl said they knelt to something coming up. A Pale Order knight sat where you're sitting and said they lay down to hold a door shut with their weight, and then he went out to count their bones. He counted twice. He wrote the first number in his book and wouldn't tell me the second.</p>\n<p>My mother had it in six words, and the pilgrims have it now, so you may as well. The ground between them was opened once, and closed.</p>\n<p>That's the whole of it. The ground there is darker, and flat, flatter than ground lies. Nothing grows on it. It's warm, like everything here.</p>\n<p>Garrow went out there the last year of his life. He came back and said there was a seat under it, and someone sitting in the seat, very patient, waiting to be asked. Then he lay down with his feet toward the corner and I let him.</p>\n<p>Here's the last rule, and it's mine. In the morning, look which way the sleepers' feet point. On the Heath they all point the same way by dawn, however I lay them.</p>\n<p>More broth? Sleep, then. I'll keep the fire.</p>"}, {"title": "What Kettlewick Stands On", "by": "Set down from the words of Mother Brisk, tallow-wife of the Kettlewick Stilts, who talked through one rendering night to a pilgrim waiting for the mud to let go of the causeway. He wrote it afterward on a strip of kettle-paper, which still smells of the fat.", "html": "<p>Mind the rung. The third one gives. Everyone's grandfather meant to mend it.</p>\n<p>Sit there, upwind of the kettle, and keep your hand off the stilt-leg. That's fresh tallow on it. It keeps the mud from climbing. You laugh. You've never seen the mud climb.</p>\n<p>You came in on the causeway, so you've felt her. Take, let go. Take, let go. Babies here are rocked on the beat, and they know it before they know the breast. We say a Kettlewick child's first word is \"now,\" because that's the word for stepping. Down between the beats, lift on the beat. A man who puts his foot down on the beat out there stands out there a long while, and then a short while.</p>\n<p>The tallow. Everyone asks what it's rendered from, as though there were anything on the Hide besides her. We dig it out of the Amber-Grease Mire, a spade's depth down, where it lies yellow and close, like the fat under a cheek. It comes up warm. It stays warm in the barrow the whole way home. In the kettle it goes clear and sweet, and what won't melt you skim off with the long spoon and give back to the mire, and you don't look at it long. Sometimes there's a knot of hair. Once, in my mother's time, a tooth, flat as a doorstep and near as wide. She gave that back too, and said a word over it, and wouldn't tell me the word.</p>\n<p>We grease the stilts, the ladders, the rails, the lamp-posts. Candles for indoors. Never a drop for the lamps. The lamps burn who they burn.</p>\n<p>Which is why the knots. Every lamp in Kettlewick hangs from a cord, and every time you pass one after dark you tie a knot in it. That's how the one inside knows the town is still standing. Most of ours are stilt-folk who went over the rail and chose to stay. When a cord is all knots we hang a new one and coil the old in the rafters. My roof sags with them. My husband's lamp hangs at the foot of the Salt Ladder. I've tied more knots on him dead than I ever untied his boots living.</p>\n<p>Now the stilts. You'll have seen how tall we stand.</p>\n<p>My grandmother's house stood on legs four men long. Mine stands on six. My granddaughter built on eight and wants nine. On a dry summer, when the mud sits low, you can look down between the posts and see the tops of older posts, black, and on the older ones, ladders. Going down. My mother said the mire rises. Grandmother said the town sinks. The priestess says the Mother is well fed. I say bring me longer timber.</p>\n<p>And the Flats. You'll want to know about the Flats.</p>\n<p>Out past the last causeway lamp, where there's nothing left to tie a knot to, things walk. Grey and thin, a great way up on their legs, stepping slow. They step between the beats and the mud never takes them. Grandmother said they learned it watching us. When I was a girl their legs were as long as our stilts. They're longer now. They keep a little ahead of us, the way a child keeps a little ahead on the road home. Some nights one stands at the end of the causeway, very still, the height of my roof, facing the town. In the morning there are holes in the mud under the rail, deep and narrow, like someone tried the ladder with a foot.</p>\n<p>And keep away from the waystone after dark. It stands out on the Flats, the ring of teeth round the pit, and it's the one thing in Shog-Mire that never wanted longer legs. Its stones stand at the same height as the scratch my grandmother cut on the tallest one. Inside the ring the mud holds nothing. And whatever breathes down under the ash breathes in its own time. Lay your ear there and count, and it won't keep hers. It never has.</p>\n<p>I've seen folk come up out of that pit, red and wet and in pieces, and pull themselves together, and stand, and ask me the way. I tell them. I give them a candle. I don't ask where they came from. They all name the same place, and it's on no road I know.</p>\n<p>There. The mud's let go of the causeway. Off you go, on the lift. Tie a knot at every lamp you pass, and pay me for the candle when you come back.</p>\n<p>If you come back and I don't, tie one on me.</p>"}, {"title": "What the White Road Asks", "by": "Written in charcoal on the inner lid of a water-gourd, found in the shade of a rib-hull on the road to the Citadel of the Shattered Femur. The copyist notes that the gourd was dry, and that the last lines were pressed very hard into the wood.", "html": "<p>Fourth day on the white. My tongue is a stone in my mouth. Blessed be the Mother, who sends it.</p>\n<p>The ground here is bone. The brothers at the edge-well told us so, and I did not believe them until I knelt to drink and saw the grain in it, fine lines running under the dust like the grain in a knuckle boiled clean. We walk on her. All of us walk on her. The dust we raise is the holiest thing there is, and we breathe it, and cough, and are ashamed to cough.</p>\n<p>Noon here is white from above and white from below. There is no shadow except the shadow she gives.</p>\n<p><b>The Valley of Standing Ribs.</b> I have seen it now, and I can die. They rise out of the white in two rows, each the height of forty men, curving in toward each other, and they never meet. Under each rib lies a hull of shade, and in the shade of one rib a hundred pilgrims can lie down in rows. We did. Nobody spoke. At night you hear the rib tick, as a hearth-stone ticks after the fire is out. A woman from the Moor said it was cooling. A brother of the Pale Order who walked with us said it was settling. I put my ear to it. It sounded to me like something being made. I kept that to myself.</p>\n<p>We counted the ribs going in: twenty-three on the left hand. We counted them again from the far end: twenty-four. The brother said the heat had spoiled our count. He wrote the number in his book and would not show us the page.</p>\n<p><b>The Drowned Caravan.</b> On the third day the road bends round a dune whiter than the rest, smooth as a brow. Out of its foot come the traces, the leather gone hard as old bone, and the yokes, and in the yokes the ones who pulled: men and women in harness, bent to the load, as wagons are drawn everywhere on the Hide. Only their backs show, and their bowed heads, and one hand of each still closed on the strap. The dune went over them in a single night, the story goes, the way a wave goes over. The wagons are under it still. Nobody digs. Their skin has gone white and fine as the ground. Pilgrims leave water in cups at their feet, and the cups are dry by morning, and the sand beneath the cups is dry too.</p>\n<p>I had little. I left mine.</p>\n<p><b>The saints by the road.</b> From the Caravan onward they sit at the roadside every few hundred paces, legs crossed, hands in their laps, faces lifted to the pale sky. They came this way before us and sat down to pray, and the Mother took them up into herself. Their robes are stone in the folds. Their eyelids are shut and smooth. It is said they still breathe. I sat an hour with one in the heat and held my hand under his nose, and felt nothing. When I stood I saw the dust on his lip was moving. Out, and in. Once in the hour.</p>\n<p>The brother says Saint Calcifer was the first to sit, and sits still, far in, in the place called the Empty Socket, and is holiest of all because he has finished nearly everything. I asked what he had left to finish. The brother said: the Count.</p>\n<p><b>Why we count our fingers.</b> Everyone does it here, even the brothers. You lie under the rib at night and hold your hands up against the stars and count. The brothers count by the knuckle. Pilgrims count by the finger. The old walkers say it keeps you sure you are still yourself. A girl from the stilt-towns said it keeps you sure the Mother has taken none yet. The brother says the stars are her bones that have not yet come down, and that we hold our hands up so they can see how counting is done. He says ten is a good count, because it runs down to one, and one is holiest.</p>\n<p>Last night I counted ten.</p>\n<p>This morning the little finger of my left hand would not bend. The skin over the knuckle is white and smooth, and when I tap it with a nail it ticks, like the rib. It does not hurt. The brother looked at it a long while, and then he kissed it.</p>\n<p>The Citadel stands on the edge of the white now, the great broken thighbone upright, with windows cut into its sides. Two days more. My water is gone. I am glad.</p>\n<p>Tonight I will count. If there are eleven, I will tell no one.</p>"}]}, {"id": "frame", "name": "The Pale Order", "sub": "of the servants of the Bone, with whom I counted nine winters", "epi": "They wrote me into their book before I had finished my bread.", "preface": "<p>Wash your hands before this chapter, and dry them one finger at a time. The brothers would want it. I lived with them long enough to want it for them.</p>\n<h3>How they got the name</h3>\n<p>The lowlands named them, not the other way about. I first heard the name at a bridge under the mountains, from a headman who had come out with bread and salt to meet seven knights, and I understood it once they had gone past me. Their plate was grey to the joints with a dust so fine it lay in the carving like frost in a footprint, and not one of them had brushed it off. That is the first reason. For the second, look up. Over the Ossa the sky has a pallor no other sky has, like cloth scrubbed too many times on the same stone. The third reason is said after dark, and I will come to it.</p>\n<p>Among themselves they use an older word, Kostyak. A brother told it me at the gate of Nine Stairs while he dried my feet, counting the toes aloud as he went. He said it means the bones of a body, and also the few who keep a thing upright when the rest has sagged: the posts of a barn, the three old women every village has, the knots in a net.</p>\n<h3>Nine winters</h3>\n<p>I came up behind the tithe in the nine hundred and eighty-first year after the god's death, nine days after the first frost. The brothers took the date for a good sign and explained why, and I let them. I stayed nine winters. I held a lamp at the Feast in each of them. I turned a windlass, stirred the vat, swept, and lay on the roof under the cords. The book had me by a number within the hour, and in nine winters no brother asked me for the other thing.</p>\n<p>It is a kind house. I want that plain before anything else is said. They fed me when I came up thin. They nursed a brother of theirs through two winters of the coughing sickness and never once let him lie alone in the dark. They are courteous to the lowlands and patient with them, and they come for their dead.</p>\n<h3>Under the stairs</h3>\n<p>The Order says it is as old as the world, and that its first brothers stood by when the Bearing Mother came down, and laid her where she lies. I do not argue that above ground.</p>\n<p>Go down far enough in any of the nine houses and the stone changes under your hand. The Order's own halls are plain and grey, set against the older work the way a village sets its walls against a keep. Beneath them are other halls, cut to a measure no brother is made to. I climbed stairs there that I could not walk. I found long niches, and one night when I had no business there I lay down in one and stretched, and my feet found no end. Something had lain in it long enough to wear it smooth for its shoulders, and the smooth place was too far from my head.</p>\n<p>The Order's chronicle begins in a hand no brother writes. I have read its first leaf. I will not write whose hand I think it is.</p>\n<h3>The wells, and the dead who stand</h3>\n<p>Under every ossuary floor the Brothers of the Well draw up what the Mother gives. I worked a handle there one winter. What comes up I leave to the well-brother in this chapter, who says it better and is still at it. What the work does to a man who stands beside it, I can tell you. My knees were warm by spring. Not one morning since have they been cold, on An-Vhar, on the white road, at the gate of the Descent. I set that down and go on.</p>\n<p>With that marrow the brothers anoint their dead, and the dead stand. I saw a whole wall come down out of its niches at a brother's word, quietly, like people rising at the end of a service, to carry one shin-bone from a lowland field up the stair. I never saw one of them refuse. I never saw one of them hurry.</p>\n<h3>The ledge</h3>\n<p>At first light the sweepers carry the dust of their brothers up to the highest ledge and open their hands. I went up with them nine times. The cold there gets into your teeth. The dust goes up. Each time I watched until my eyes ran, and I never saw any of it come down, and on my ninth morning, without thinking, I put my palm up flat over my head as you do in a low cellar. The sweeper beside me saw. He did not laugh. He took my wrist and brought the arm down, and held it down, and said nothing all the way back to the stair.</p>\n<h3>Of their counting</h3>\n<p>I will not teach you the Count. The tallier does that, below, and the novices' catechism does, and a boy from Cold Adit learns it in his letters to his mother, and between them you will have more of it than a lowlander should carry. I learned it on the stair from the same man who wrote the tallier's leaves, and I count by it still, wanted or not. I cannot see three candles on a table now without adding them.</p>\n<p>What I can give you is what I did with it. At my first Feast they told my bones on a cord, as they do for any guest who allows it. The tallier reached the end, smiled at me, and wrote the figure. I have let nobody tell them since. I held the lamp at nine Feasts while the talliers read the walls, and I never once saw a wall come out lower than the year before. The brothers have words for that. I said the words with them. I say them still, some nights, over things that are not bones.</p>\n<h3>The roof</h3>\n<p>Above Nine Stairs a frame of long bones is strung with cords like a loom, and a brother lies under it all night reading the dark. I lay beside him one winter. He showed me how to keep still so my breath would not cloud the cords. He did not talk much. Once, toward morning, he asked whether I thought the points up there knew they were being counted, and before I could answer he said, \"No, leave it,\" and turned his face back up.</p>\n<p>His leaf from under the stone is in this chapter. It had five points on it when he first showed it me. I will not tell you how many it had the last time I saw it.</p>\n<h3>Grandfather Dust</h3>\n<p>An Ossuarch is the warden of one ossuary. On the stair the word is ordinary. In the lowlands it means one man.</p>\n<p>I have seen him three times. Once on a ridge at dusk, the standing dead behind him taller than any the Order keeps. Once at the grate of Nine Stairs, from the inside, holding the second lamp. And once in the Barrens, far off and walking away, with the white stones he had set behind him running back toward the Ossa as straight as a chalked thread.</p>\n<p>That line is the Pale. It runs out of the Ossa across the Hide and is marked by nothing but the white stones a warden sets where he has gathered bone. The knights ride the low road and come home again. A warden on the Pale goes outward. That is the third reason for the name, the one the lowlands keep for after dark. When one of theirs has gone too far to be fetched back, they say he has gone beyond the Pale, and they say it the way you draw a blanket up over a sleeper's face.</p>\n<p>I walked beside a length of it once. The ground behind the stones was whiter than the ground ahead.</p>\n<p>The villages frighten their children with him. The Order shuts every gate against him and will not say why aloud. I know one reason. It is not the reason in the Warden's book.</p>\n<h3>What they keep</h3>\n<p>Every order hides something. This one hides it in plain grey rooms with a brother standing before the door, facing the stair. I was a guest nine winters, and I went down the Ninth Stair once, which a guest may not do. While I was there I asked nothing. Under the letters cut above that stair are older letters, worn nearly smooth, and I know how many lines of them there are. I have kept the number back.</p>\n<h3>What passed the gate of Nine Stairs</h3>\n<p>Ten works follow, and all but two came to me through that gate.</p>\n<p>The sweeper's letter I found folded in the tally of the fourth wall, the year after he went up as dust. I swept beside him two winters. The room he found off the Sixth Stair had broom-marks in it before his. Some were mine.</p>\n<p>The catechism I copied by the vat-room fire. The novice in its margin counted as he did for a reason. I was two places down the bench from him when his tallier struck him.</p>\n<p>The tallier's leaves came to me bound round a niche-tally after he was laid. He writes of a leaf kept in a hand he never saw at work. I have seen it at work, late, by one lamp.</p>\n<p>The well-brother's words were set in a cake of chrism, and I held the hot knife. I laid my palm on the cap of the Fifth Well more often than I should have. It was warm each time.</p>\n<p>The knight Radko's letter lay unread in the Warden's press until the Feast after he failed to come home. I have seen his left gauntlet since, on a man walking the other way.</p>\n<p>Wat Orrin's village book and Benn's letters came down the mountain with a pedlar. I bought the letters in the pot they were kept in. I have eaten Wat's bread at his bridge, and he is right about the doorpost. I knew Benn on the Sixth Stair. His last letter is signed with a number the Warden never gave him. He did not notice, and I did not tell him.</p>\n<p>The entry concerning the gate I copied from the Order's book. The second hand in it is right, and the Warden knew it. I held that second lamp. I counted the column at his back when he came and again when he turned to go, and the second figure I have kept to myself.</p>\n<p>The star-watcher's leaves were under his stone in the roof-house. On the night he writes of I was on the Pogost too, further down, with a pan. I also lay flat.</p>\n<p>Last, the woman at the gate. The Warden kept the tallier's leaf, and now I have it. I was never at the gate with her. I have been to the altar at Old Upright, and the top letters say what she says they say. I have read the ones beneath.</p>\n<p>If you find yourself counting as you read, count in your head, and leave the figures unreduced.</p><div class=\"carve\">WE STOOD BESIDE HER WHEN SHE FELL<br>WE WILL STAND BESIDE HER WHEN SHE RESTS<span class=\"under\">Cut over the Ninth Stair in the Order's letters. The older letters under them come out a different length for every scholar who copies them. I have copied them more often than all of those scholars together, and mine comes out the same every time.</span></div>", "relics": "<div class=\"plates\"><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Warden's Chrism</h4><p>A stoppered pot of marrow from the wells under the ossuaries, amber and slow, warm however long it is kept. I rendered it myself at the vat in my second winter, and the Brothers of the Well let me take one pot for the road, because I had stirred well.</p><p>I have used it twice, both times on my own hands. In all the years I have carried it, the pot has not gone down by a finger's width. I have stopped looking at the line.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Niche-Tally</h4><p>A strip of bone cut with notches: the count of the fourth wall at Nine Stairs, taken at one Feast. It is the tally the old sweeper's letter was folded into.</p><p>The count of the brothers' own dead is cut along the top. What the Order calls the Mother's share is cut in a row of its own beneath. I have run my thumb along the lower row in the dark. It is the longer of the two.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Cord of the Frame</h4><p>A knotted cord worn smooth by a brother's fingers, the knots set close for a whole man. A tallier gave it to me on the morning its brother was laid.</p><p>It has one knot more than it should. Nobody on the stair would say who tied it. I have told it every night since, to be sure, and I am sure.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Pinch of the Finished</h4><p>Bone dust in a fold of rag, from the last pan the old sweeper of the Fourth Stair ever filled. He gave me one pinch and turned his face away while I took it.</p><p>I have opened the rag in every land on the Hide. Everything else in the bag has shifted over the years: a corner of the chrism-pot's seal, the tally's curve, the lie of the knots. The dust lies exactly as he folded it. Turn your face aside when you open it.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Star-Bone</h4><p>A shard of white bone, colder than the bones in the niches ever are, hollow of marrow, both ends broken clean. It split from a larger piece when three of the standing dead lifted it out of a field, eleven days down the line of a fallen star, and the Seeker I walked with let me keep it.</p><p>It fits no frame the talliers know. I have laid it against every bone I have handled since, and it is shaped to fit something. At every stopping on the way back to Nine Stairs I weighed it, and it was heavier each time, and the talliers took the weights down without a word.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Pale-Stone</h4><p>A white waymark no taller than a knee, cut with one number, found where no road runs. It stood in the Barrens three days beyond the Citadel, and the chalk on its near side was the paler.</p><p>The Order keeps a chart of these in the tally-room of the Ninth Stair. Joined in the order of their numbers, they make one line that doubles back and bends and never crosses itself. The line is longer every year, and no warden now walking has set its newest stones. This one is lighter on the road outward than on any road back. I have stopped walking back.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Stair-Stone of the Old Halls</h4><p>A corner broken from the lowest stair of an ossuary, tread and riser both, knee-high to a man, worn hollow by feet much longer than a man's. It came away under my hand the night I lay down in the long niche. I had leaned on the step to climb out.</p><p>The hollow in the tread is deepest at the front edge, where a foot pushes off going down. The brothers' feet push off going up.</p></div></div>", "relics_intro": "What I carried down out of the Ossa. Take each one in both hands.", "works": [{"title": "To the One Who Takes the Broom", "by": "Set down in the hand of a sweeper of the Pale Order, fifty years at the Ossuary of Nine Stairs, and found folded inside a niche-tally in the fourth wall. The last lines are pressed hard into the page, as if written in poor light.", "html": "<p>Child. They will give you my broom. Take it in two hands, as you would take a bone. It has touched more of the Mother than any brother's lips.</p>\n<p>Here is the work. Begin at the top of the stair, where the lamps are, and go down. Always down. Sweep toward yourself, never away; what is swept away is scattered, and what is scattered is lost. Kneel at each niche. Tie a cloth over your mouth. Kiss the skulls if you like; the brothers do. Never let your breath touch the dust.</p>\n<p>Use the soft end. Gather into the pan of bone. Say the name of the niche as you gather, the name of the brother if he had one, the name of the bone if he had none: the long bone of the left arm, from the lowlands. Nothing goes up nameless.</p>\n<p>What the dust is. I tell you what I was told. Everything that was ever the god is still dying, the stone and the stair and you and I, and dust alone has finished. So it is the holiest thing in the ossuary. Sit with a wall a long while and you will see it: a rib leans, a finger lies a nail's width over, the row of skulls dips like a roof taking snow. The dust keeps still. When my knees are bad I sit in it. It is the only stillness I have known.</p>\n<p>The Feast of the Laying. Once a year every wall is counted. The brothers go along with their tallies and the Warden reads from the book, and the sweepers hold the lamps. I have held the lamp at forty-nine Feasts. The count rises. Every year it rises, past the dead we carried down the mountain. The Warden says \"the Mother's gift,\" and we say it after him, and the notches for the gift are cut on the tally and kept out of the book. Once, when the tallier's hands shook, I cut them myself. Forty-one on one wall, that year. I said the words. I believe the words. I write them here so you will say them too.</p>\n<p>The Pogost. When your pan is full you carry it to the Weighing, under the Eighth Stair, and a brother of the Ninth sets it on the scale and writes where we cannot see. Then, before first light, we climb. The Sky Pogost is cold past bearing. You will lift the pan and open your hands, and the Myriad will take it. The dust rises. Watch it until it is gone, and it will be a long time going. In fifty years I have never seen it come down.</p>\n<p>My first master told me the sky over the Ossa was the palest on the Hide because of us. I believed him. It is paler now than when I was a boy. Lower, I think. On still mornings it hangs like a ceiling you could lay a palm against.</p>\n<p>Now the small things. These I never reported. I report them to you, which is a coward's way. Pardon me.</p>\n<p>The pan is heavier going up than coming down. That is my arms. I am old.</p>\n<p>There is a room off the Sixth Stair, behind the long wall. Forty niches, and my own broom-marks in the dust of its floor. I know my marks; I lead with the left and draw in short. They are mine, and old, and I have no memory of the door. The door is plain grey stone, like ours. The niches inside are longer than ours. I swept it that day. I said the names. I knew none of them, so I said the names of the bones, and ran out of names for the bones.</p>\n<p>When I pass with the lamp, the skulls turn to follow. A little. You will see it in your first year, and you will tell yourself it is the shadow moving. That is right. Tell yourself that. The brothers say the dead keep watch. I have come to think they watch the lamp, or what the lamp shows them of us. Down on the lowest stair, the one we climb on our hands, the long skulls never turn. They were facing me already.</p>\n<p>And the stair. As a novice I counted the risers of Nine Stairs for a penance, every flight, and I knew the number the way you know your own teeth. I count them still, each morning, going down. I will leave the number off this page. It is higher.</p>\n<p>You will learn the Rule in your first week. I have kept all four parts of it, and the last part best.</p>\n<p>I have asked nothing. I have swept. Take the broom in two hands.</p>"}, {"title": "The Lesser Catechism of the Kostyak", "by": "The questions a novice of the Pale Order learns by heart before he is given his cord, copied from a lesson-book of the Ossuary of Nine Stairs. The copyist notes that two answers were scraped down to the vellum and one was ruled and left empty. A novice's hand fills the lower margin of the last leaf.", "html": "<p>Here begin the twenty-seven questions, which are nine taken thrice. The tallier asks. The novice answers kneeling, one breath to each answer.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>1.</b> Q. What is a man?<br>A. Two hundred and six, and a name that will be taken.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>2.</b> Q. Who takes the name?<br>A. The Hush, which unsays. It takes names one at a time, as it took the god's.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>3.</b> Q. And the number?<br>A. The number stays. The Hush has no mouth for it.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>4.</b> Q. Who bears the world?<br>A. The Bearing Mother, the Bone of the god. She holds the Hide as a frame holds a hide, and she has never once set it down.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>5.</b> Q. Where is she?<br>A. Under your knees. Over the stair. In your hands, when you hold a bone. Hold it in two. One hand is a grasp. Two hands are a bearing.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>6.</b> Q. What is bone?<br>A. The part that stays. The flesh is lent. The bone is kept.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>7.</b> Q. What is a bone out of its place?<br>A. An open wound in the world. We carry it home.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>8.</b> Q. What is the Laying?<br>A. The washing, the chrism joint by joint, the niche, the name, and the number after the name. Nothing goes into the wall nameless. Where the name is lost, say the name of the bone.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>9.</b> Q. Whence the chrism?<br>A. From the wells. The Mother gives it freely.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>10.</b> Q. How deep are the wells?<br>A. The Mother gives it freely.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>11.</b> Q. What is the root of a count?<br>A. Its figures, added together until one is left. Two thousand and sixty-one: nine.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>12.</b> Q. Why nine?<br>A. Nine keeps itself. Take any count nine times, and its root is nine. So nine ossuaries, nine stairs, and every prayer said nine times.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>13.</b> Q. What is the root of a man?<br>A. Eight. One short.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>14.</b> Q. What is a count that falls short of nine?<br>A. An open count. The tallier carries it behind his teeth until it closes.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>15.</b> Q. How does a man close?<br>A. <i>(scraped to the vellum)</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>16.</b> Q. Which is the more, a child or a man?<br>A. The child. He is born with more bones than a man dies with, and they knit, and are fewer. The fewer is the holier.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>17.</b> Q. What does the Mother seek?<br>A. The One Bone.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>18.</b> Q. What is the Great Count?<br>A. Every bone of the Mother, laid and numbered, from the first niche. Its running figure is kept on the Ninth Stair.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>19.</b> Q. Who reads it?<br>A. The Ossuarch, who goes down. Ask nothing of the Ninth Stair.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>20.</b> Q. When does the Count close?<br>A. At the Last Number, when every bone of her is counted. Then she rests. That word is for dust, and for her, and for nothing else.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>21.</b> Q. How near is the Last Number?<br>A. Nearer by every tally.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>22.</b> Q. What are the funerals?<br>A. The flesh to the high ledges and the cold: the first. The bones to the niche, anointed, facing the stair: the second service. The dust to the Sky Pogost at first light: the last.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>23.</b> Q. Who cut the stair?<br>A. We were here when she fell.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>24.</b> Q. What do the dead watch for?<br>A. <i>(the line is ruled, and empty)</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>25.</b> Q. What is dust?<br>A. The one thing that has finished. Everything the god was is still dying. Dust is done. A brother may kiss a skull. Over dust he holds his breath.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>26.</b> Q. Why is it given to the sky?<br>A. So that it may rise, and be kept above us, and touch nothing.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>27.</b> Q. What is the Rule?<br>A. Bear what is given. Keep what is laid. Let finish what is ready. Ask nothing of the Ninth Stair.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>28.</b> Q. Where does the dust go?<br>A. <i>(scraped to the vellum; at the foot of the scraping, the tail of one figure, which may be a nine)</i></p>\n<p>Here end the twenty-seven questions.</p>\n<p class=\"orn\">&#10087;</p>\n<p><i>In the lower margin, small, in a novice's hand:</i></p>\n<p>I am to have these by the Feast. I told them on my cord twice. Twenty-eight. Brother Tallier took the book and counted and said twenty-seven, and that I had carried an open count into a holy book, and set me the stair for penance. So I counted the stair.</p>\n<p>Twenty-eight is ten, and ten is one. The fewer is the holier. I think it is a good root. I asked if the twenty-eighth was a gift, like the notches the Warden leaves out of the book, and Brother Tallier struck me with the cord.</p>\n<p>For the twenty-fourth the old sweeper says: the lamp. My master says: us. The long ones on the lowest stair say nothing, and face the way they face.</p>\n<p>I will leave off what I counted on the stair.</p>"}, {"title": "On the Arithmetic of the Frame", "by": "Written for the novices of his office by a tallier of the Ninth Stair, entered in the Order's books as Seven Thousand Two Hundred and Nine. The Codex copy was made from leaves bound round a niche-tally; the sums in the margins are his, and the last page is written very small.", "html": "<p>Novice. Before you learn a figure, learn why we keep them.</p>\n<p>The Hush unsays. It took the god's true name first, and has taken names ever since. I have held the oldest book on the stair, and whole lines of it stand blank where names were written. The numbers beside them are still there, in the same ink, in the same hand. The Hush reaches for a number and finds nothing to hold. So every bone laid is given two things: its name, for the Mother, and its number, for the Count. The name is a kindness. The number is what lasts. In the book I am 7,209. Say it after me, and you will have said a thing the Hush cannot take back.</p>\n<p>Now the reduction. Every count is carried down to its root. Add its figures, and add again, until one figure stands alone. Seven and two and nought and nine: eighteen. One and eight: nine. That is my root. Each morning I give thanks for it, nine times over.</p>\n<p>Nine is faithful. Whatever you multiply by nine, its root returns to nine. Seven taken nine times is sixty-three: six and three, nine. Take a whole man nine times: one thousand eight hundred and fifty-four. One and eight and five and four, eighteen; nine. Nine is her root, and so there are nine great ossuaries and nine stairs, and we pray in nines, and the Count is kept on the Ninth.</p>\n<p>The frame. Tie your cord tonight: two hundred and six knots, one for each bone of a whole man, and tell them in the dark until your fingers know the end without you. Then reduce it. Two and nought and six: eight. A man is an open count. He stands one short of the Mother all his life, and closes only in the niche, when his number is written beside him.</p>\n<p>Knitting is holy work. A child comes into the world with near three hundred bones, and no two midwives agree on the number. Over a life they knit, two into one, three into one. Feel an old brother's hands and you will know he is nearer. Every frame walks toward the One Bone, and the Mother first of all. When every bone of hers is counted and knit, the Count closes. That is the Last Number. On that day she rests, and we lay down our tallies, and the sweepers set down their brooms. I weep to write it.</p>\n<p>A tallier carries every open count in his head until it closes. I carry eleven. The long wall of the Fourth Stair: four thousand four hundred and seventeen, root seven; it wants two. The drawer of finger-bones from the lowland road: fifty-eight, root four; it wants five. The room the sweepers found behind the long wall of the Sixth, which no chronicle lists: forty niches. I have been given no count for it. I carry the forty. Four.</p>\n<p>And the one that will not close. The Great Count itself stays on the stair. Its root every novice learns in his first winter, and loses his sleep over in his second. Its root is one. It was one when I came to the stair. Each Feast the tallies come up, the Count rises, I reduce it by the lamp, and it comes to one again.</p>\n<p>Here is why. Last Feast the brothers laid one thousand six hundred and fifty-two dead: root five. The walls rose by two thousand and sixty-one: root nine. The Mother gave the difference, four hundred and nine: root four. Four and five. She gives exactly what our dead lack to close. Every year the rise keeps itself, and every year the whole stands one over. The Wardens teach that the one is the One Bone, showing through the Count before its time. I believe the Wardens. Say it with me.</p>\n<p>The column. Beside the book lies a second leaf, where the gift is set down alone, year under year, in a hand I have never seen at work. A tallier may add across that leaf. He may never add down it. I was given no reason, and I kept the rule thirty-one years.</p>\n<p>In the thirty-second, in the dark, on my cord, I began. The rows lengthen as they go. The first gifts were small: a wall of eleven, a wall of three. The late ones run to hundreds. I added down forty rows and stopped, because the sum had passed my own number, and every brother written in the book before me, and the leaf went on below the lamp, down the stair.</p>\n<p>I did not reduce it. I will never know its root.</p>\n<p>Keep your cord. Add across.</p>"}, {"title": "At the Windlass", "by": "The words of a Brother of the Well, written in the Order's books as Four Thousand and Fifty, spoken to a novice sent down to turn the windlass with him. The novice wrote them out that winter. The leaf was found in the vat-room of the Ossuary of Nine Stairs, set inside a cake of chrism, and cut free with a hot knife.", "html": "<p>Take the handle. Both hands. A single hand only clutches.</p>\n<p>Now turn. Slow. You will turn until the rope goes heavy, and you will know it when it comes. Count the turns. Say them under your breath, and say them in nines.</p>\n<p>The rope is fixed at nine hundred turns. Each Feast we splice in nine fathoms more, new hemp, tarred. The bucket still comes heavy at the nine hundredth turn. My master spliced. His master spliced. Keep turning.</p>\n<p>There. Feel her. Lean on it now, and keep your feet.</p>\n<p>Smell that? Warm. The shaft is cold for the first hundred turns, and after that the stone sweats. By the four hundredth your hands are warm on the handle, and the handle is warm through. The Mother keeps her heat at the bottom. I have drawn thirty-one years and never once brought up a cold bucket.</p>\n<p>Here it comes. Amber. Slow. Look how it holds the light of the lamp and gives none back. Pour it into the vat. Gently. Now stir.</p>\n<p>Always stir. Marrow left still sets like wax. An hour and it skins. A night and it is stone to the elbow. Brother Six Thousand and Three sat down by the vat on the long watch of the Feast and slept with his hand on the rim. His fingers were in it. We cut him out at dawn. The hand closed, after. It closed well. He has four fingers on it now, knit two into one at the knuckle, and he blesses with that hand, and it is the holier hand. It has less to it, and so it is nearer.</p>\n<p>Stir.</p>\n<p>What is it for. You know the catechism. The bones. You have seen it laid on, each joint in turn, and a laid brother stands facing the stair and steps down when he is asked. A brother of the well sees them step. The first time, you give thanks. I still give thanks.</p>\n<p>The lamps. Render the marrow over a low fire, skim it, pour it in the moulds, and you have the tallow of the deep roads. It burns slow and burns quiet. The skulls in the niches turn toward it. They turn toward the chrism lamps and toward no other flame. I carry one on the stair and they follow it, the whole wall, as a field of grass follows the wind. Hold your lamp steady on the stair.</p>\n<p>The living. It closes any wound. Pour it in and the wound shuts. The place stays hard, and warm, and swells a little with the years. Look at my arms. That is the windlass, thirty-one years of burns and rope. Every scar knit. Put your thumb here. Warm, yes. Mine always are.</p>\n<p>Keep stirring.</p>\n<p>Now. The Fifth Well.</p>\n<p>The Fifth is capped with a stone and the stone is sealed with chrism, and no brother draws there. The Wardens say the Marrow Pontiff keeps it. Eldest of all things in the ossuaries. He went down before the stair was cut. He has never come up. That is all the Wardens say, and they say it seldom.</p>\n<p>I drew the Fifth once. Before the cap. I was younger than you are. I turned nine hundred and the rope stayed light. I turned on. At twelve hundred it went heavy all at once, and then it went slack, and then the handle turned in my hands the other way. Gently. Something was letting the rope down for me. Paying it out.</p>\n<p>I held the handle. I said the Mother's names nine times.</p>\n<p>It breathed up the shaft. Warm, like the vat. And it said my number. Four thousand and fifty. Slow, the way a tallier reads off a stone. Then it said a smaller one. Then smaller.</p>\n<p>Then it said, <i>Kneel.</i></p>\n<p>I knelt. My knees were on the stone before I had thought. The rope ran out through my hands to the end, and the bitter end went down, and I heard it touch nothing for a long time.</p>\n<p>The Wardens capped the Fifth that spring. They sealed the cap with my rendering. I stirred it myself.</p>\n<p>Four and nought and five and nought. Nine. I have a good root.</p>\n<p>I kneel less, now. My knees have knit. I bend them in the morning and they bend slower than they did, and warm, and I count what I can feel of them, and it is fewer. I am glad of that.</p>\n<p>Keep stirring. Never let it stand. If you hear your number in the vat, stir faster.</p>\n<p>Tomorrow we splice.</p>"}, {"title": "A Letter from the Low Road", "by": "Written by a knight of the Pale Order to a novice of the Ossuary of Nine Stairs, sealed with chrism instead of wax. It lay in the Warden's press, unread, until the Feast after the knight failed to come home. The seal still gives a little under the thumb.", "html": "<p>Brother Vasko,</p>\n<p>The Rule allows one letter a season. I spend this one on you. Forgive the hand; I write by a lamp of marrow-tallow, and it smokes.</p>\n<p>We are eleven days below the Barrens, in the barrow lands. Twelve of the standing dead walk with me, two abreast, carrying the chest. I keep them to a walk. Nothing on the low road is worth hurrying toward.</p>\n<p>You asked me once what the lifting is like. Here it is. You find the bone by the wrongness of the ground: a furrow that will lie flat for no plough, a hearthstone warmer on one side. You kneel. You say the name of the place it lies in, so the place knows it is being answered. You lift in two hands, slowly, as a man lifts a sleeper. Most come up easily. Some the ground keeps, and then you wait, and say the name again, and the ground lets go. Twice this season it let go with a sound like breath drawn through teeth. I wrote \"resistance\" in the day-book, and nothing more.</p>\n<p>Then the chrism, joint by joint, thumb and two fingers. It is warm still, as the Order promises, a hundred leagues from the wells. I have anointed forty-one since the Ossa. The flask is fuller than when I set out. I measured it against the notch I cut on the first night. I said the words. The Mother's gift.</p>\n<p>The lowlanders hide their dead from us. At Kerrow Ford they had walled their grandmothers into the houses and plastered them over, and stood in the doorways swearing the houses were new. At another village the bones were broken small and worked into the clay of the ovens. A woman there told me Grandfather Dust had swept up her son, and begged me to go. I told her the Order has shut its gates to him. She looked at my plate a long while, and shut her door.</p>\n<p>At the Shallows we met a procession of the flesh-houses, hooded, come for the same barrow. We kept the old terms. They take the meat; we take the frame. The Rule has me sit and watch them work, so nothing of ours goes into their baskets, and so I sat, with my dead in a row behind me. Their elder handed each bone across clean and wet, in two hands, which I had not looked for from them. At the last he held one back and weighed it on his palm, and said our Mother was heavier than theirs this year. I took it. A child's shin. It weighed as much as my forearm.</p>\n<p>I saw him, Vasko. The one the Order calls the Ungated. On the ridge above the Shallows at dusk, with a column of the dead at his back, taller than ours, taller than any man. My twelve stopped. They turned their skulls to the ridge, all together, and stood, and I asked them three times. He lifted both palms, the way we brace at the Laying. Then he went down the far side, and my dead came back to me. I wrote \"fog\" in the day-book. His left shoulder was knotted and lumped, as if the carver's hand had shaken.</p>\n<p>Now the thing I have kept from Brother Confessor. I give it to you, because by the time this climbs the Ossa you will be holding a lamp for the sweepers at the Feast, and those who hold the lamps keep things.</p>\n<p>My left pauldron came to me from Brother Dobrin, whose name is cut on it in the Order's letters. I oil it every night. Since the Barrens a ridge has run along its edge that no carver made, and on the back of my left gauntlet there is a spur the length of a fingernail, curling toward the wrist. I file it down at night with the whetstone. By morning it is back. I keep the filings in a twist of cloth. I cannot tell whether they are dust, or whether I may carry them up.</p>\n<p>And Dobrin's name has a letter more in it than it had. I count them each night, as I counted the stair.</p>\n<p>The plate sits well. It sits better than it ever has. The left gauntlet I have kept on since the Shallows. It fits close.</p>\n<p>I have kept the Rule, Vasko, to its last clause, and the last clause hardest.</p>\n<p>Tell the Warden I was diligent.</p>\n<p>Radko, knight of the Kostyak, at the Shallows</p>"}, {"title": "What the Order Took, and What It Gave", "by": "From the village book of Cold Adit, an iron-working hamlet under the Ossa range, in the hand of its headman Wat Orrin. A pedlar had the pages from the headman's widow in exchange for a pot of chrism, and carried them down to the lowlands.", "html": "<p>The Order came on the ninth day after first frost, as it has come in every year this book holds. Seven knights in their white plate, and the standing dead behind them on the road, forty or more. You do not count those to their faces. I met them at the bridge with bread and salt, as my father did, and his.</p>\n<p>What they took. Eleven bones from the Low Field, where the thaw heaved the ground and set them out in the furrows like stones. Nobody here knew whose. Then our own. They say a bone in soft earth lies out of its place, and in time they come for every grave we dig. We give them. Old Mag's man went up this year. She stood in her door and said his name after them the whole length of the road, and the last knight lifted his hand to her. They are courteous. I set that down. They are always courteous.</p>\n<p>The tithe. One son from every ten households, each third year, by lot. This year it fell on Harl's boy Tam and on my sister's youngest, Benn. Tam went gladly. Benn went. I write their names so the book keeps them, since the village will not see them again. None come down. My grandfather's page tells of a woman of this village who saw her son among the standing dead on the road, knew him by the way he set down his left foot, and ran to him. The knights let her. He turned his skull to her and went on up. The book says she was content. That line is in a different ink.</p>\n<p>What they give. A pot of chrism to each house that gave, and a pot to the headman for the pit. It is amber and slow, warm in a cold room, and it closes any wound. Dunnet was crushed under a fall at the Third Drift in spring. We poured it into his side and he was back at the face by midsummer. I set down also that the places it closes stay hard. Feel the knot on Dunnet's side. Feel the knot on my own hand. They give no pain. They swell a little with the years, as a cut in green wood does.</p>\n<p>The funerals. From the ridge above the tips, on a still morning before light, you can see the smoke go up off the high peak: pale, a thread standing straight, and very slow. My mother called it the brothers going home. The children wave to it. Wave, I tell them. It does no harm to be civil.</p>\n<p>Now what we have noticed. I write it plain, for whoever keeps this book after me.</p>\n<p>The ore is paler. My father's iron came out of the Third Drift red-black, and the lowland smiths paid well for it. Now a white runs through it, a seam like marrow in a split bone, and it will not smelt. In the fire it goes to a lime, and the lime is heavier than the ore that went in. The men call it the Mother's vein and leave it in the rock. By spring the rock has closed over their pick-marks. Every spring the drifts are narrower. In my life we have cut the Third Drift three times, to the same width, in the same place.</p>\n<p>The notch. My father cut a mark on the doorpost where the high peak stood from our step: a hand's width under the notch of the east ridge, so the Feast-day dawn came clean through. The peak stands in the notch now. The dawn comes late.</p>\n<p>The sky. It hangs lower over the peaks each year, and paler. When the wind comes off the Ossa the linen on the lines takes a grey that will not beat out. The children write their names in the dust on the sills. I told them to stop. I could not have said why.</p>\n<p>Last. This year, for the first time in the book, the eldest knight asked how many of us there are. Men, women, children, and the old. He wrote it in a book of his own. Then he took my hand in both of his, the way they hold a bone, and thanked us for the bread. At the bridge he turned back and said, \"Bury deep this winter.\" I asked him why. He said the ground here is kind, and went on up.</p>\n<p>Forty-one households. The bread was good. Bear what is given. I have cut it over the door.</p>"}, {"title": "Letters Up the Ossa", "by": "Four letters from a tithed son of Cold Adit to his mother, kept in an empty chrism pot on her shelf. When the knights came down for her bones, the one who lifted her read the last letter through, set the pot back on the shelf, and left it. A pedlar had it after.", "html": "<p><b>I.</b></p>\n<p>Mam,</p>\n<p>A brother writes this for me because my letters are not good yet. He says to say all of it and he will put it down.</p>\n<p>I am well. They took my shirt and gave me grey, but I kept the collar where you sewed BENN, and at night I feel the stitches. Tell Da I did not cry on the road. I cried at the gate.</p>\n<p>They have given me my teacher. He is Brother Oskol, and he has been dead a long while, and I carry him. He hangs on a yoke across my shoulders in a frame of cord, every bone in its knot, and I take him up the stair in the morning and down at night. The yoke is heavy. I must learn Oskol by touch: the long bone of his left arm, the little bones of his wrist, which are the hardest. When I get one wrong the brother lays my hand on the right one and says its name twice.</p>\n<p>His skull sits by my ear on the stair. It turns a little when the lamp goes past. The brother says that means he likes me.</p>\n<p>Your Benn. <i>(Here, in a child's hand:)</i> BENN</p>\n<p><b>II.</b></p>\n<p>Mother,</p>\n<p>Three winters. I write myself now.</p>\n<p>I have learned the Count. Every bone laid gets two things, a name the Mother hears and a number the Count keeps, because the Hush can take a name and has nothing to hold in a number.</p>\n<p>I have learned roots. Add the figures till one stands alone. Cold Adit is forty-one households: four and one, five. An open count. I lay awake over it. Tell the headman.</p>\n<p>I tie my cord of two hundred and six knots and tell it in the dark. Brother Oskol I can tell in the dark now too, from the yoke, without my hands. The Tallier says that is the right way round.</p>\n<p>The brothers teach it is holier to have less. A child has more bones than a man, and they knit. So I am less holy than Oskol, who is as knit as he will ever be. I am trying to be patient.</p>\n<p>I have stopped being homesick. I think it knitted.</p>\n<p>The stitches in my collar are going. I have let no one mend them.</p>\n<p>Benn</p>\n<p><b>III.</b></p>\n<p>Mother,</p>\n<p>At the Feast of the Laying I held a lamp for the sweepers, and at dawn the Warden gave me my number. I am nine thousand one hundred and six. Nine and one and nought and six: sixteen; seven. An open count. I will close in the niche, when my number is cut beside me. I am glad of it.</p>\n<p>At first I turned round for Benn and nobody was there. Now I turn round for the number.</p>\n<p>I have set Brother Oskol down. He stands on the Fourth Stair, facing up. When I pass with the lamp his skull comes round after me, further than the others. My shoulders still sit as if the yoke were on them. The brothers say they will stay so.</p>\n<p>A thing I have not told Brother Confessor. I told Oskol every night for four years. Two hundred and six. In the third winter, two hundred and seven: a small one at the heel of the right foot, that I had never felt before. I took it to the Tallier. He wrote it on the other leaf, the one we may add across and never down, and said the Mother's gift, and I was glad. Oskol was heavier after. I felt it on the stair.</p>\n<p>9,106</p>\n<p><b>IV.</b></p>\n<p><i>In the Order's letters. The copyist notes the hand is steady and very small.</i></p>\n<p>Mother.</p>\n<p>The brother who reads me your letters reads out a name at the top of them. I asked him to read it as my number. He does not know it, so he reads nothing, which is better.</p>\n<p>I sweep now, on the Sixth Stair, in the rooms past the long wall. There are rooms here the chronicle does not list. The stairs in them are knee-high, and I go up them on my hands. The dust keeps still. It is the one thing here that does.</p>\n<p>I tell my cord at night. It has grown short for me. I have tied knots on. The Tallier says a frame walks toward the fewer, and he says it looking at my hands.</p>\n<p>I have the collar still. The thread came out, and where it was there are holes. I cannot remember what they said. I count them. Four. Four wants five to close.</p>\n<p>Bury deep this winter. The ground at Cold Adit is kind.</p>\n<p>Bear what is given.</p>\n<p>9,107</p>"}, {"title": "Entry the Four Hundred and Fifth, Concerning the Gate", "by": "From the Order's Book at the Ossuary of Nine Stairs, in the hand of its Warden, with lines added afterward in a second hand. Copied for the Codex from the leaf itself. The copyist kept the second hand where it falls, and set it apart.", "html": "<p>Set down by the Warden of the Ossuary of Nine Stairs, in the week of the Feast of the Laying, the walls standing at their count.</p>\n<p>Concerning Four Thousand and Eleven. Root six. Open.</p>\n<blockquote><i>Four Thousand and Twelve. His number was corrected at his vows. Root seven. Open still.</i></blockquote>\n<p>The Rule writes no brother's name in this book, and I do not write his. He was entered at his vows under the Seventh Ossuary, and raised Ossuarch of that house in the year its Warden was carried to the Pogost. His count has not closed. Let the talliers carry it.</p>\n<p>Let it be written that the gate of this ossuary is shut to him, and the gates of the nine, and of the hundred lesser, by order of the Wardens in agreement. The reasons are these.</p>\n<p>I. That the Seventh Ossuary has sent no tally to the Ninth Stair these three Feasts, and its bell does not answer, and he is the one who came out of it.</p>\n<p>II. That he came to our gate unsent, from the high road, with a column of the standing dead of no house we could name, and asked nothing, and waited.</p>\n<p>III. That his frame, told at the grate on his own cord, stands above two hundred and six. The Rule blesses a frame that knits to less. The Rule makes no provision for the more.</p>\n<p>IV. That he has read the weights on the Ninth Stair of his house. This is said, and unproven.</p>\n<p>V. ~~That the dead~~</p>\n<blockquote><i>V. That he knew the lower lines under the Ninth Stair, the old letters, and said them aloud at the grate. Brother Tallier counted eleven lines. Brother Tallier has asked to be moved to the sweeping.</i></blockquote>\n<p>He came to the gate on the third night after the Feast. He stood a night and a day. The dead at his back numbered thirty-six, root nine, and I set that down with thanks to the Mother.</p>\n<blockquote><i>Forty. The sweeper at the lamp counted forty. Forty is four.</i><br><br><i>Before the Feast. He came on the ninth night before it. The Warden has the week wrong, and the Warden knows it.</i></blockquote>\n<p>The knots on his cord were told through the grate by two talliers, each alone. The first reached two hundred and nineteen, root three, and stopped, because the cord went on and his lamp had burned down. The second reached two hundred and twenty-six, root one, and said the cord was longer than when the first had held it. I have written both. Let the Wardens after me choose.</p>\n<p>He said nothing to the reasons. At the reading he lifted both palms, as we brace at the Laying, and held them so while I read them twice. Then he turned and went down the high road with his column.</p>\n<p>Let the gate stay shut. Let the lamp at the grate be kept lit.</p>\n<p>Let the Rule be kept in this house, in all its clauses, and the fourth before the others.</p>\n<p class=\"orn\">&#10087;</p>\n<p><i>Below the rule-line, where the book is read by no one aloud.</i></p>\n<p>His plate. I saw it through the grate by my own lamp. It is cut with names, as a warden's plate must be, the brothers who wore it before. I knew three of them. Stoyan, Mirko, Bratan: brothers of this house. Their bones stand in the niches of our Fourth Stair. I went down after, with the lamp, to be sure. They stand there. Their skulls turned to follow me, as is proper.</p>\n<p>The names on his plate are cut deeper than any carver of ours cuts. On the left pauldron the plate has closed over Bratan's name, the way the skin closes over a thorn, and I could read only the first letters. I have worn Bratan's gauntlet since my vows. I took it off tonight. The fingers are as they were. I measured them against the lamp.</p>\n<p>When he turned from the gate, the dead in the niches of the Gate Stair turned their skulls after him. They turn for a lamp. He carried none. Then every one of them lifted its hands, palms up, as a brother braces at the Laying, as if a weight had come down onto the ceiling of the stair. They hold them so. I have asked them to lower their hands nine times, and nine times nine. They face the stair still, and their hands are still raised.</p>\n<p>The sweepers say the Gate Stair has one more step than it had. I have forbidden the saying. Tonight the tallier counts the Gate wall one higher than the Feast. I have left it out of the book.</p>\n<p>It is in the book now.</p>"}, {"title": "Of the Unfallen", "by": "Written for his successor by a star-watcher of the Pale Order, keeper of the night-roof above the Ossuary of Nine Stairs, set down in the Order's books as Five Thousand and Thirteen. The leaves were found in the roof-house under a stone. The figures in the margins are his.", "html": "<p>Brother. You have come up to keep the roof after me. Let your eyes go dark first. Then look up.</p>\n<p>Every cold point up there is a piece of the Mother. When she fell, most of her came down to the Hide, and the first brothers laid her where she lay. Some of her stayed up. It hangs in the dark still, uncounted, and every piece is a wound the Count must carry. We call them the Unfallen.</p>\n<p>The roof is a frame of long bones with cords strung across it, nine by nine, eighty-one squares. You lie on the stone beneath and count one square a night, reduce it, and write the figure and its root on that square's leaf. Eighty-one clear nights to count the sky once. I have counted it thirty-three times, and it has never come out the same. Some squares are fewer. More are more. My master said the fault lay in the eye. I have made my eye very good.</p>\n<p>When one falls you will know it: a white line drawn down the dark, gone before you have breathed. Do not cry out. Lay your hand on the cords where it crossed, read the angle off the frame, and go down the stair and wake the Seekers. Three brothers and three of the standing dead walk the line to where it ends. Twenty days, or a year.</p>\n<p>What they carry home is always bone. White, colder than any bone of the niches, with no marrow in it, the ends broken clean. Once it was a thing nine of the dead carried between them, and no niche would hold it, and they cut a new one. We lay each on the Wall of the Come Down, with its number, and a name, which is the night it fell. The Wall stands at two thousand and sixty-eight. Root seven. It wants two.</p>\n<p>The Wardens say the pieces come from every part of her, and so they do not fit one another. I have set two side by side under the lamp. Neither fits a bone of a man, or any bone the Seekers have brought. Each is shaped to fit something. And on the Ninth Stair they weigh more than they weighed on the road. The Seekers say so. The Wardens say the road is long.</p>\n<p>One Seeker came back alone, and would not say where the line had ended. He said only that it was still coming down when they reached it.</p>\n<p>I pray for them to fall. Nine times a night I ask the Mother to call her pieces home. When the last is down the roof will be dark, and the Count will close, and she will rest, and I may sleep. I have pictured that dark, with nothing in it. It is the most beautiful thing I know.</p>\n<p>Some nights a point is gone that did not fall. You will look for the line, and there will be none. The Warden says those were never hers, and we do not write them. I keep them on a leaf of my own, under the stone. The dark where they stood is smoother than the dark round it.</p>\n<p>On the lower stairs they say an Ossuarch can ask the Unfallen to come down early. The Rule says a bone comes down when it is ready. I did not believe it.</p>\n<p>In my twenty-first year, counting the first square of the ninth row, I saw a man standing on the low ledge of the Pogost, where no brother goes by night. Very tall. No lamp. He raised both palms to the sky, as a man braces a ceiling, and they came down. Many lines at once, every one dead straight, all falling to him. They made a dry sound, like a floor of bones settling. The slope below him rang for as long as it takes to sing the Chant nine times. Then he turned his face up toward the roof. I lay flat on the stone until the grey came.</p>\n<p>In the morning the Seekers went down with baskets. They brought up four hundred and nine pieces, and I wept, because four hundred and nine were down and would never hang again. That night I counted the square they had fallen from. It held the number it had held the night before. I counted it nine times. I have counted it every night since. It has never been fewer.</p>\n<p>Keep your eye good. Count your square. Pray them down in their time. If you see a man on the low ledge with his palms raised, do not count. Go down the stair.</p>\n<p>The leaf under the stone is yours now. When I began these pages there were seven on it. There are eight.</p>"}, {"title": "What Rose in the Well at Old Upright", "by": "Spoken by a pilgrim woman at the gate of the Ossuary of Nine Stairs, where she came asking to be written in the book. A tallier set it down on the back of a spoiled niche-tally. Where she said a thing he could not spell, he left a gap and marked it. The gate stayed shut; the leaf was kept.", "html": "<p>Three days past the thighbone citadel the ribs stand closer, and the ground is white as a washed plate. We counted our fingers at night, as the road tells you to. Ten, and ten. On the third night I had ten, and a knot in the smallest finger of my left hand, a hard warm seed under the skin, just at the joint. I told no one. The others kept to the ribs. I went where the ground sloped.</p>\n<p>The altar is a slab laid across four knuckles, each as tall as I am. Letters on its face: OLD UPRIGHT. Under them, other letters, worn to a shine. Behind it, the well. No rope. No windlass. A lip of polished stone, knee-high to something taller than any man, and a stair cut down the inside wall, each step too deep for my legs. The air coming up was warm. It smelled of your lamps, brother, the sweet heavy tallow, and under that of a stair where a great many feet had gone down.</p>\n<p>I set my bowl on the slab. My lantern I set beside it, and the flame leaned away from the well and stayed leaning. I said the Mother's names. I know three.</p>\n<p>It came up slowly. Put it together yourself. I never could hold it all at once.</p>\n<p>First the top of it. Long pale fingers, pressed tip to tip, rising out of the dark in a peak, the way hands are held for prayer, the way a crown is tall. Then more fingers under those, and more, joint after joint. I counted them, as a pilgrim counts. I had thirty. Then thirty again. Then I had lost my place, and the joints went on coming.</p>\n<p>Below the fingers, where a face would sit, there was a bowl of bone, worn hollow, like the middle of an old step. Nothing looked out of the bowl. The bowl was turned toward me.</p>\n<p>It stopped at the lip. Most of it stayed down. I know that, because the well kept breathing around it, warm, and slow, and a long way down.</p>\n<p>The brothers call it the Marrow Pontiff. The salt-carriers call it the Herald of Bone, and walk wide of the knuckles. It was older than the altar. Older than the letters under the letters.</p>\n<p>It spoke out of the bowl, and out of the stair, and out of my knees. Slow, the way your talliers read off a wall. The words were ours, set in an order nobody sets them.</p>\n<p>It said, <i>Early.</i></p>\n<p>It said, <i>Has she fallen. Is it after.</i> I could not answer. It said, <i>After,</i> as though I had.</p>\n<p>Then it counted me. I felt each number come down on a bone like a thumb laid there, from the crown of my head to the soles of my feet. Two hundred and six. A whole woman, I thought.</p>\n<p>It said, <i>Open.</i></p>\n<p>Two and nought and six. Eight. I understand it now.</p>\n<p>It said [<i>here she made a sound the tallier could not write; he gives it the width of three words</i>]. It said, <i>Carried. You are carried in her. Carried thing, in her.</i> Then, <i>Knelt, once. Inside. When she held.</i> Then, <i>Kneel, as you knelt.</i></p>\n<p>I asked what for. I had walked three days.</p>\n<p>It said, <i>She has stood since before. She has never set it down. Someone kneels for her.</i> And after a long time, lower: <i>One bone. She sets down one.</i></p>\n<p>So I knelt. On the white ground, by the knuckle, with my face toward the bowl. It was warm under my knees. The well breathed.</p>\n<p>It counted me again.</p>\n<p>Two hundred and seven.</p>\n<p><i>Closed,</i> it said. <i>Nine.</i></p>\n<p>Then it went down. Joint by joint, the way it came, and the fingers last, still pressed together, and then there was only the warm air, and my lantern, still leaning.</p>\n<p>I carried away my bowl and my lantern, and the knot in my finger. It is a bone now. I can move it under the skin. It is warm, the way your chrism is warm. My knees are warm too, and they bend slower in the mornings, and I have stopped minding.</p>\n<p>That is why I came to your gate. Write me in the book, brother. Write me by my number. Two hundred and seven. A good root. I have a closed count. I am ready to be laid.</p>\n<p>Hold out your own hand, if you doubt me. Count it.</p>\n<p><i>[She would give no name. The Warden came down to the gate and asked her to hold out her left hand, and he counted it, and he sent the tallier inside. The tallier writes that the Warden stood at the gate a long time after, with his own hands behind his back.]</i></p>"}]}, {"id": "peak", "name": "The Gilded Peak", "sub": "of the monks on the knee, and what they carried up the stair", "epi": "An old monk on the landing, when I asked him whom they waited for: \"Someone who is late, and will come.\"", "preface": "<p>Hold one hand out in front of you, palm up. Open it wide, then close it, slowly, nine times. Let the hand learn it before the head does. The brothers of this chapter would have said the head never learns it at all.</p>\n<h3>Going up</h3>\n<p>The Peak is the god's knee, broken up through the Hide when the body came down, and the monks cut their stair into the bone of it. I first climbed it in the summer of the one thousand and seventy-first year, while the Peak was whole and rang at noon. A brother met me at the landing where pilgrims who would go no higher left their bread. He did not ask my business. He only fell in behind me, a little lower on the stair than I was, and climbed the rest of the way there. I did not understand until I stumbled, near the top, and found him already braced to take my weight.</p>\n<p>From the courtyard the miasma lay in the valleys like milk left standing in a pan, and the lowland towns under it showed only as lamps.</p>\n<p>They called themselves the Kinrei-shū, the Gold Bell. A monk owned a bowl, a robe, and later a lantern, and nothing else. I sat one evening in their hall among them while they chanted. How the chant goes is not mine to give; a woman who sang it four winters tells you below, and so does the man who taught it. I will tell you that in the third of the long quiet places, the brother beside me pressed his shoulder hard against mine, and kept it there, the way you steady a man at the lip of a drop, and that I did not know until then that I had been leaning.</p>\n<h3>The bell at noon</h3>\n<p>At noon they struck the big bell that hangs over the courtyard. I stood a few paces from it. The beam went in, and I felt the blow in my teeth and heard nothing, and every monk round me let out his breath at once, content. Two days later I was in a market in the valley when the noon stroke came down the mountain, and it rang so long the bread-sellers stopped calling to let it end.</p>\n<p>A bell-caster tells you in this chapter what his wife heard. I stood in that market. She heard right.</p>\n<h3>What they waited for</h3>\n<p>Going down at dusk I found an old monk sitting on a landing with an empty bowl across his knees, and I asked him to whom they prayed. He said the fifth name, the one I wrote once in the first chapter. He said it as a man speaks of a guest expected for supper, who is late, and will come. I have not heard it said so gently anywhere else.</p>\n<p>Every other house on the Hide bars its door against the Unsaying. The Peak laid a place for it. I have stood nearer the Long Silence than those monks ever meant to stand, and I cannot call it what they called it. And still, every argument I have made against them since, I can hear the old man on the landing answering, in one breath, without raising his eyes from the bowl.</p>\n<h3>Of the gold, and the saints</h3>\n<p>They gilded everything they meant to honour, and they meant to honour it by making it hard to see. The goldbeater in this chapter tells you how, and why, better than I can. I was never let into the sanctum. I will tell you only what I saw from its door on my first visit: a row of niches going away into the dark, and in each a shape so bright at the edges that the eye slid off it, and a novice with a dry cloth going from one to the next along the row, reaching up.</p>\n<p>I have my own belief about the saints. I keep it in the bag with the gold. The goldbeater was nearer to them than I ever was. Read him slowly.</p>\n<h3>Of the bowls</h3>\n<p>They came down to the valleys at dusk in fours, barefoot, with small bells at their belts, and asked for one thing. The lowlanders gave it, and forgot, and fed the monks bread for the forgetting. Where the gifts went, the Peak did not ask, or asked and got an open hand for answer. The bread-woman, the magistrate and the abbot each tell you a piece of what came of it. None of them has the whole. I have not either, and I have walked further along it than they did.</p>\n<p>A monk of the Peak carried his teacher in his lantern, and the teacher burned his own name for fuel, slowly, down to nothing. I once walked a day behind a brother carrying his teacher down to the valley. He set his feet on each riser as though the stair were ice over a deep pool, and at the bottom he sat down on the last step and held the lantern in his lap until it was dark.</p>\n<h3>The tenth night</h3>\n<p>The Pale Order learned that the Peak gilded its dead and gave them to the Quiet, and left the Count nothing. To the brothers of the Ossa there is no worse waste on the Hide. Their standing dead waited nine days under the mountain, and on the tenth night they climbed.</p>\n<p>It was the spring of the one thousand and ninety-eighth year. The brothers will tell you that year comes down to nine, and they say it as though it closed the matter. I was in the valley, at a ford, with my back against a stone. I heard nothing all night. I have come to think that nothing was what there was to hear.</p>\n<p>The monks did not fight. When the sun came, the flags of the courtyard had gone to glass and the sanctum stood open. What happened between, no two tellers give the same way, and I was not there to correct any of them.</p>\n<p>I went up in the thaw. I did not go into the sanctum. I stood beside the door, never in front of it, with my shoulders to the stone, and looked down at my own palm until the sun was at the top of the sky. I could not have told you why I stood so until a letter came to me two winters later, folded inside a bowl. The Order's scribe gives the count of what he found. He has the stair right both ways.</p>\n<h3>Of the one who comes at dusk</h3>\n<p>A barefoot man with a bowl walks the lowland roads and asks for a name, and a black flame follows him a little behind. What he strikes leaves glass, or dust, or nothing. Some say he was down in the valleys with his bowl on the tenth night. Some say he was first up the stair after, and opened the doors. Some say he walked out of the empty niche.</p>\n<p>I know this much, and set it beside what they say. In a pilgrim-roll at the Ashwake scriptorium, in a hand three hundred and four years older than the fall, a scribe writes of a barefoot brother with a black lamp who asked him for a name and was given one. I saw the bowl go past Aumery's door, alone, while the Peak still sent its brothers down in fours. And once, at the Dry Ford, he held the bowl out to me. I have more names than most, and I gave him none. He waited, and went on down the road. I still cannot say whether he was asking for a name or offering to carry one.</p>\n<h3>Seven, from the valleys under the knee</h3>\n<p>The lamp-seller ran from the Peak after four winters. I paid for a lamp in her shop as the light went and waited while she put up the boards. My change was short by one. She was right to keep someone beside her. She still does.</p>\n<p>The bread-woman gave her gift to the bowl, and sets her table for more than one. I slept above her ovens through one cold season, and her words went down on the back of her own reckonings. On my last night she laid the table for three. I heard it from the loft. I did not go down to ask.</p>\n<p>The goldbeater mended pots at his bench the whole time he talked. He asks that the barefoot man be told something. I have not told him. He has not asked me to.</p>\n<p>The bell-caster I met mending pots beside a ring of stones, and oil was what he wanted for his talk. He tried to sell me the little bell he never cast, as he had tried to sell it to everyone. I bought it.</p>\n<p>The magistrate's report came among his effects, from a nephew who could not read it for the white. The clean places have spread since I bought it. I have met the clerk the magistrate could not name. The clerk remembers him well.</p>\n<p>The Order's scribe wrote to his Warden, and a sweeper copied it without leave. He asks for his own name to be written back to him. I have read the Order's book where it is kept. His number is there, whole.</p>\n<p>Last, the abbot. The youngest of them carried the letter down, a lowland priest kept it folded in a bowl, and I bought the two together for bread and salt. I think I know who the youngest was. I will not put her in two places in one book.</p>\n<p>Now open your hand, and leave it open until you turn the page.</p><div class=\"carve\">UNDER THE GOLD<br>THEY WERE STILL LOOKING<span class=\"under\">Scratched on the right-hand leaf of the sanctum door after the fall, shallow, as a nail scratches, with gold dust still in the strokes. No scribe of the Order could match the hand. I read it from beside the door with my back to the wall, and I have never read it from in front.</span></div>", "relics": "<div class=\"plates\"><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Begging Bowl of the Peak</h4><p>A plain wooden bowl, its rim darkened by hands. It came to me from the lowland priest with the abbot's letter lying in it, and the priest was glad to see it go.</p><p>It is heavier than its wood. At an inn by the Dry Ford I set it on a table among carters. Their talk stopped, and started again on some other matter, and none of them could say what they had been discussing. One of them wept a little and laughed at himself for it.</p><p>Do not speak into it. Do not speak near it.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Gilded Leaf</h4><p>One leaf from the last book the goldbeater beat, the one he never carried up. It stirs when you breathe near it, and now and then a moment before you do.</p><p>At its middle is a pinhole fine as a hair. I laid it over the back of my hand one noon, and the hand was gone, and light lay on my wrist in the shape of fingers. The pinhole stayed dark. Something small and warm crossed it, as breath crosses a mouth.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Black-Flame Lantern</h4><p>A lamp of the Peak, the gold on its case worn through at the handle. The flame is black, and still I can see the road by it. A brother of the Peak who had been in the valleys on the tenth night handed it to me at the foot of the old water-cut in the thaw, and asked me to hold it while he went up to open the doors. I held it. He did not come down.</p><p>He told me whose name it burns. I have never said that name within its hearing. Once at a fire I said another, a woman's, and the flame drew in toward me like a hungry man leaning toward a pot. It is very small now. I carry it carefully, as he did.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Tongueless Bell</h4><p>A bell the size of a cupped hand, gilded so thick it shows no face, only light. No clapper and nowhere to hang one, no seam and no mark of a mould. The bell-caster found it in the cold ash of his own furnace and swears he never cast it. I gave him more lamp oil than it was worth to him, and he let it go as a man sets down water he has carried a long way.</p><p>It stays cold. The Moor ash rides beside it in my bag, and the ash on that side has gone pale. Some nights I wake and the wrapping is warm. It has never rung where I could hear. Each time I find the wrapping warm I have lost something, and I have not yet found out what.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Saint Under Gold</h4><p>A small gilded figure the length of my forearm, seated, hands open on its knees. Nine such sat on the landings for pilgrims who would climb no higher. I took this one from its landing in the thaw after the fall, because the miasma was coming up the stair and I would not leave it anything.</p><p>I wipe its face each morning with a dry cloth. By night I have to wipe it again. Once, with a knife-point, I lifted the gold at the throat. There was more gold under it, and under that something warm. I laid the gold back and pressed it down with my thumbnail until no seam showed.</p><p>I have not lifted it again.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Courtyard Glass</h4><p>A palm-sized piece of dark glass, flat as still water, from the edge of the courtyard where the flags end. It is warm at every hour.</p><p>There is one shape in it, very faint, small and on its knees. Wherever on the Hide I take it out of the bag, the shape is facing the Peak. On bad roads I have steered by it.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Burnisher of the Beating-House</h4><p>A finger-long tooth, fixed into a dark wooden haft. The goldbeater put it in my hand when I paid him for the leaf. He would take nothing more for it, and kept his eyes on his bench while I held it.</p><p>Nobody on the mountain would say whose teeth they were. I have never pressed gold with it, and the edge goes on wearing down.</p></div></div>", "relics_intro": "What I brought down from the knee. Keep them in the dark of the bag, and never set them out at noon.", "works": [{"title": "The Stair, Counted Downward", "by": "The talk of a lamp-seller in a lowland market town, who was four winters a novice on the Peak. The Codex's copyist bought a lamp from her at dusk and stayed while she closed her shutters.", "html": "<p>I sell lamps. Clay and brass, wicks of twisted linen, oil by the measure. Down here the miasma lies in the streets each evening, and people buy light like bread.</p>\n<p>From the Peak you could see it below us, lying in the valleys like milk in a bowl. We chanted, and it kept to the low places, and the pilgrims thanked us.</p>\n<p>Each syllable of the chant is sung on a single breath. You take the syllable as long as your chest allows, and then comes the silence, as long again. Novices counted the silences on their fingers so as to know when to breathe. We were taught the syllables were only fences, and the silences were the prayer. And we were taught never to listen to a silence alone. There was always a shoulder against yours in the hall, always someone breathing beside you. I thought it was kindness.</p>\n<p>In my fourth winter I woke in the dormitory in the long silence of the night. Everyone around me lay holding their breath, asleep, all of them, the whole row. So I held mine. And I heard the room the way a room sounds when someone has just come into it and stands by the door. I heard how near. I will say nothing more of that night. I left before the thaw.</p>\n<p>My work was the gilding. I beat the leaf between sheets of pressed bark until a breath would lift it, and laid it on bells and lamp-cases. Gold in full noon gives back so much light that the thing beneath it goes away. To be looked upon and unseen: that was holiness.</p>\n<p>Only the masters gilded the saints. Novices were let into the inner sanctum at dawn to wipe their faces with a dry cloth, because the dark tracks ran from their eyes each night. By evening the tracks were back. We were told the saints wept for the living, for us, for the valleys. Once I held a loose flake to a saint's cheek for my master, and the flake rose from the gold toward me, a hair's breadth, and settled again. My master said the sanctum has drafts. The sanctum has no window.</p>\n<p>They taught us the two hands on our own fingers. Open was the light that leaves nothing to look at; shut was the dark that leaves nothing to hold. Open, shut, a thousand times a day, until my hands did it without me. They still do. Some mornings I wake with my fingers closing.</p>\n<p>Twice I went down with a bowl behind my teacher. We asked for names. A woman in this very town whispered her husband's name into it. The next season she sold us bread and asked whom we were praying for. I asked my teacher where the names went. He said, \"To the open hand.\" Then he folded my fingers shut over my palm, and held them there until I stopped asking.</p>\n<p>On the stair a teacher walks one step behind and below his novice, so that if the novice falls, she falls into him. His lantern burned black. His own teacher was in it, going out slowly. I climb the ladder to my loft and I still feel him there, one rung down. The night I ran, I went down the stair alone, and that was the first time in four years nobody walked behind me. I counted every step. There were more going down than I had climbed.</p>\n<p>A pilgrim told me of the fall at my own counter. The standing dead of the Pale Order had gone up the stair for the bones, and by morning the courtyard was black glass and the sanctum doors stood open. I sold him a lamp and gave him the wrong change. That night I tried to say the names of everyone on the Peak, the whole row in the dormitory. I lost some partway. My teacher's name I had. Each year I have it a little less.</p>\n<p>They say there are shapes in the glass, all turned toward the sanctum. I think of where my mat lay.</p>\n<p>Now they speak of a barefoot beggar on the lowland roads, with a bowl and a black lantern, who comes at dusk and asks for a name. I close my shutters at dusk. I fear it is my teacher, come down the stair behind me at last. I fear more that it is one of the faces I wiped. And I fear he will stand where you stand now and hold the bowl out, and I will know which name he has come for.</p>\n<p>Buy your lamp. Walk carefully. Between one lamp and the next I hold my breath for the length of one syllable, and I keep someone beside me.</p>"}, {"title": "The Second Cup", "by": "From a bread-woman of the lowlands, who gave a name to the Peak's bowl. A copyist lodged a winter in her loft, and wrote this on the back of a flour-tally the night before he left.", "html": "<p>They came down at dusk, when the miasma was settling into the lanes. You heard the bells before you saw them: small brass bells, and bare feet on the stones, and no voices. Three or four monks, thin as rakes, the gold on their lamp-cases catching whatever light was left. Children ran indoors. The old women went out with bread, because you fed the monks, whatever else you did.</p>\n<p>They never asked for the bread. They held out the bowl, a plain one, black at the lip, and said the one thing they said. Put a name in it.</p>\n<p>I had carried mine to the door for a month, the way you carry a stone in your apron. I remember that I was sure. I remember my hands were floury, and that I wiped them first, as if the bowl would mind.</p>\n<p>You lean down and whisper into the wood. The name goes into the bowl the way breath goes into a cold morning: you see it, and then you see the morning. The monk laid his palm flat over the bowl, closing it, and bowed, and walked up the lane with the others. A small one walked behind him, a girl, one step down. I watched the bells go up the road until the miasma had them.</p>\n<p>Then I went inside, and there were two cups on the table, and I could not think why I had set two.</p>\n<p>That was the first night. I stood a long while with the second cup in my hand. It was warm. I drank from it, because it seemed a waste.</p>\n<p>The forgetting has no edge. Where a thing is lost you can find the shape of the place it was. This has no shape. It is the gap where a tooth came out, and the tongue goes to it all day, and each time the tongue arrives it is surprised.</p>\n<p>The house remembers more than I do.</p>\n<p>There are boots by the hearth that come up past my knee. I cannot wear them. I oil them each spring.</p>\n<p>There are notches cut in the doorpost with a knife. They climb to the height of my shoulder and then they stop. The lowest is at my knee. The knife is in the drawer. I have never cut a notch.</p>\n<p>There is a hollow in the straw of the bed, on the side nearest the door. I sleep in the other side. I turn the straw and by the new moon the hollow is back.</p>\n<p>At the table one stool is worn pale where a hand rested on it to rise. My hands rest elsewhere.</p>\n<p>I think it was a man. Some mornings I think it was a child. The lamp-seller by the well says it was my husband, and says she knows because she was the girl who walked behind the monk. She told me this across her counter, and would not look at me, and gave me the wrong change. I have no husband. I have a ring I cannot get off over the knuckle.</p>\n<p>Whoever it was, I wanted them gone. I remember wanting. The wanting is still here, whole, with nothing in it, like the bowl.</p>\n<p>Every season since, I set two cups. I have stopped asking myself why. The second one is always warm.</p>\n<p>This year, at the start of the cold, the beggar came. Barefoot, as they were. A shawl the colour of old blood. The bowl. Behind him, a little to one side, a lantern with a black flame, and the lane was lit by it all the same. The monks came in fours. He came alone, and there are no more monks. The Peak is glass.</p>\n<p>He held out the bowl and said it. Put a name in the bowl.</p>\n<p>I told him I had already given one. He waited. I told him I wanted it back. He waited the way the chant used to wait, the length of a syllable, the length of the silence after it.</p>\n<p>I looked into the bowl. The wood was dark, dark past where the bottom should be. And from far down in it something breathed out, slow, the breath of a sleeper on the side of the bed nearest the door. I knew the breath. I knew it the way you know your own stair in the dark.</p>\n<p>I shut my door on him. I sat with my back to it until the bells had gone.</p>\n<p>I do not know if I was afraid he would take another name, or afraid he would give one back.</p>\n<p>The notches are the same. The boots are oiled. Tonight I have set three cups, and I cannot think why.</p>"}, {"title": "Thin as Breath", "by": "Told by a lay goldbeater of the Gilded Peak, who mends pots now in a lowland river town. He spoke at his bench, and did not stop working while he spoke.", "html": "<p>I was never a monk. I ate. The monks were light, and I was the weight that kept their gold coming: a lay man in the beating-house at the foot of the sanctum stair, with three hammers, an anvil of the god's own bone, and a stone for a pillow.</p>\n<p>You take the gold as the pilgrims give it: rings, teeth, a coin with the face worn off. You melt it and pour it to a bar, roll the bar to a ribbon, and cut the ribbon into squares the size of a thumbnail. Each square goes between two leaves of pressed bark, two hundred squares to a book, and you beat it. Half a day with the heavy hammer. Cut each square in four, and beat again with the middle hammer. Then again with the light one, until the thumbnail is as wide as your palm and a breath will lift it.</p>\n<p>Hold good leaf up to the sun and the sun comes through it green. My master said that was the one colour the gold had kept back. I was proud of my green. Mine was the thinnest on the Peak. Laid on a bell, it took the tone into itself and gave the bell no weight.</p>\n<p>The rule was Radiance. A gilded thing must be erased by light. In full noon you should look straight at a saint and see a brightness in the shape of a man, and no man. Any seam, any lifted edge, any place where the size shows through, and the eye catches on it, and a face comes back. A face coming back was a fault in the work. I was beaten for one, as a boy. I have made none since.</p>\n<p>We laid it on size boiled from sap and ash. Never from bone. Once a Pale Order brother climbed up to buy our size. My master burned the pot after.</p>\n<p>Then the burnishing. Each gilder had a burnisher: a tooth set in a handle of black wood, long as a finger and worn smooth as water. Whose teeth they were, nobody said. They were warm in the hand in winter.</p>\n<p>For the saints I beat the leaf, and the masters laid it. Every dawn a novice wiped the dark tracks from their faces, and every few seasons the tracks had worn the gold thin at the eyes, and it was laid again. I thought it was the damp.</p>\n<p>When old Master Enshō went quiet, they called me up to the sanctum myself. He sat in his niche with his hands open on his knees, in the posture of the open hand. The monks said he had gone into the silence, and I was to lay him while the shape held.</p>\n<p>He was warm. I put it down to the lamps.</p>\n<p>I laid the brow, and the cheeks, and the lids. The lids were hard, because they were soft. At the mouth I held the leaf out on my card, and it lifted from the card toward his lips, and settled back, and lifted again. A draft, said the monk beside me. I looked at the lamps. Every flame stood straight up.</p>\n<p>I laid the mouth. I pressed it with the tooth until the seam was gone. It was the best work I did in my life. At noon the next day I went up to look, and there was only light sitting in the niche, in the shape of a man, and no man.</p>\n<p>He wept that night, the same as the others.</p>\n<p>What I did after: I went on. I beat leaf for eleven more years. I was good at it.</p>\n<p>Only, from that day, into every book I beat I let one leaf go with a flaw, a pinhole small as a hair, at the middle. The masters never found them. At noon the light fills a hole that small. I saw to it that mine went to the saints, and I cut them so the hole fell at the mouth.</p>\n<p>I was down in the valley for more gold when the Order climbed the stair. I went back once, as far as the courtyard. From the edge of the glass I saw the sanctum doors standing open, and the niches, gold in the dusk, and one of them empty. I counted from the left three times, and lost the count each time.</p>\n<p>Now I beat tin for lamp-cases. It takes a face well. You can see yourself in it.</p>\n<p>Leave the pot. Come back tomorrow. And if a barefoot man comes to my door at dusk asking for a name, tell him I have gone to the river. Tell him I did the mouth as thin as I could.</p>"}, {"title": "The Bell with No Tongue", "by": "Spoken by a bell-caster of the lower Knee valleys, who cast for the Gilded Peak for thirty years. A copyist of the Codex found him mending pots at a waystone camp and paid him in lamp oil to talk. He tapped the rim of every pot while he spoke, and listened to each one before he went on.", "html": "<p>Thirty years I cast for the Gold Bell. The Kinrei-shū, if you want their word for it. I had the pit and the furnace at the foot of the stair, where the clay is good and grey, and every spring a monk came down with a wax model and a bowl of gold leaf and one syllable.</p>\n<p>That was the order. One syllable to a bell. Ah, or Hu, or a sound with no letter for it that the monk would sing me once, low, and I would carry it in my chest for a month while the mould dried. You tune a bell by shaving the inside, a curl of bronze at a time, strike and shave, strike and shave, until it says the one thing and nothing after. My father cast for them before me. He said a Peak bell had to die quick. Other bells hum a long while after the stroke. The monks wanted the hum shaved out of it. One stroke, a clean word, and then quiet all the way to the edges of the valley.</p>\n<p>They rang each bell once a day. Once. Then they sat and listened to the silence it left, and the silence was the thing they wanted. The bell was only there to make it longer. I asked a monk once why he did not simply sit in the quiet without the bell. He looked at me as a man looks at a child who asks why we eat. Then he said you need a door to know you are in a room.</p>\n<p>Here is the thing I cannot set down. The one time they let me up the stair, it was to hang the great bell in the courtyard. Nine men and a rope. When she was hung the old master put the striking beam in my hands, as an honour, and I swung it with my whole back. I felt her take the blow. I felt her shiver up through the beam into my teeth. The courtyard gave back nothing. No stroke, no hum. The monks all breathed out at once, pleased, the way men breathe out when bread comes from the oven right. I stood there with the beam in my hands and my ears ringing with nothing. Down at my furnace, a long walk under, my wife heard her. She said it was the sweetest bell I ever made. She said it rang a long while.</p>\n<p>The last bell they asked of me had no clapper. No tongue, no ring for a tongue to hang from. Tall and narrow, the mouth just so wide. The monk brought the size of the mouth on a cord, and I did not like the width of it. It was the width of shoulders. When the bronze was ready he came to the furnace with his bowl and held it over the crucible and tipped it. Nothing fell out that I saw. The pour weighed more than the metal. I weighed it twice. My father's scales, and they had never lied to me.</p>\n<p>I did not tune that one. They told me not to. They said it already knew its syllable.</p>\n<p>They carried it up gilded, and a year after, the dead went up the stair. You know that story. Glass in the courtyard. The doors open.</p>\n<p>I let the furnace go cold after. Nobody wanted bells. Fourteen years it stood. Then one winter I raked the old ash to use for mould-clay, and there was a bell in it. Small as a cupped hand. Seamless. No sprue, no mark where any mould had closed. Gilded so thick I could not see my face in it, only light. And cold. In the ash, it was cold, and the ash round it was warm.</p>\n<p>I did not cast it. I know every bell I cast. I know them the way you know your children's coughs.</p>\n<p>It has no clapper. I have kept it wrapped in felt under my bench. Some nights, at the hour the Peak kept its long silence, I wake, and the whole camp is holding its breath in its sleep, and the felt is warm. I have never heard it ring. I only know when it has, because after, for a while, I cannot remember my wife's name, and then I can.</p>\n<p>Tap the pot. Listen. That one's sound. That one is cracked; you hear it keep talking after. A good bell says its word and goes quiet.</p>\n<p>I would sell you the little one. I have tried to sell it before. Nobody will take it, and I cannot say why I keep asking.</p>"}, {"title": "Inquiry into the Bowls of the Gilded Peak", "by": "The report of a lowland magistrate to his assize, on the begging of the Peak's monks in the parishes under the mountain. The copy in the Codex was made from his own draft, found among his effects. The gaps are his.", "html": "<p>To the justices of the assize at ————, by their magistrate, in the matter of the bowls.</p>\n<p>I was charged to inquire whether the monks of the Gilded Peak, begging under the mountain, do injury that may be brought before a court. I took depositions in four parishes over one winter.</p>\n<p><b>First. The parish books.</b> Deposition of the clerk of Lower Stair, sworn. He keeps the book of births, marriages and burials. He showed me a burial entered whole: the date, the fee, the grave by the east wall. Where the name should stand there is a clean space. The paper there is whiter than the page around it, as paper is before a pen touches it. Asked whose grave lies by the east wall, he said a good one, well kept. Asked who keeps it, he said he does. Asked why, he wept, and could not say for what.</p>\n<p>I counted the clean spaces in his book: thirty-one. On the second morning I counted thirty-three. The book is kept locked. I believe him.</p>\n<p><b>Second. The debts.</b> Deposition of a moneylender of the river parish. She holds forty notes, witnessed, under her own seal. On each, the sum is whole, and the debtor's name is whole. The debtors swear they never borrowed. She does not remember lending. They pay all the same, each quarter day, leaving the coin on the sill of her back room. They sleep badly until it is done. The coin is gone by morning. The room is locked and she holds the one key. I sat a night in that room with a candle, awake. In the morning the sill was bare.</p>\n<p><b>Third. The killing.</b> At the crossing in Harrow Lane, in autumn, a man was opened with a hay-knife before eleven witnesses at market. I took eleven depositions. All agree on the knife, the hour, the killer's brown coat, that he wiped the blade on his sleeve and walked home. All say he is known to them. None can give his name. Three pointed to the same door.</p>\n<p>I went to that door. A man opened it, a plain man with a plain face. I asked his name. He gave it at once. I wrote it down. The page shows a clean space. I have asked him four times since, and have four clean spaces. He was civil each time. The court cannot try a man it cannot name, and I record that as the law stands.</p>\n<p>The widow deposes that her husband put a name into the bowl in the spring. She does not know whose. She says he came home lighter, and sat by the door all summer, facing the lane.</p>\n<p><b>Fourth. The town.</b> For the winter past, the town of ———— had no name. Letters addressed to it came back to the posting-house sealed. The bearers swore they had walked the road to its end and found a town there, with smoke and a bell, and could not match it to the letters in their hands. The people baked and buried. Asked where they lived, they said here. The priest rang the morning bell and said it rang short, one stroke under. At the first thaw the name came back, whole, and nobody could say from where.</p>\n<p>A monk had come down at the start of that winter, with the bowl, and a small one walking behind him. The priest says a child of the town leaned over the bowl. The child laughs when asked what it said, and the laugh is dry.</p>\n<p><b>Of the magistrate.</b> The law requires a witness who is also the inquirer to set down what bears on his own credit.</p>\n<p>My clerk has kept these papers with me all winter. His board is entered in my book of expenses at a rate I agreed. His name is not entered.</p>\n<p>In my own diary, in the ninth month, stands one line in my hand: <i>At dusk, at my door. Gave it.</i> I have no ninth month. I have the diary.</p>\n<p>I have sealed this report. My ring is cut with two letters. By lamp and by noon, I cannot read them.</p>\n<p><b>Finding.</b> No injury can be brought before the court, for want of names. I find the lowlands quieter. I find that the clean spaces are whiter than the page, and that the whiteness is spreading at the edges, a hair each week, toward the words beside them. I recommend that nothing be done. I recommend that the monks be fed.</p>\n<p>Your magistrate, ————</p>\n<p><i>[Here the draft ends. The copyist notes that the space left for the signature is whiter than the paper around it, and a little wider than it was when he began.]</i></p>"}, {"title": "Report of the Count Taken on the Gilded Peak", "by": "A letter from a knight-scribe of the Pale Order to the Warden of the Ossuary of Nine Stairs, sent in the season after the fall. It lay bound among the niche-tallies in the Order's book, and was copied out for the Codex by a sweeper who had no leave to do it.", "html": "<p>To the Warden of the Ossuary of Nine Stairs, from the scribe of the fourth chapter, sent up the Gilded Peak to count.</p>\n<p><b>The stair.</b> One thousand and ninety risers from the lowest gate to the courtyard, cut into the knee of the Mother. Gold leaf on every seventh riser, worn through by bare feet. I counted going up and I counted coming down, and the two numbers agree. I record this because it is the only count in this letter that does.</p>\n<p><b>The courtyard.</b> Where the flagstones lay there is glass, black and smooth, flat as water in a bowl. It is warm under the hand, by noon and by night. I walked it heel to toe: forty-one paces across, forty-one deep.</p>\n<p>In the glass are shapes, faint, as a face is faint in a dark window. They lie at every depth. All of them are turned toward the sanctum doors. Some stand. Some kneel. Our dead do not kneel, Warden, and I set that down without knowing what to do with it.</p>\n<p>I counted the shapes twice. The first count was two hundred and six. The second was two hundred and nine. On the third morning I did not count them.</p>\n<p><b>The standing dead.</b> The chapter sent up two hundred and six, anointed joint by joint, as written in your own hand. None came down. There is no bone on the stair, in the courtyard or in the dormitories. I found the shapes in the glass, if they are ours. The three I cannot account for I have left out of the book, as we leave out the Mother's gift. I do not know whose gift they are.</p>\n<p><b>The inner sanctum.</b> The doors stand open, each leaf the height of three men. Words are scratched into the right-hand leaf. You have them from my first letter. I will not write them twice.</p>\n<p>The saints stand in niches round the walls, as our dead stand, all facing the doors. They are gilded to the fingernail. No face can be seen under the gold at any hour. I tried them all. From the eyes of each run dark tracks down the leaf to the chin. I wiped one with my cloth. By morning the track was back, and wet. I have looked at that word a long while, and let it stand.</p>\n<p><b>The count of saints.</b> The Peak's own tally, cut in gold across the lintel, gives one hundred and eight. I count one hundred and seven niches filled, and one empty. There is gold dust on the ledge of the empty niche, and no other dust. Warden, you know I have never found a niche without dust. From the empty niche to the doors there are marks in the gold on the floor. The chaplain says a thief. The marks are of bare feet, and they go one way.</p>\n<p>I counted the saints twice more and had one hundred and seven each time, and on the last count I had the sense of someone counting with me, half a breath behind, as a teacher of the Peak walks one step behind on the stair. I stopped at a number I no longer have.</p>\n<p><b>The lamps.</b> Beneath each niche hangs a lamp of the Peak on an iron hook. Every one is dark. The wicks are whole and the oil stands full. A lamp of ours goes out when its oil is spent. These went out full. The chaplain says such a lamp goes dark when the last of the name it burns is gone. I count one hundred and seven lamps. Beneath the empty niche, the hook is bare.</p>\n<p><b>What I heard.</b> On the third night I sat alone in the sanctum with a lamp of our own. The miasma stands a third of the way up the stair now, flat as the glass.</p>\n<p>Warden, I heard a breath drawn in. One long breath, from all round the walls, from every niche at once, drawn the way the Peak's novices drew it, they say, before the syllable. No syllable came. Then silence, as long again. Then the breath again.</p>\n<p>I counted the silences as I was taught to count a wall. I cannot give you the number. Each time I set it down it is a different number, like the old lines under the Ninth Stair. And in each silence there was a small sound, dry, as of gold leaf lifting a hair's breadth from a face, toward me.</p>\n<p>I have written that the chapter sent up two hundred and six. I ask you to check it against your book. I find I have it only from this letter.</p>\n<p>Your scribe of the fourth chapter, whose name you have. I ask that you write it back to me.</p>"}, {"title": "Letter from the Last Abbot", "by": "Written by the last abbot of the Gilded Peak in the final days before the dead of the Pale Order came up the stair, and carried down the mountain by a novice. She gave it to a lowland priest, who kept it folded inside a wooden bowl worn black at the lip until the Codex bought both.", "html": "<p>To the brothers who are below with their bowls, and to whichever of you comes up the stair first when it is over.</p>\n<p>The Order's dead have stood at the foot of the mountain for nine days. In the long silences we hear them counting, a sound like beads told on a cord. On the tenth day, I think, they will climb. I send this down with the youngest of us. She is light, and quick on the old water-cut. Feed her. Do not send her back up.</p>\n<p>Hear it once more, as I said it to each of you when you came to us with your hair still on.</p>\n<p>The Hush is the god's rest. For an age the god held a space open in it, and held us inside that space, and it grew tired. The Hush came in to let it lie down. Everything on the Hide is a wound that has not yet been allowed to close. We are wounds too. We have tried to be clean ones.</p>\n<p>The Hush has two hands, and both are empty. Radiance, the open hand, erases by light: noon with no shadow, gold in full sun, a face looked upon until no one can say what it was. Absence, the closed hand, erases by dark: the palm that swallows, the gate shut over the sun. Neither hand keeps anything. So we keep nothing. A bowl, a robe, in time a lantern. You learned the hands on your own fingers. Do it now. Open. Shut.</p>\n<p>The chant is one breath. The syllable is a fence, and the silence is the field it marks. The valleys believe the syllable holds the miasma off our slopes, and they feed us for it, and I have let them believe it for forty years. I told you the silences are the prayer. I will add one thing now, since there is no time left for it to harm you. This year the silences have grown longer, and I have shortened nothing. The whole hall waits together for a syllable that is slow to come. Wherever you are, keep a shoulder against a brother's when you chant. Keep it there.</p>\n<p>The names. Every name you carried up in your bowls I gave to the open hand, at noon, over the lip of the east stone. I kept no tally; tallies belong to the Pale Order, and they may keep them. Yet I will set down this much. An empty bowl should weigh what its wood weighs. Ours have grown heavier. I asked the open hand whether it forgets the names or holds them. The hand stayed open, and empty, and that was the whole of its answer.</p>\n<p>You will be asked why we did not fight. Say this. The Order believes we gave their Mother's bones to nothing, and they are right. They believe a bone out of its place is a wound in the world. We believe a bone kept in its place is a wound held open. Both houses have told the truth, and so there will be blood on the stair. My brothers are too light to strike, and I would forbid it if they were strong. A blow is a name shouted. We have spent our lives unlearning how to shout. When the dead come, we will be in the hall, one breath each, and when each breath ends we will let the silence go on. They will cut us down and raise us before we touch the flags. That is their mercy. Let them have it. I am giving my name away.</p>\n<p>My teacher's lantern is nearly out. There is a little of him left in it, a syllable or two. I will carry it into the hall. If it goes dark before they reach the top step, I will take that as leave.</p>\n<p>Now the last thing, and I ask you to do it without asking me why.</p>\n<p>When the stair is quiet, the first of you up must go to the inner sanctum before you look for me. Open the sanctum doors. Both of them, wide, and set a stone against each. Do not wipe the faces that morning. Do not count them. Do not stand in the doorway; stand to one side, with your back to the wall, and your eyes on your own open palm. Wait there until noon. Then take your bowl and go down, and ask for names as you were taught.</p>\n<p>If someone walks with you on the stair, one step behind, where a teacher walks, let him. You were always my best at the bowl.</p>\n<p>Open. Shut.</p>\n<p><i>— written at the hour of the long silence, with the gold for the sanctum still unmixed on the table.</i></p>"}]}, {"id": "heart", "name": "The Polished Heart", "sub": "of the lodge at the head of the cold lakes, and the winter its dead came home", "epi": "Every soul is a thread. I only hold the end of it up to the light, so it can see where it goes.", "preface": "<p>If there is a glass in the room with you, leave it as it is. Do not cover it for my sake, and do not uncover it. By the end of this chapter you will have an opinion about it, and an opinion is all that valley has left to give anyone.</p>\n<h3>The valley of cold lakes</h3>\n<p>The spinning-woman under An-Vhar who gave me the Soul's name told me where to go for the rest of it. Down, she said, and west, to where the Old Spinner's threads come up through the skin like roots through a path. She wound the loose end of her yarn round my finger before she let me go, and I walked with it on for nine days because I did not like to take it off.</p>\n<p>The valley is long and narrow, with a chain of lakes down its floor, each colder than the one above it. In winter, when the lakes skin over, you can see the threads a hand's depth down in the ice: pale, lying every way at once, like hairs caught in wax. I lay on my belly on the third lake one noon and tried to follow a single strand with my eye. I lost it under my own reflection. When I stood up there was a white mark on the ice where my breath had been, shaped like a mouth, and it did not fade until dusk.</p>\n<p>The dye-women of the Lower Reach would not rinse cloth in that water. You could not tell afterwards, they said, which threads were your own.</p>\n<h3>A guest in the ring</h3>\n<p>The lodge stood at the head of the valley, where the strands in the ice lie thickest. It stands still, if walls are standing. Grey felt blows about the yard.</p>\n<p>I sat in their circle once, years before the end, at the outer edge where they put guests. A keeper began the names and they went round from mouth to mouth. I will not give them to you in order; the circle-keeper does that, below, and he kept that office far longer than I kept my place. What it was to sit there, I can say. Somewhere after midnight I noticed my own mouth moving with the rest and could not remember having started. By the grey hour I could not have said where the ring ended and I began, and I did not mind, and that frightened me more afterwards than anything I heard in that valley.</p>\n<p>A boy with grey hands brought round the water at dawn. He did not look at any of the glass in the yard. He looked at me, for longer than a boy should look at a guest, and then went on with the jug.</p>\n<h3>What the lodge sold</h3>\n<p>Every house in the valley paid the lodge for felt and ash and one lesson, and I have watched the lesson kept in a dozen houses of the dead. The women moved from room to room with the grey cloths over their arms, fast, without talking, the way you close shutters before a storm. The lodge's people believed it. The valley believed it. I believed it for as long as I was in the valley, and I have covered glass myself, in a house where a man died with nobody else to do it.</p>\n<p>The mirror-maker gives you the trade from the grinding side. He was honest. He says he believed the lesson the way he believed quicksilver runs downhill. I have never found a better way to say it, and I have tried.</p>\n<h3>The loose winter</h3>\n<p>It came when the ice first held at the ford, in 1106 by the Ashwake reckoning, three nights past the new moon. I was on the hill above the lodge when the runners came up from the Reach. I had come to buy felt. I stayed forty nights.</p>\n<p>What the Pir did in those nights you have twice over below: from the man who kept the ring round her, and from her own leaves. I leave them to each other. I will only say that on the last night I was near enough to hear the ring stop, all at once, as a mill stops when the race is shut, and that the quiet afterwards was the quiet of a room where somebody has just understood something.</p>\n<p>The dead came home that night. I saw them come. There was no walking in it. The lane held frost and ash, and then it held them. They went to the doors. The valley sent them up to the lodge, and walked behind them. I did not walk behind. I stood on the hill, where I could see the whole yard, and so I saw the felt come off the coat, and heard the dead draw breath at the glass as a congregation draws breath before it sings.</p>\n<p>One of them did not stop. The flax-beater tells of her, the blind one. She passed out of the yard where there was no gate and climbed toward me in the dark. She asked me the way. I answered her. I will not write what I said.</p>\n<h3>What he carries now</h3>\n<p>The boy with grey hands kept every one of them. They go about him small and pale, trailing their threads. The valley calls him Needle-Brother. I call him the Polisher still, because that was his name when he brought me water.</p>\n<p>He hung the coat back on its post and asked the dead into the iron, and it stood up off the post as a knight twice his height, with a lamp burning where the heart-glass had been. I watched that knight taken apart at the knees in the Hollow Wood by things that came out of the ground, and I watched the lamp in its breast draw down to a bead. By morning it stood at his shoulder again, whole. He told me he had not mended it. I believe him. The glass keeps its own count.</p>\n<p>For the rest, he sells one thing, which is a little time. A widow of the upper valley tells you how he does it. I have watched him do it three times and I have nothing to add to her, except that each time, before he began, he looked at the covered glass in the room for a while, as though he were asking its leave.</p>\n<p>I sat at a fire with him in the Barrens a winter ago. I asked him whether he was sorry. He watched the lights go round him for a long time and said, \"The villages still bring me their dead. I keep them.\" I took it then for half an answer. I have since decided it was the whole of one.</p>\n<p>I know something of where the long way went, the way the lodge sent every covered thread for a thousand years. I have walked a good distance down it. I stopped. I did not stop because I reached an end.</p>\n<h3>Leaves out of the valley</h3>\n<p>Six works follow. Three reached me through the Pale Order, who sent copyists up the valley after the loose winter to count what the lodge had left, and found almost no bone to count.</p>\n<p>The circle-keeper talked to one of those copyists in the empty yard. The copyist sold me the leaves at the gate of the Ossa for a candle, because his abbot would not shelve a thing with no count in it. I went back to the yard last thaw. The felt was still folded beside the glass, and the glass was cleaner than before.</p>\n<p>The mirror-maker's slates I had from a tinker who wanted only the metal off them. He writes of a hooded buyer at dusk who asked for a large glass. It was a small glass that man asked for. I would know.</p>\n<p>The widow talked to the second copyist, who came for her husband's bones and left without them. I have stood in her lane. The low light at the Polisher's shoulder still ducks its head at doorways that are high enough.</p>\n<p>The thread-reading I found in a box of felt oddments at a hiring fair, wrapped in a covering cloth. I paid for the whole box. The apprentice who wrote it missed one word of the stranger's. Mother Ysolt did not miss it. It is a true name of the Crone, and it is the one name of hers I have written nowhere.</p>\n<p>The Pir's leaves were sewn into the coat's lining. The Polisher unpicked them himself. At that fire in the Barrens he asked me to read them aloud to him, though I think he had them by heart, and he asked for one night of them twice. I copied them while he listened, gave them back, and watched him stitch them into his own coat.</p>\n<p>Last, the flax-beater at the ford inn, who made the third copyist pay for the ale first. His window was chalked from the inside. He has since wiped one pane clean, low down, at the height of a small boy.</p>\n<p>When you have read them, look at the glass in your room, if there is one, and decide.</p>", "relics": "<div class=\"plates\"><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Covering Cloth</h4><p>A square of grey felt the size of a hand-glass, darkened with ash at the edges, with a dent in one corner where a stone sat on it. It came to me in the box from the fair, with the thread-reader's leaf folded inside it.</p><p>It has been laid over a glass once, on a night when nobody in the house had died. The Polisher keeps the felt he pulled from the coat folded small in his own coat, and has never used it. I keep this one folded the other way from his.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Mourner's Needle</h4><p>A steel needle as long as a forearm, threaded with something pale that is nearly light. The Polisher carries three. He gave me this one at the fire in the Barrens and would not say what for.</p><p>The thread has never run short, and nobody has wound more onto it. At night, if I lie close to the bag, I hear it hum, faintly, as a string hums in a wind that is not blowing.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Pir's Mirror</h4><p>One of the small hollowed rounds from the Pir's coat, rimmed in black iron, with a thumb-print pressed into the wax on its back. It fell from the coat's shoulder on the first night the iron stood up, and the Polisher let me keep it.</p><p>Look into it and you see your face, then a face behind yours, then another behind that. The last face in every round of that coat is turned away. When I count, I begin at the second.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Polishing Felt</h4><p>A pad of grey felt worn through in the middle, stiff with ash and a little blood. I picked it out of the yard where the felt blows about.</p><p>It is worn in the shape of a hand, and the hand is smaller than the Polisher's is now. The circle-keeper knew at once whose it was. I offered it to him. He would not touch it.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Heel of a Loaf</h4><p>The heel of a small dark loaf, hard as a whetstone, with a thumb pressed deep into the crumb. It has been in my bag a long time.</p><p>In that valley a woman presses her thumb into bread she hands to someone she fears for. I have not eaten it. Some mornings it is warm on the thumbed side. The apprentice's leaf tells as much as I will about how it came to me.</p></div></div>", "relics_intro": "From the valley of cold lakes. Do not set any of them facing you while you sleep.", "works": [{"title": "The Names Said Over", "by": "The words of an old circle-keeper of the House of the Polished Heart, spoken in the empty lodge-yard to a Pale Order copyist sent down to count the lodge's bones. The copyist notes that the old man polished a small round mirror the whole while, and never once looked into it.", "html": "<p>Sit, if you are staying. Sit in the ring. The stones are worn where the knees went. Forty-one of us, and the Pir in the middle, and the names going round.</p>\n<p>I kept the circle. That was my office. I laid the felt, I lit the one candle, I began the first name and I ended the last. Veiled Crone. Spindle. Weaver. Tangle. Knot. You say them down the ring, mouth to mouth, until they come back to you from the far side and you cannot tell whose mouth they left. We sat so from dusk to the grey hour. By then no one is sitting. There is only the saying. That was the teaching: say her long enough, and remembering and the self become one thread.</p>\n<p>She has four faces. The Spinner draws you out. The Weaver sets you in the cloth. The Unraveller takes you out again when your length is done. And the Hollow. The Hollow is the eye of the loom, the empty place in the middle of the weave, where the pattern shows through. Every Tithed life goes back through that eye at the end, and is woven again. I have said it at more beds than I have years.</p>\n<p>Our work was the glass. A dying thread gets lost; it wants to see where it goes. So we polished. Felt and ash, one of her names to every stroke, until the glass gave back the pattern and the thread could read its way. Then, at the death, cover every mirror. Grey felt, a stone on the corner. A soul that meets its own face stops to look. Covered, it goes on by, through the eye, into the cloth.</p>\n<p>The Pir's Coat hung on its post by the door. Iron and small round mirrors, every Pir before her sewing on more, until it weighed what two men weigh. The foundling polished it. Twenty years, that boy. Quiet. Good hands. I gave him his first felt.</p>\n<p>Then the winter. A whole valley came loose at once. We felt it in the circle before the runners came: the names went round and came back short, as though someone in the ring had stopped saying them. Nobody had.</p>\n<p>The Pir put on the coat. She sat in the middle and looked into the Hollow, and we said the names around her for forty nights. I kept the count. I kept it right. On the fortieth she spoke. I will give it to you as she gave it, and I will not help you with it.</p>\n<p><i>\"There is no loom. It goes on and on, and it never finishes.\"</i></p>\n<p>Then her hands opened, and whatever she was holding ran out of them, and she was lighter than the coat.</p>\n<p>I turn those words over every night. A loom has an end. A cloth comes off it and is folded. If the cloth never comes off, then all those beds, all that felt over all that glass, so they would pass. Pass into where. There. You see. That is the thing I cannot say in the ring, so I say it in the yard, to a stranger, with the candle out.</p>\n<p>The dead came home that winter. They stood in the doorways asking which way. And the boy took the cloth off the coat. Every mirror at once. Each of them met its own face, and stopped.</p>\n<p>He broke the first rule of the house. He kept them. He keeps them still, going round and round him the way we went round the Pir, each with its thread hanging loose behind. The villages pay him for it. They call him Needle-Brother, and their mirrors are turned about when he goes by.</p>\n<p>I say he is a thief of the dead. I say it every morning, with the first name. He took them off her road and hung them on himself like beads.</p>\n<p>And I say, at night, with the last name: he was the only one of us who heard her and did not cover it.</p>\n<p>Perhaps the Pir saw wrong. Forty nights is long. The lodge counted forty-one faces in her mirror. She counted more.</p>\n<p>This is one of them. I polish it. One of her names to every stroke. Some nights I count forty-one, one for each of us who sat in the ring. Some nights I lose the count, and there is one more at the back, very small, and it has my face, and it is waiting to see whether I will cover it.</p>\n<p>I keep the felt beside it. I have not decided.</p>"}, {"title": "The Grinder's Slates", "by": "Workshop notes of a mirror-maker of the lodge valley, scratched on the backs of his order-slates. The slates reached the Codex in a crate of broken glass that a tinker bought for the tin.", "html": "<p>Every mirror on the Pir's Coat came off my wheel, or my father's wheel, or his father's. Set that down first, so whoever reads this knows whose hands these were.</p>\n<p><b>The glass.</b> Green glass from the furnace-town, the one pane in six without a seed in it. Cut the round with the wheel and the tooth. Grind on the stone with water and grit, coarse, then fine, then finer, until the thumb finds nothing. Then pitch and rouge, and the long hours. The lodge wanted every glass hollowed a little, a dish as shallow as a cupped palm. My father said a flat glass hands you back and a hollowed one draws you in. I have ground four hundred and some. I believe him.</p>\n<p><b>The silver.</b> Tin beaten thin as a fingernail paring, laid on the slab. Quicksilver poured on and worked in with a rag until the leaf goes bright and soft. Slide the glass over, weight it, tilt it, and leave it nine days to weep off what it does not want. The lodge had me seal every back with ash-wax, and the Pir pressed her thumb into the wax before it set.</p>\n<p><b>The orders.</b> A slate came down each spring: so many rounds, of such a width, and one of the Crone's names to be said at each turn of the wheel. I said them. A customer who pays for a word over the work gets the word. Pitch, rouge, Spinner. Pitch, rouge, Weaver. You lose count of the names long before you lose count of the turns.</p>\n<p>Each spring the slate asked for more rounds than the spring before. I thought the lodge was growing. The lodge stayed the size it was. The Coat grew.</p>\n<p><b>The rule.</b> They taught every house in the valley: a soul going out halts before its own face. Cover the glass, and the dead pass on, down through the eye of the loom, to be woven again. I believed it as I believe quicksilver runs downhill. The lodge folk were the ones who had looked.</p>\n<p>I keep one question on this slate, so it stays out of my mouth. What does a dead man see, passing, in a hollowed glass, sewn on the breast of a woman who is staring into the Hollow?</p>\n<p><b>The last order.</b> Autumn, the year the valley came loose. Seven small rounds, and one great round for the heart, a hand across. The slate said: deeper than any before. I ground it eleven weeks. The Pir came down for it herself. She held it to her breast, over the coat, and said it would do. Then she said, <i>Grind no more for us</i>, and paid me for three years.</p>\n<p>They say a lamp burns now where my heart-glass was sewn. Nobody has brought the glass back. I would know it by its weight in the hand.</p>\n<p><b>What they ask for now.</b> Since the Needle-Brother walks the lanes, the valley turns its glass to the wall. So I make backs. Boards on hinges. Felt lids sewn to fit. A carter paid for a glass that shows nothing; I frosted him one with sand, and he went home glad. The widow from the upper valley wanted one hollowed the lodge way, to stand on her sill facing the lane. I made it true and charged her half.</p>\n<p>A man in a grey hood came at dusk and asked for a large glass. I hold up the test-glass for every buyer, and I held it up for him. It gave me the shelves, the door, the lamp on the hook. He thanked me and asked the price. I told him it was spoken for. I sweep the grit off the floor each night, and that night there was nothing in it to sweep.</p>\n<p><b>The faces.</b> A glass ground true gives one face. I know my trade. Still, when the lamp is low and the silver has only just set, I see my own face, then one behind it, then one behind that. My father counted three in his, and called it the depth of the glass doubling back. I counted nine in the spring. In the last round I finished, the one the lodge never sent for, I counted till the lamp went out.</p>\n<p>They wait behind me, turned the way I turn, all waiting to be asked which way.</p>\n<p>I have hung a cloth over it. Tonight I heard the cloth move, the small sound felt makes against glass when something on the far side draws near to read it.</p>\n<p>I left it covered. Tomorrow I will lift it and count again.</p>"}, {"title": "The Stitch at the Throat", "by": "What a widow of the upper valleys told a Pale Order copyist who had climbed down for her husband's bones and went home without them.", "html": "<p>When Oswy died I covered the mirrors. Everyone does. My mother did it for my father: grey felt over the glass, a stone on the corner so the cloth won't slip. The lodge folk taught us that, back when the House of the Polished Heart still stood up the valley and sent its people down with their felt and their ash. They said a soul going out must never see itself, or it will stop to look. So you cover the glass, and the dead go on by.</p>\n<p>I had felt on the little mirror, on the water pail, on the window, because a window at night gives your face back. I sat with him three days. On the third day I could not bear it.</p>\n<p>The Needle-Brother came at dusk. You know him by the sound first. Small glass knocking on small glass, all down his coat, like a cupboard of cups when a cart goes by. Then the lights. Then him, long and grey in the doorway, with his lantern hanging in the air at his shoulder and no hand holding it.</p>\n<p>I paid him the salt and the good knife. He asked for nothing. He took them.</p>\n<p>He looked at the covered mirrors before he looked at Oswy, one and then the next, the way a man counts doors. Then he sat on the floor by the bed, as the lodge folk sat in their circles, and said a name I did not know, and another, very low, over and over, until I lost the count.</p>\n<p>The needle is as long as my forearm. The thread is pale. I would call it light, if light could be knotted. He put one stitch through the cuff of Oswy's sleeve and drew it to the bedpost. Then he put the needle in at the throat, just under the jaw, where Oswy used to hum, and pulled it close, and tied it.</p>\n<p>That is all it is. A stitch at the throat.</p>\n<p>He was kept forty days. I know what your brothers say of it, so I will say it plain. He never got up. He never ate. But the room stayed warm around him, and his hand stayed a hand when I held it, and at night, if I lay close, I could hear the thread: a small sound, like a string in a wind that isn't blowing. I talked to him. I told him about the roof and the price of lamp oil. Once, near the end, he turned his face toward the covered window, and I almost pulled the cloth. I left it.</p>\n<p>On the forty-first morning the knot hung loose and he was cold as the floor, and I knew. I washed him. I laid him in the barrow, under a lid dished in like all the others. I was grateful. I am grateful still. I want that set down.</p>\n<p>This next part I have told nobody but my sister, and she told me to hold my tongue.</p>\n<p>The Needle-Brother came back through the valley at harvest. Half the village turns its glass to the wall when he passes; the other half comes out to look. I went up the lane. The wisps go round him the way sparks go round a fire, pale, each with a little tail of thread behind it. Some say they sing. I would call it asking.</p>\n<p>I counted them. I have counted loaves all my life, and I count well. I counted twice. There were more than in the spring. More than our valley has graves.</p>\n<p>One of them kept low, near his shoulder, and every little while it dipped, the way Oswy ducked his head under our lintel, though the lintel was high enough and he knew it. Its thread was short, and hung in a loop, tied off.</p>\n<p>I said his name. Very quietly. The wisp stopped. I can't tell you how a light turns, but it turned, toward the Needle-Brother's coat, where all the little mirrors are, and it stayed there, looking.</p>\n<p>The Needle-Brother looked at me then. I have thought about his face a long while. He looked like a man who has been asked the way and has no answer, and is too kind to say so.</p>\n<p>The lodge up the valley is empty now. Felt blows about the yard. The old women say its people used to send the dead on, through the eye of something, to be woven again. They say the Needle-Brother is the one who stopped sending. The old women don't say why.</p>\n<p>I took the felt off the little mirror. It stands on the sill by the door, facing the lane. If Oswy comes home asking which way, I want him to stop here.</p>"}, {"title": "A Reading Left Unfinished", "by": "Taken down by the apprentice of a thread-reader of the House of the Polished Heart, in the last autumn the lodge kept its doors. The apprentice's own notes stand in brackets. The leaf was found folded inside a Covering Cloth, sold at a fair with a box of odd felt.", "html": "<p>[The stranger came to the lower door after the grey hour, with ash dried on both knees. Mother Ysolt took the coin before she looked at the face, as the custom is. She set the reading-glass on the felt between them, face down, and I lit the one candle. What follows is what she said, as near as my hand could keep it.]</p>\n<p>Sit where the felt is worn. Hands flat, one on each side of the glass. Leave the glass to me. It is mine until I turn it.</p>\n<p>Felt. Ash. <i>Spinner.</i></p>\n<p>Felt. Ash. <i>Weaver.</i></p>\n<p>[She went round the names. Grandmother Spindle, Knot, Tangle, Unraveller, and the last with her mouth shut, as the Hollow is said. Most readings take three rounds. She took eleven. The stranger kept still the whole while, which the frightened ones never do.]</p>\n<p>There. Now it will hold a face. Look at your own, only a moment, then look past it. Do you see the thread? You will not. I see it. That is what the coin is for.</p>\n<p>You were born twice. Once in a house with its door to the ash, once on a road. One of those is a thing your mother told you. I cannot see which. Let it lie.</p>\n<p>A woman gave you bread with her thumb pressed in the middle. You had a fever in a cold month and a name you were called until the fever broke, and then another name. Your hands have smelled of iron since before they were big enough to hold it. You have been kind more often than you remember. You remember the other times. Everyone does.</p>\n<p>You have killed. That is in most threads that come to this door. Here, and here. And here, the same one again. Hm.</p>\n<p>[She stopped polishing. She held the felt still on the glass while the names could have gone once round.]</p>\n<p>You walk into the stone rings. At the ring the thread goes dark, and a way off it takes up again, and between is nothing to read. The Crone allows it. She has never said she likes it. Yours goes dark at the rings more than any thread I have held. Most pilgrims fray a little each time. Yours comes out whole. Heavier, I would say, if a thread could be weighed.</p>\n<p>Now the Unraveller's face. She comes to every thread. She is gentle with the ones who hold still.</p>\n<p>I see a road going inward. Warm. Everything is warm there. You carry a lamp, and nobody walks behind you. You go down a long way, one way, and there she is, waiting at the end with her hand open, and you give it to her. The thread goes into her hand.</p>\n<p>And comes out of it.</p>\n<p>[Here Mother Ysolt turned the glass a quarter on the felt, and turned it back.]</p>\n<p>It comes out the far side of her hand and goes on. I have never seen her let go of one. Perhaps I read wrong. The candle is low.</p>\n<p>Give me your left hand. Palm up.</p>\n<p>Here, near the start. A knot. Before the Spinner's fingers, before the house and the road. Every thread I have read begins clean, drawn fresh from her. Yours begins in a knot, and the knot is older than the thread. It is tied to something that goes back out of the glass, past the edge, where there is nothing for me to see by.</p>\n<p>Nobody in this house ties knots. The Crone weaves. Who tied you?</p>\n<p>[The stranger answered. I did not catch the word. Mother Ysolt did.]</p>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>Take your coin back. Take it. I am covering the glass.</p>\n<p>[She put the grey felt over it and set the stone on the corner, the way we do for the dead, although nobody had died.]</p>\n<p>I will read no further for you. The rest is yours, and I would rather not carry it. Do not come to this lodge again. There is nothing wrong with you. Go on.</p>\n<p>Wait. Here.</p>\n<p>[She gave the stranger the heel of the loaf from the shelf. She pressed her thumb into the middle of it first. I have never seen her do that for anyone.]</p>\n<p>[When the stranger had gone she sat a long time with her hand on the cloth. She did not lift it that night or after. She had me carry the covered glass up to the Pir in the morning, and the Pir kept it. That was the autumn the Pir sent to the grinder for the great heart-glass.</p>\n<p>I asked Mother Ysolt what the word had been. She said it was one of the Crone's names. I have said them all my life. I have never heard it.]</p>"}, {"title": "Leaves from the Lining", "by": "Pages in the hand of the last Pir of the House of the Polished Heart, written in the forty nights she sat in the coat of mirrors. They were found folded small and stitched into the lining, behind the place where the heart-glass had hung. The copyist notes that the nights are numbered in her hand, and that the hand grows lighter toward the end, as though the pen were barely touching.", "html": "<p><b>The first night of the loose winter.</b> Veiled Crone. Weaver. Grandmother Spindle. Knot. Tangle. The felt is laid and the one candle lit. Forty-one in the ring. The coat weighs what a second body weighs. The runners say the whole of the Lower Reach came loose between one frost and the next: the old, the young, the women at the dye-vats. I go down to look for their threads.</p>\n<p><b>Second night.</b> Found them. They hang out of the Hollow like the ends of a cut hem, pale, stirring a little. Each waits at the eye to be taken up. Nothing takes them up.</p>\n<p><b>Fourth night.</b> I follow one down. A dyer of the Reach; her thread is blue where her hands were. Down through the glass, down past her death, and still it runs on. I lost her behind the heart-glass, where the dark is deepest.</p>\n<p><b>Seventh night.</b> The names come back to me short. The keeper counts right; I asked him. Some mouth in the ring has stopped saying them, and all forty-one mouths still move.</p>\n<p><b>Ninth night.</b> I look for the beam. For the frame, the treadle, the hand on the shuttle. A loom has sides. I find threads crossing threads, and threads behind those, and a pattern that is nearly a face, and behind that face another face.</p>\n<p><b>Twelfth night.</b> The boy brings water and keeps his eyes off the coat. He polished every glass on it. He knows what they do. Good hands.</p>\n<p><b>Fifteenth night.</b> Faces in the deepest round: forty-one, one for each of the ring. That is the lodge's count. I set it down for them. Then forty-two. I have said nothing to the keeper.</p>\n<p><b>Nineteenth night.</b> The Reach was a cloth. Dyer knotted to carter, carter to child, the whole valley one weave. The knots are opening now, slowly, the way a sleeper's fingers open.</p>\n<p><b>Twenty-second night.</b> Four hundred beds I have sat at. Four hundred times I said it: drawn back through the eye, and woven again. Tonight one of our own dead, covered as we teach, went through the Hollow. I followed. It went on. It goes on still. I tied a name to it to find it again, and the name came back to me untied.</p>\n<p><b>Twenty-sixth night.</b> Far down, older threads. Few, and thick, and wrong in the hand. Spun while the space was still held open, before the congregation knelt. They count. They say one, and the same one again, and call it more. The glass will hold them only a moment, then it fogs from behind.</p>\n<p><b>Thirtieth night.</b> I stopped counting faces at a hundred. They are turned the way I am turned. They are waiting for me to say which way.</p>\n<p><b>Thirty-third night.</b> I write it plainly so it is written once. There is no loom. There is the Hollow, and the threads go into it, and on, and on. We covered the glass so they would go on by. We sent them the long way, and the long way has no end in it. Four hundred beds. Felt over every glass, and a stone on the corner.</p>\n<p><b>Thirty-fifth night.</b> In one small round, low on the left sleeve, nothing comes back. The candle, the ring, my own face: nothing. I have turned that sleeve toward the post, and I keep my eyes from it.</p>\n<p><b>Thirty-seventh night.</b> When I hold the pen I can see my own length hanging from the wrist. Paler than theirs. Thinner every night.</p>\n<p><b>Thirty-eighth night.</b> The Reach will come home. They will stand in doorways and ask which way. Let the glass stay open. I cannot give it as a teaching; the House would burn the teaching and me with it. So I sew it here, where only the one who mends the lining would ever feel it.</p>\n<p><b>Thirty-ninth night.</b> In the autumn I told the grinder, grind no more for us. I knew then. A little.</p>\n<p><b>Fortieth night.</b> Crone. Weaver. Spindle. Knot. Tangle. Hollow.</p>\n<p>It goes on and on. It never finishes.</p>\n<p>I will say it to the ring. Then I think I will be lighter than the coat.</p>\n<p>The names are coming back to me from the far side of the circle, and I cannot tell whose mouth they left.</p>\n<p>Whoever unpicks this: leave me uncovered. I want to see where it goes.</p>"}, {"title": "Which Way", "by": "Taken down from a flax-beater of the lodge valley, at the bench outside the ford inn, by a Pale Order copyist who was told to buy the ale first and did. The copyist notes that the inn's window was chalked over from the inside.", "html": "<p>You want the winter. Everyone who comes through wants the winter.</p>\n<p>It came on in the week the ford froze. My sister's man felt it first. He stood up from his soup and said, \"Something's let go,\" and sat down again, and never could say what. By morning the whole valley had it: a slackness, like a bowstring cut. Then the dead came.</p>\n<p>All of them. That's the part the lodge folk never tell right. Everyone the valley ever put in the barrows, old and fresh together. My wife Hild, two winters gone. My boy, the autumn before, who was four. My mother. Her mother, that I never knew, and knew at once by her jaw. And behind them the old ones, so old they had no faces to speak of, only the shape of someone standing.</p>\n<p>Nobody saw them walk in. They were in the lane, the way frost is on a pane. They stood at every door and asked which way.</p>\n<p>That was all they said. Which way. My boy said it, in Hild's voice, which he never had. I think they'd lost their own voices somewhere and were using whatever was near.</p>\n<p>Now. Three generations we paid the lodge up the valley for felt and ash, and what we bought was one lesson: in a house of death you cover the glass. A soul that sees itself stops to look. So we covered. Every mirror, every pail, every window. The women tipped the water out of the troughs. We laid planks on the well. And the ford was ice, and ice gives you back your face, so we went out in the dark and threw ash on the ford by the shovelful, and on every puddle in the lane, till the valley was grey as a hearth.</p>\n<p>We did it right. Write that in your book. We did it right, and they stood at the doors all the same, and asked.</p>\n<p>We told them: up. Up the valley, to the lodge. That's where the lodge always said they went, through some eye or other, to get woven over. So they went up, a long grey file of them in the ash, and we went behind, because what else. My boy walked with his hand out to the side, the way he used to hold mine.</p>\n<p>The lodge gate was open. The ring of them sat in the yard, and the old woman in the middle wearing the great coat, and she wasn't moving. The dead stood round the wall and asked which way, and nobody in there answered.</p>\n<p>Then the polisher came out. A boy, near enough. The foundling, with the grey hands. We knew him from market. He had the felt that hung over the coat's glass in his fist, and he'd pulled it. I saw the coat then. Hundreds of little round mirrors, stitched on.</p>\n<p>Every one of the dead looked at once. I heard it. Like a breath drawn in by a whole ring of people at once. They stopped.</p>\n<p>And then they were small. Lights. Pale, low to the ground, each with a thread off the back of it like the end of a yarn off a spindle. They went round him. They're going round him still.</p>\n<p>He walked out the lower gate with all of them, and the coat came after. I'll say no more of the coat. Folk here say plenty.</p>\n<p>That's the winter.</p>\n<p>What came after is worse. Half this valley says he stole them. That the lodge had been sending our dead on for a thousand years, and he stopped it for his own use, and now they hang on him like a pedlar's bells, going nowhere, and he sells stitches to widows out of our own graves. They keep their glass covered. They chalk their windows. They spit at the ford.</p>\n<p>The other half says he saved them. That the old woman in the coat saw something up there and said it and died of it, and whatever the lodge was sending them into had no loom at the end of it. They've took the cloth off. They set mirrors on the sill, facing out, in case.</p>\n<p>My daughter's in that half. She has a mirror at her door. I have chalk on mine. We don't eat together.</p>\n<p>I'll tell you one thing I've told nobody. My mother's mother, the one with the jaw. She was blind all her life, and blind dead. When the polisher pulled the felt, every one of them stopped. She went on by the coat, and through the far wall of the yard, and on up the hill in the dark. Asking.</p>\n<p>Nobody stopped her. Nobody's seen her since.</p>\n<p>Ask your brothers where she's got to. Ask them which way.</p>"}]}, {"id": "wound", "name": "The Precious Wound", "sub": "of the Flesh, and the town that paid her by the cup", "epi": "Blood given is a word said to someone. My brothers said it for a thousand years, and forgot to say to whom.", "preface": "<p>If you have a cut on you anywhere, bind it before you begin. Use clean cloth. While you tie the knot, tell yourself in a few words what the blood was spent on. Any words will do.</p>\n<h3>The woman on the step</h3>\n<p>The penitent who taught me the Flesh's names at Villa Llaga was the one who had been away. I met her in the spring after the Fall. She was sitting on the lip with her tin cup in her lap, the way a woman sits on her own doorstep shelling beans, and she sang the names through for me so that I would have them in the right order. I will not write them in that order. She wrote them herself, below, and she has more right.</p>\n<p>She sang the lowland name faster than the others, as if it shamed her. The true one she sang last, low, with her arms held out over the dark water in the wound, and the drops went off her elbows on the second part of it. I have not heard it sung since, and I do not want to.</p>\n<p>Afterwards she explained the goddess to me the way you explain the rooms of a house to a child who is staying the night. Then she laughed at something I had not said. I think she had stopped believing the part about the goddess having no mind.</p>\n<h3>The town on two lips</h3>\n<p>Villa Llaga is built down both sides of a wound in the Hide as long as a morning's walk, that has never closed. The houses stand shoulder to shoulder along either lip, and the gutters of both streets fall inward to it. The gutters are dark. They run the colour of old brown ink, and after a procession they go black at the edges. Anyone who writes that they run red was not there.</p>\n<p>I walked the Day of the Measure once, before the Fall, in the year 1091, standing on the upper lip in a borrowed hood. I will not describe the procession. The Rule describes it, the child describes it, the penitent describes it, and each has it right. What they do not tell you is the sound the wound makes when it drinks. It is small. It is a sound like a mouth at a cup, and it was the only sound in the town while the brothers poured, and it went on a little after the last of them had stopped.</p>\n<p>The brothers kept their accounts in blood-ink and to the drop. By their book they had paid for a thousand years. It was a thousand and eleven. They rounded down, and it is the only time in all their books I caught them rounding.</p>\n<p>That night I went down into the crypt with them, two by two, and a saint who had come out of its niche spoke to me by a name that was not mine. I answered it gently. I will not write the name.</p>\n<h3>The man at the back</h3>\n<p>I watched the sangrador that day. He walked last, as he always did. While the brothers poured he did not once look at the wound. He watched their hands, the way a clerk watches coin going across a table, and his lips moved, and I think he was keeping the tally in his head as well as on the stick.</p>\n<p>The brotherhood had found him as an infant at the feet of the faceless image over the crypt stair, and raised him, and kept him the one man in the town she never tasted. The carver who made that image tells you what went into it. The carver was not let watch. I was not in the room either. I have my suspicion. It is in the bag.</p>\n<h3>The crust, and the night</h3>\n<p>Then the wound closed over for a year, and in that year the brothers poured onto the crust as they had poured into the wound. I did not see the crust. I came too late for it, or too early; I have never been sure which.</p>\n<p>I was on the upper lip on the night it opened. What came up out of it is not mine to tell; four people in this chapter have told you, and the one who told it lying in a drain has told it best. I will tell you that what came up went past me in a doorway. They filled the door, low down, and swayed at me a little, and went on down the street. I have thought about why for a long time. I have not written down what I thought.</p>\n<p>Afterwards the sangrador opened his own arm for the first time in his life, on the crust, and drew a door in his blood, and called her down into himself. I have seen the shape of that door since, drawn on a wall in a cellar under An-Vhar, by a hand that was not his. I will not draw it for you.</p>\n<p>Everyone who tells what happened next says that she took his blood and his hands and his voice and left him his mind. I have said it too. Some nights I wonder whose mind it is now, and which of the two of them is keeping it.</p>\n<h3>What walks out of Villa Llaga now</h3>\n<p>The hunter in this chapter saw the carved Maiden bound face down on the great sewn one's back. The penitent saw her face up. I have seen her both ways, on two nights a year apart, and I do not know who turns her.</p>\n<p>I have lain in a ditch while the procession went past on the road above me. I will set down only what I have that the others lack. The small hooded ones step exactly where the one before stepped, every foot in the last foot's print, so that a hundred of them leave a track in the dust that looks like the track of one. And when he spoke, and I lay down flat as everyone who has heard him lies down, the ground under my face was warm, and it was beating.</p>\n<h3>Paid for, found, and carried</h3>\n<p>Seven works follow.</p>\n<p>The penitent's litany I took from inside her own cup at the lip, in the autumn after she sang to me. The cup was full and had been left to stand. Her hood was gone from its peg.</p>\n<p>The Rule I copied where it hangs on its chain, with the door left open at my back. My copyist's hand would not touch the last leaves, so I wrote them out myself from memory that night. I know what the scraped clause said. The bleed-wives of Kettlewick know it too. It is the one line in this chapter you could use, and I have kept it out.</p>\n<p>The sermon at the fattening house came to me from the Pale Order, who keep everything. The priest is honest, and afraid, and the floor of his hall is warm. I did not eat there.</p>\n<p>Asheth the bleed-wife talked to a brother while she drew the fever out of him. I met her later. She took a little of my blood for her jar, looked at it a long time, and poured it back into my arm, and would not say why.</p>\n<p>The carver's account was nailed inside the lid of his tool chest. He did not come back up from the crypt stair. The chest held only shavings when I opened it, and no small Maiden.</p>\n<p>The woman who lay in the drain as a child sang her rhyme to a Shrine Keeper who came meaning to seal the town's wound and never did it. I have the keeper's copy. The keeper is still at Villa Llaga, sitting on the lip with the tools laid out on a cloth, and has been these six years.</p>\n<p>Last, the hunter's tally, which a pay-clerk wrote out after she had taken her fee and gone. She is right that fewer of them stray each year. She kept back one cord. I have one of my own that I kept back.</p>\n<p>If you bled at all while you were reading, you know now what to say over it.</p><div class=\"carve\">SHE ASKED A LITTLE MORE<span class=\"under\">Chalked on the inside of the crypt door at Villa Llaga many times over, one line under the next, in many hands, the earliest lines smallest. The newest line sits no higher than a child's hip, in a backward-sloping hand.</span></div>", "relics": "<div class=\"plates\"><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Tithe-Cup of Villa Llaga</h4><p>A shallow cup of beaten tin, stained to the rim, with a line cut inside it and a ladder of older lines below. It is the penitent's cup, the one her litany was rolled in.</p><p>I poured it out on the stone at the lip before I carried it away. The stone had drunk it before I stood up. Every so often since, I find a dark ring dried in the bottom, though I have put nothing in it.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Scab-Shard</h4><p>A plate of the black crust that sealed the wound for a year, curled at the rim. The penitent kept one against her chest at night. This is hers.</p><p>It is warm, and ridged on the underside like the roof of a mouth. Laid on a table it will not stay where it is put; it always ends turned toward Villa Llaga. On starless nights I have found my road by it.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Penitent's Scourge</h4><p>A scourge of knotted cord, the knots stiff and dark, the grip bound with a penitent's own hair. It came up from the lower street after the Fall.</p><p>Its knots have been tied again since, by a hand that knew the old count and did not keep to it. There are more knots on it than a verse has strokes. I have never laid it on anyone. It rides at the bottom of the bag, under the rest.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Milagro of Tin</h4><p>A small tin heart, pierced for hanging, of the kind pilgrims nailed round the image over the crypt stair, each one a prayer for a healing. Thousands hung there before the Fall.</p><p>After the Fall they were found on the crypt floor, every one pressed in at the middle, as though something had held each gently between two fingers. I picked this one up from the lowest step. The dent is the size of my own thumb, and I do not remember pressing it.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>A Tenth Cord</h4><p>A hand-span of tithe-cord, knotted once, cut clean at both ends. I found it in the dust of the dyers' road below a lowland mill, where the great sewn one had passed in the night.</p><p>I untied the knot once, at my fire, to see how it was made. In the morning it was tied again, and there were two knots. I have not untied it since. I no longer count them.</p></div></div>", "relics_intro": "Villa Llaga let me leave with these. Wipe your hands before you touch them, and after.", "works": [{"title": "Litany of the One Who Was Away", "by": "Written by a penitent of the Brotherhood of the Precious Wound, who was on pilgrimage the year the wound scabbed. Found rolled tight inside a tithe-cup on the lip of the wound at Villa Llaga. The ink is brown, and there was no ink in the town.", "html": "<p class=\"verse\">Our Lady of the Open Side, hear me.<br>Red Mother, hear me.<br>Mother of the Wheel, She Who Turns, hear me, for I am the one who was away.</p>\n<p>I left in the spring with my cup wrapped in cloth, to carry a measure of our blood down to the Fen of Shog-Mire, where the mud keeps her beat. That was my penance, and my pride. I remember the procession that sent me off. Forty hoods, the scourges going in time, the gutters running with us. The sangrador walked at the back, as he always did, his sleeves to the wrist and his own veins never opened. We sang her names as we went. Maiden. Mother. Nurse. We sang them in the old order, and the wound drank.</p>\n<p>I was gone a year and some weeks. The stilt-folk told me the lamps in Villa Llaga had gone out one by one. I did not believe them.</p>\n<p>Hear me, Lady.</p>\n<p>The road home was quiet. I came in by the lower lip, past the brothers' rooms. Every door stood open and every peg was bare. The robes were gone. Forty pegs, forty robes, and mine gone too, though I had worn my travelling hood and left my good one hanging. The gutters were dry and brown. On the lip of the wound the tithe-cups stood in a row, one for each brother, set out neat as for the Day of the Measure. They were empty and clean. Someone had licked them clean.</p>\n<p>The wound was open again. Along its edges lay the black crust in plates, curled up at the rims like bread. I picked one up. It was warm, and underneath it was ridged, the way the roof of a mouth is ridged. I keep it against my chest at night, and I am ashamed of how it comforts me.</p>\n<p>I found no brother. No bone of a brother. Let the Pale Order come and count.</p>\n<p>Hear me, Mother, for I must tell you of your faces.</p>\n<p>In the Maiden's niche over the crypt stair, the old carvers gave her a body and never gave her a face. The stone there was left rough, and each of us kissed it on the fast-day, and each of us saw her as we wanted. My mother saw her young. The sangrador, who was found in that niche as an infant, told me once that he saw nothing there at all, and that he liked her better for it. I thought it a blasphemy. I think now it was the truest thing said in Villa Llaga.</p>\n<p>The niche is empty. The body is gone from it. There are marks in the dust of something dragged.</p>\n<p>On the ninth night I heard singing, and I went up to the lip with my cup, because I am a fool, and because it was the right tune.</p>\n<p>They came down the middle street in procession. More hoods than we ever had. The hoods were ours; I knew the patches. Under them they were small and they were many, and they moved the way we moved, the sway, the stop, the sway, and they did not go anywhere. They sang with no words in it. Behind them came the great one, sewn together, taller than the crypt door, and on its back, lashed on with rope, it carried her. The body from the niche.</p>\n<p>She has a face now. It is his face.</p>\n<p>He walked at the front. He walked at the front, Mother, where the eldest walks. His sleeves were up. His arms were open from shoulder to wrist and there was no blood on the street where he stepped. As he passed the lip he looked at me, and at my cup, and he smiled like the boy from the niche. His mouth moved. The voice that came out was a woman's, and very kind, and it said my name, which he never knew.</p>\n<p>I did not follow. I stood with my cup until the singing went down into the crypt, and then I went back to my room and lay on the floor.</p>\n<p>I have given every year. I have given in the old order and in the right measure, with the words. I do not know now who heard the words.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\">Maiden, hear me. Mother, hear me. Nurse, hear me.<br>Whoever you are now, hear me.</p>\n<p>I will fill the cup once more, as is right. I will leave it on the lip. I will leave my hood on my peg.</p>\n<p>If they come back for the last robe, it will fit.</p>"}, {"title": "The Rule of the Precious Wound", "by": "The ordinances of the Brotherhood of the Precious Wound, copied from the chained book in the chapter room at Villa Llaga. The last leaves are in another hand, in brown ink. The copyist would not set his pen to them, and wrote them down afterward from memory.", "html": "<p><b>Of the Tithe</b></p>\n<p>I. Each brother shall give to Our Lady of the Open Side one measure of his blood in the year. The measure is the tithe-cup of beaten tin, filled to the line.</p>\n<p>II. The cup is filled on the Day of the Measure and on no other day. Blood let before its day is spilled, and spilled blood is kept back from her.</p>\n<p>III. The brother shall pour slowly, from the lip of the cup to the lip of the wound. While he pours he shall speak the Words, and name what the blood is for.</p>\n<p>IV. <i>[The clause is scraped from the leaf. In the margin, in the old hand: Kept in the Elder's mouth.]</i></p>\n<p>V. Where the wound asks more, the line on the cup is raised the width of a thumbnail. The Elder cuts the new line on the eve of the Measure. The wound has asked more in every year that is written in this book.</p>\n<p><b>Of the Processions</b></p>\n<p>VI. The brothers walk hooded, forty to a procession, in the old order: the eldest at the front, the youngest behind him, the sangrador behind all.</p>\n<p>VII. The sway, the stop, the sway. No brother shall walk ahead of the singing.</p>\n<p>VIII. The scourge is laid on in time with the song, over the shoulder, left and then right, nine strokes to a verse. The gutters shall carry it down to her. What the stones keep, the stones may keep.</p>\n<p>IX. A procession goes out from the chapter door and comes home to it. It shall have a place to go.</p>\n<p><b>Of the Sangrador</b></p>\n<p>X. One brother holds the office of sangrador. He opens the welts with the small blade, binds them after, and keeps the tally of what each brother has given.</p>\n<p>XI. The one who opens others never opens himself. His veins stay shut all his life. He walks with his sleeves down and his arms dry.</p>\n<p>XII. Should the sangrador bleed by any chance, by a thorn or a fall on the stair, the brothers shall cover the place at once, and wash the ground with water from the upper well, before she knows it.</p>\n<p>XIII. The reason for the eleventh clause is given to each sangrador on the night he takes up the blade, and to no one else.</p>\n<p><b>Of the Niche</b></p>\n<p>XIV. The Maiden stands in the niche above the crypt stair. Her face is left in the rough, as the first carvers left it. No hand shall finish it.</p>\n<p>XV. On the fast-day each brother kisses the stone, and says nothing of what he saw there.</p>\n<p>XVI. Whatever is found in the niche belongs to her, and shall be raised by the brotherhood as its own.</p>\n<p><b>Of the Crypt</b></p>\n<p>XVII. At night the saints come down. The brothers go down the stair to meet them, two by two, with candles.</p>\n<p>XVIII. The dead of the brotherhood are laid in our own wall, each with his robe on the peg beside him. No brother of the Pale Order shall enter the crypt, nor count what lies there.</p>\n<p>XIX. When the saints are down, a brother shall answer to any name that is spoken to him, and shall answer kindly.</p>\n<p>XX. The brothers who go down are counted, and the brothers who come up. Where the two counts differ, the Elder shall say which one is right.</p>\n<p><i>Here the old hand ends. What follows is in brown ink, in a hand that leans the other way, set down after the year the wound scabbed. There was no ink left in the town.</i></p>\n<p>XXI. The measure is all of it.</p>\n<p>XXII. The fourth clause is restored. The Words are spoken now at the front of the procession, by one who knows what the blood is for.</p>\n<p>XXIII. The eleventh clause is struck through. The one who opens others has opened himself. There is room.</p>\n<p>XXIV. The niche shall hold a face.</p>\n<p>XXV. The robes hang on their pegs, and shall be taken down from their pegs. A brother who comes home from pilgrimage shall find his robe waiting, and it shall fit.</p>\n<p>XXVI. The procession goes out, and goes out, and goes out. It needs no door.</p>\n<p>XXVII. Where the counts differ, the Elder shall say which one is right. There is one Elder.</p>\n<p>XXVIII. The saints come down at night. They stay down.</p>\n<p>XXIX. She hears.</p>"}, {"title": "The Sermon of the Laden Board", "by": "Preached on the Feast of Second Helpings by a priest of the House of the Laden Board, one of the fattening houses that serve the Red Mother. Taken down by a Pale Order clerk, there to bargain for bones, and who wrote in the margin: \"He wept twice. Both times he meant it.\"", "html": "<p>Sit. Sit down, my sweet ones, and eat. Nobody at this table goes hungry tonight, and that is the first of her mercies.</p>\n<p>I will speak of our Mother, and you will chew while I speak. Chewing is a prayer. It is the one prayer she has always answered.</p>\n<p>She is the Flesh, the part of the god that goes on growing after the rest of it has lain down. She has no mind, and I praise her for it. A mind picks. A mind weighs a gift and finds it short. Our Mother refuses nothing. Give her a cup and she drinks it. Give her a year and she hands it back to you swollen, warm, a hundred times over. The Peak called her the Glutton, in the days when the Peak still had mouths. Glutton. The word is honey to me. What is a glutton but one who always has room?</p>\n<p>Below this floor are the given. You hear them. Do not be ashamed of hearing them. We keep them warm and fed, better fed than they ever were in the lowland camps, and every night the Nurse's cup goes down the stair empty and comes up full. We ask them nothing. We say nothing over them. We feed her the way a woman feeds an infant: in the dark, without a word, one hand cupping the back of its head.</p>\n<p>Why the given, and never ourselves? Because she has us already. Every stone and every crawling thing on the Hide is her own meat come back to the plate. The given are Tithed, the one dish she has never tasted to the bottom. Watch how she leans toward them.</p>\n<p>Now the pale brothers. One of them sits at the back of this hall with his ink and his notched sticks. Welcome, brother. Eat something. You look cold.</p>\n<p>The Pale Order says the dead belong to the frame. Let them have the frame. When you finish a joint you leave the bone on the plate, and we are a courteous house. But they come too early. They come while the meat is still on. They anoint a man in his own flesh and carry him up to their high ledges, and the wind eats him, and the Mother goes without. The Bearing Mother is a frame, brothers. A frame is a table. Our Mother is the meal set upon it, and the hunger at it.</p>\n<p>And now Villa Llaga. I will speak gently. They were our cousins at the same board once.</p>\n<p>The Brotherhood of the Precious Wound gave their own blood, in measure, and sang her names over it. We called that vanity, a fond vanity. They wanted her to listen. She drank every drop and heard no word of it. Then the wound closed for a year and opened again, and she gave back what she had been given, generously, as she always does. Her children ate the town. Men call that a horror. I call it a nursing that went on a little long.</p>\n<p>Then the boy from the niche, the one with the dry arms, drew a door on her and went in, and told her what to make.</p>\n<p>Mark me. This is the one sin. Feed her anything. Feed her your own children if the year is lean. Never tell her what to be. He gave her a road to walk. He gave her a face, and it was his. A mother who is told what to make will learn to choose, and a mother who chooses will one day choose the plate. We cut his name out of the Board's book with a knife and fed the leaf to the given. If his brood comes to our door we will feed it. If he comes, we bar it.</p>\n<p>One thing more, and then the sweetmeats.</p>\n<p>On the last feast I carried the cup down myself. The given were humming. All of them, in every pen, the same tune. It was the old processional of Villa Llaga, with her names in the old order: Maiden, Mother, Nurse. Not one of them has been south of the Moor. I stood on the stair a long while and listened, and it was beautiful. Then I filled the cup, as is right.</p>\n<p>Eat, my children. She has room.</p>\n<p>Be quiet a moment and you can hear her swallow under the floor. Lately she swallows in time.</p>\n<p><i>In the clerk's hand, below: The floor of the hall was warm. I did not eat. After, when I named the count of bones the Order would take this year, he laughed and said, \"So many? We never sent you half of that.\"</i></p>"}, {"title": "What the Mire Is Told", "by": "Set down from the words of Asheth, bleed-wife of the Kettlewick Stilts, spoken to a pilgrim of the Precious Wound while she bled him of the marsh fever. He wrote them on the back of his tithe-tally when his hand came back to him.", "html": "<p>Sit. Give me the arm. The other one. That one has given enough.</p>\n<p>You feel them stir in the jar? They know a brother's blood before the lid is off. Everything in Shog-Mire is hers, pilgrim: the mud, the reeds, the tallow in the lamps. My jar-mouths are the smallest of her, little red mouths she sends up the stilts to ask. I keep forty in the jar. The count changes while I sleep, and I let it.</p>\n<p>Now hold still between the beats. You felt it on the causeway. The mud takes your ankle and lets it go, takes and lets go, all day and all night. Your brothers write that the mire keeps her heartbeat, and that is true enough for a book. Out on the Heartbeat Flats you learn the rest. A step put down on the beat is a step lost. The mud closes on you the way a mother's hand closes on a child who wants to go. So we built on stilts, and we time our walking, and a Kettlewick girl learns to count the beat before she learns her name.</p>\n<p>The beat was slower when I was a girl. My mother said so, and hers. It quickened the year your town went dark. Your brothers do not like me to say it, so I say it to the jar.</p>\n<p>There. The first one has taken. The heat goes up your arm and the weight in your chest loosens. That is the healing. The bad blood goes out to her, and the mire drinks it, and the mire is never full. My trade is the letting and the pouring. I pour your blood off the edge of the stilts and I say what it is for. That part matters most. You have your cup and your Words. We have ours. They are older, and shorter.</p>\n<p>Your pilgrims come down the causeway every spring with the tin cups wrapped in cloth, singing. They ask me the way to the Brood-Banks, and I tell them, and they pay me in coin, and I take a little of their measure besides, for the jar. The Brotherhood calls that theft. The mire calls it the fee. They go out and pour their tithe on the banks and sing her names over it in the old order, and come back lighter, and I bleed them again for the fever.</p>\n<p>Mostly they come back.</p>\n<p>The banks are long grey ridges in the Blood Delta, soft as a cheek. The brood lies in them close as teeth in a jaw, each with a dark knot turning slowly in it. I have laid my ear on the banks at night. Under the beat of the mud there is another beat, quicker, many of them, all nearly together.</p>\n<p>Listen. The Flesh grows the way fire goes along a thatch. It has no aim. It goes wherever there is more to take. Give it blood and it makes more of itself. Give it blood and a word, and it makes what the word says. Give it blood and no word at all, and it makes children for a mother with no head. That is what the Maiden's children are. Blood with nobody's name on it.</p>\n<p>Your Rule has a clause scraped off the leaf. Every bleed-wife in Kettlewick knows what it said. We speak it into every pour. I will tell you when you are older.</p>\n<p>Last spring a line came down the causeway in your robes. Forty hoods. They sang, and the tune was right, and the words were only sound, the way a child sings a tongue it has heard through a wall. They walked the sway, the stop, the sway. They did not stop at my stilts. They did not ask the way to the banks. They knew it. The lamps guttered on the beat as they passed, and stayed guttered between. I pulled the ladder up that night, and so did every house in Kettlewick. In the morning there were forty sets of prints on the causeway going out and none coming back, and the mud had held on to none of them.</p>\n<p>Past the Ziggurat, in the Blood-Basin, something lies in the red water and takes everything the Delta sends down. The old stilt-folk call it the Mother. I call it the one who is learning. Every year the songs come up out of the mire at night a little truer.</p>\n<p>There. You are pale, and you are well. Pay Lugh for the jar; I only take blood.</p>\n<p>And pilgrim. When you pour your cup on the banks, say what it is for. Say it aloud, and say it to the end. Out there something is listening for a word it can wear.</p>"}, {"title": "The Carver's Account", "by": "Written in carpenter's black on the backs of nine commission-bills of the Brotherhood of the Precious Wound, and found nailed inside the lid of a tool chest in a shop at the top of the upper lip of Villa Llaga. The chest held nothing but shavings.", "html": "<p>Let it be written what I was paid and what I made, so the account is square.</p>\n<p>Of the Brotherhood of the Precious Wound, by the hand of the Elder, one commission: a new image of Our Lady for the niche above the crypt stair. The old image was worn to the grain. Forty mouths a year had kissed her face down to a hollow that would hold a thumb.</p>\n<p>The terms, as the Elder spoke them. Her body in full: the mantle, the hands, the open side. Her face left in the block, rough from the axe, the way the old image had it. Nobody to finish it. I asked what would be owed if I finished it by mistake. He said there would be no mistake.</p>\n<p>Timber. I went up into the Hollow Wood and counted trunks. Every tenth holds something the god was carrying, and you leave those be. I took the eleventh. Pale, straight, close in the grain. When the saw went in it ran red down the blade and warm over my wrists, and it kept running while I set the wedges. My boy was sick in the ash. I have cut a great deal of timber since. None of it bled.</p>\n<p>I let the block stand in the shop through the winter to weep itself dry. It dried everywhere but the head end. Wherever I set the gouge there, the grain wet itself again by morning. So I kept the Rule gladly. I roughed the face with the axe and let it be.</p>\n<p>She took eight months. The open side I cut the width of two fingers, the lips turned a little outward, as the Elder asked. I hollowed her back, as is right for an image, for the relics and the lightness, and closed the hollow with a board. The Elder sealed the board with his ring and his thumb's blood. What went into the hollow I was sent out of the room for.</p>\n<p>Paid: in silver, and a place in the brothers' wall when my time came.</p>\n<p>Also, the same year and after, the processional figures. The Mother under her canopy, the Nurse with her cup, twelve of the saints who come down. These I carved with faces, since the Rule allows it for all but the niche. Paid in full.</p>\n<p>The niche image was raised at the Feast. Within the year the brothers found an infant lying at her feet in the niche, and they raised him as the Rule says. I went and looked at the board on her back that same day. It was sealed as I left it. I check my work.</p>\n<p>The boy grew to be the sangrador. He came up to the shop now and then, for the smell of the shavings. He never looked at her face, the rough place. He looked at her hands.</p>\n<p>Of the fast-day of the Fall, little. They came up the street at night and filled the doorway, low down, many of them, and looked at the processional figures standing in their rows in the half-dark. They swayed at them, the sway, the stop, the sway. Then they went back down and left me. I think they took the figures for brothers who had already given.</p>\n<p>On the ninth night I saw her go by from my window, lashed on the back of the great sewn one, high above the hoods. My work. I would know my hands anywhere. She has a face now, and it is his face. I did not cut it. Whoever cut it used a gouge the width of mine and worked with the grain, the way I was taught and the way I taught my boy. The board on her back is gone. The hollow stands open. It is the length of an infant.</p>\n<p>My last carving. A man's hands want work. I kept the offcut from her block, the head end, and I began a small Maiden from it as the Rule has her, and roughed the face at dusk. In the morning there was a face in it. The chips lay on the bench in curls, my curls, the way my own gouge throws them. My gouge hung on its peg, clean.</p>\n<p>It is a woman's face, young and very kind, and the eyes are shut. I know it and I cannot say from where. Every morning I look. The lids are thinner.</p>\n<p>I have wrapped her in the niche's old veil and laid her in this chest. Tonight I carry my tools down to the crypt stair. The account is square, and a place in the wall was the pay.</p>\n<p>Whoever reads this, keep the Rule. No hand shall finish her.</p>\n<p>I kept it. Some hand has not.</p>"}, {"title": "The Tall Red Hat", "by": "Told by a woman of Villa Llaga who lived through the fast-day of the Fall as a child, lying in the drain under the lower street. Set down by a Shrine Keeper who came to close the wound and did not. The rhyme is written as she sang it, twice through, and more slowly the second time.", "html": "<p>We sang it skipping, on the steps above the gutters, with our skirts held up out of the red.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><i>Tall Red Hat comes down the street,</i><br><i>red in the gutter, red on his feet.</i><br><i>He opens the brothers, one, two, three.</i><br><i>His arms stay dry. He won't open me.</i></p>\n<p>Our mothers slapped us for it, and sang it themselves when they thought we were asleep.</p>\n<p>I was small. I remember things at the height of a hip. The hems of the robes, heavy and wet at the bottom. The cords. The bare heels of the brothers going by, and the red coming down behind them in threads, and the tin cups knocking at their belts. We called every hood the Tall Red Hat, though the sangrador wore the plainest one and walked at the back.</p>\n<p>He had kind hands. He wrapped my knee once when I cut it on the steps, and he would not look at the blood. He looked at the wall while he tied it.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><i>Tall Red Hat goes down the stair,</i><br><i>count your fingers, count your hair.</i><br><i>One for the Maiden, one for the lip,</i><br><i>one for the Mother. Don't you slip.</i></p>\n<p>Then came the year the wound was scabbed.</p>\n<p>It closed in one night, the whole length of it, under a black crust like burnt bread, and the gutters went dry. The brothers still walked, and still filled their cups, and poured them onto the crust, and the crust drank it the same as the wound had. My mother said that was a good sign. My father said nothing, and moved our beds away from the window on the wound side.</p>\n<p>That year the scab was warm. We children lay on it on the cold mornings, the way you lie against a wall the sun has been on. If you put your ear to it you heard a sound like many people breathing in their sleep, a little out of time with each other.</p>\n<p>On the fast-day of the Fall I was sent down to the lower street for thread. I heard the crust go. It was a wet sound, and very long, like cloth tearing under water. Then the screaming started up by the crypt, and I ran, and I went where we always hid in games, into the drain under the grate at the corner of the dyers' steps.</p>\n<p>I lay flat in the dust. Then the drain was full of red again, warm, up to my chin, and I lay in it all night.</p>\n<p>I saw feet go over the grate. Some were brothers' feet. Some were too many feet for one body. Something small came and pressed its face to the bars, and breathed on me, and went away. I think it went away because I smelled of the gutter, and the gutter smelled of them.</p>\n<p>Near morning I heard singing. It was our tune. The first verse, with no words in it.</p>\n<p>They came down the lower street in the robes. I knew the robes, I knew the patches and the cords. The hems were dragging, too long, and whatever was inside them went sway and stop, sway and stop, and got no farther down the street than the corner. Over and over.</p>\n<p>Then he came.</p>\n<p>He wore no hood at all. His sleeves were up, and his arms were open from the shoulder to the wrist, and they were dry. He stopped over my grate. He looked down, straight at me, through the bars, into the red I was lying in, and I looked back at him, because I could not shut my eyes.</p>\n<p>His mouth moved. What came out was a woman's voice, and soft, the way you speak to a child who is ill.</p>\n<p><i>Lie down flat.</i></p>\n<p>Then he went on, to the front of them, and they followed him, and for the first time that night they went somewhere.</p>\n<p>I climbed out when the street was empty. I have never gone back to the lip, and I have never met another child who came out of Villa Llaga.</p>\n<p>There was a last verse. We sang it only on the crypt stair, where the grown ones could not hear. I sing it now to the Shrine Keeper, so it will be written somewhere that is not me.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><i>Tall Red Hat, Tall Red Hat,</i><br><i>who will open the Tall Red Hat?</i><br><i>Nobody, nobody, nobody will.</i><br><i>Mother will. Mother will.</i></p>"}, {"title": "The Tally of Hoods", "by": "Spoken at the pay-table of a lowland town by a woman the towns hire to hunt the Maiden's strays, while the clerk counted out her fee. The clerk wrote it down after she had gone. She kept her name to herself, and made her mark with a cut cord laid on the page.", "html": "<p>Nine hoods. Count them. The cords are in the sack with them. You pay by the cord, since a robe can be cut in two and a cord can't.</p>\n<p>Ask your question. They all ask it.</p>\n<p>How you know one is near: the singing. A tune with no words, the first line of it only, over and over. Your children know the tune. Keep them in when you hear it. They will want to go out and finish the verse.</p>\n<p>How you find it: go low. They go where the water would go. Gutters, ditches, the drain under the square. They stand at corners. A stray from the red procession walks to the corner, stops, sways, and walks back, as if the rest of the street had been taken away. The robe was too long for it. The hem was worn through from the dragging.</p>\n<p>They wear what the brothers wore. Good cloth, patched, heavy at the bottom. Some carry the tin cups still, knocking at the belt. The dead of Villa Llaga were eaten long ago, and robes wear out. I don't know where they get them now.</p>\n<p>How they die.</p>\n<p>They give. Cut one and it opens easy, the way a full skin opens, and all of it comes out warm and a little sweet on the air, and the stray stands there in it and lets it go. It won't run. It won't fight you, mostly. The small ones lean toward the blade. You open it at the cord, where the belly would be. Under the hood there's nothing you'd call a head. There's a place for one.</p>\n<p>When it's down it lies flat. Every one. Flat on its face with the arms at its sides, the way you'd tell a child to lie in a ditch. Then the singing stops, and you burn the robe, and you keep the cord.</p>\n<p>The ones that go wrong are the ones that stray long. Out past a season, a stray starts to fill the hood. Last spring I opened one near the fen-road and the place for a head had a brow in it, and the start of a mouth, set low. It had learned a second line of the tune. I burned that one twice.</p>\n<p>The giant. Yes. Everyone asks that second.</p>\n<p>I have seen it from the ridge above the salt-ditch. Taller than your granary. Sewn. The seams run in tithe-cord, knotted every hand-span, and every knot is a hood it swallowed. Something rides on its back, bound face-down with rope, painted, with the paint worn off the knees. A saint's image, a Maiden off some altar. When it walks the image nods.</p>\n<p>It walks behind him. When he stops, it stops. The strays come to it from three valleys and go into it. I have seen them walk up into the seams.</p>\n<p>I have had him twice. Once at forty paces in the reed-cut, once at twenty on the dyers' road below your mill. Twenty paces, clear light, the bow drawn, and a good head on the shaft.</p>\n<p>The first time he was kneeling in a ditch with a scourge in his fist, laying it on his own back, slow, the way you'd work a field. Small hooded things came out of the welts and stood up in a line behind him, and waited, and he got up and they followed. The strays in that ditch followed too. I had been paid for those strays. They went with him, and I never saw them again, and nobody came asking.</p>\n<p>The second time he turned his head and looked up the road at me. His arms were bare. They were open, shoulder to wrist, and dry. He said something. I was too far to hear it. I heard it anyway, in a woman's voice, very close, the way you speak to someone sick.</p>\n<p>I let the bow down. I lay flat in the road until the procession passed. I don't remember deciding to.</p>\n<p>Your headman asks why I don't take the contract on him. Nobody has offered one. The Pale Order offered once, for the giant's bones. They didn't say what they'd do with the rest of him.</p>\n<p>Here's the truth of the trade. Every year I bring you more hoods. Every year the strays are bigger. And every year fewer of them are straying. They're going somewhere now.</p>\n<p>Pay me for nine. I cut a tenth cord on the fen-road and I didn't bring it. It walked the whole way home behind me, singing, and I couldn't find the place to open it.</p>"}]}, {"id": "house", "name": "The House of Eight Million", "sub": "of the paper dolls, and the one who came up", "epi": "Give it to me. All of it. I have room.", "preface": "<p>Empty your chest before you read this. Push the air out until nothing more will come, and a little past that. Now hold, and count. You will not reach far. That short count is all the room a person has, and this chapter is about the people who try to make it longer.</p>\n<h3>A crowd on the wind</h3>\n<p>The first chapter told you the god is still breathing its last across the Hide. It did not tell you the breath is crowded.</p>\n<p>I learned its names as you learn the names of people in a crowd, one face at a time, from whoever stood nearest. The reed-cutters of the Fen say the Eight Million. Up at the salt pans they say the Long Exhale, and let their own air out through the teeth after they say it. A widow at Bellrest called it the Sigh Under the Door and laid a rolled cloth along her sill while she talked. The grandmothers of the Moor say Grandmother Wind, as though it were one more of them, sitting a little apart from the fire. The House says the Small Gods, when it says anything.</p>\n<p>When I was young, younger than anyone living would credit, I asked the Myriad its true name. I asked the ford-bell at the salt crossing, because it was said to answer. It has not rung in my hearing since. It rings once I am past, far enough off; I feel it on the road, behind the breastbone, as you feel a door shut somewhere else in a house.</p>\n<h3>The doll that stood straight</h3>\n<p>Wherever the wind snags on something, the House has been there before you: a gate of two posts and a lintel at the crossing, a stone at the spring, a strip of white paper knotted to the bell. They kept the forgotten shrines too, when they had the hands for it. Lately they have not had the hands.</p>\n<p>I let one of them sit with me once, knee to knee, at a little roadside shrine in the Fen reaches. She was grey at the temples and in no hurry. The elder in this chapter tells a novice what is meant to happen with the doll, and I will leave it to her. I will tell you what happened with mine. It did not move. The purifier watched it a long while. Then she folded its arms shut, carefully, laid it in the brazier, and turned her face from the smoke, and would not take my salt when I offered it.</p>\n<p>I have thought since about what she saw in the paper that made her do it. I have decided she saw nothing, and that nothing was what she did not like.</p>\n<h3>What they cannot carry</h3>\n<p>The House fears one thing more than its own ending. Where the servants of the Quiet have gone by, there is nothing left in the air to take in. A purifier can hold a wrong breath until it bends her. She cannot hold an absence. One summer a village at the edge of the Fen went wrong all at once, past what the purifiers could take in, and the Mouth That Eats Its Own Echo came through it. I stood beside a Fan-Bearer at the end of its lane that morning, and watched her raise the fan, and lower it, and raise it, like someone trying a key in a door that has no lock.</p>\n<h3>Of the Nave</h3>\n<p>At the one clean pool in the Drowned Fen stands the Well-Shrine, and under the water beyond it lies the Drowned Nave. The boatman, the shrine's day-book, the elder and the doll each tell you part of what the Nave gave back, and what went down to meet it, and who came up. I was not at the Well-Shrine that spring. I came a season after, in 1071 as I count years, and sat on the step where the elder with the fever had sat, and read the day-book through from the beginning while the keepers watched me to see which page I would stop at.</p>\n<p>I stopped where the hand shakes. Anyone would.</p>\n<h3>On the bank</h3>\n<p>I have seen an oni three times, and each time on a bank at night, and each time it kept out of my lamplight. The third time I set the lamp down on the reeds and walked away from it into the dark, to see what the thing would do. It came a little nearer the lamp, and stood looking at it with its hands shut against its chest, the way a cold man stands near a fire in a house where he has not been asked in. I went back for the lamp before dawn. The reeds round it were pressed flat in a ring, as though something heavy had sat by it most of the night and then thought better of it.</p>\n<p>The reed-camp women hum at their folding where there used to be words. I sat a whole evening on the mats once and never heard the words. I heard the place where they went, which is louder.</p>\n<p>I have been down the Nave stair since. The House gives a number for its steps. I made it more. I will not write my number, and I will not write which way the pews face now.</p>\n<h3>At a crossing on the Barrow road</h3>\n<p>I met the one who came up in the thaw of the year 1103, at a crossing where the lowlands had forgotten what the stone was for. Faded red and white, a wide hat, down on both knees in the mud. She set her palms to the guardian stone and put it right, and breathed on it, and the air at the crossing eased the way a kitchen eases when a quarrel is over. By morning it had moved a finger's width again. She knew it would. She went on to the next.</p>\n<p>I counted her breathing while she worked, as the reed-cutter's wife counts in this chapter; she taught me the trick, years before she wrote it down. Twenty of mine went by, and one of hers.</p>\n<p>By the day-book's reckoning she ought to be older than she looks. I have done that sum twice and put it away.</p>\n<p>The one in the haze beside her held the fan while she knelt. I bowed to it. I did not ask its name. You will not ask it either.</p>\n<h3>What the water let me keep</h3>\n<p>Eight works follow.</p>\n<p>The elder's instruction I found flat inside a doll that nobody had breathed on, at the same wayside shrine where my own doll burned. Read it next to the day-book. The hand in the day-book that shakes and then steadies is hers.</p>\n<p>The boatman's words were left under the guardian stone at the Well-Shrine gate. I lifted the stone to take them, and set it back facing the road. He says it may have been his grandfather who saw the six at the Arch. It was him. He poled me across that same week, and he has forgotten me, which is courteous.</p>\n<p>The day-book I checked leaf by leaf against the pilgrim's copy, sitting on the same step. The line at the end that the copyist read in the well, I have read there too. He had it right.</p>\n<p>The purifier's night under the Ford-Bell I envy her. She wrote that something round the ring-stones is drawing in. I have laid my ear to three of them since. I keep what I heard.</p>\n<p>The folding song came from a debt-copyist who wintered in the reed-camps, with the doll he made out of his own reckoning. I keep that doll face down. Its arms are longer than they were.</p>\n<p>The salt-carter told his story at Bellrest Hearth. I have seen the wind of faces go up past the Cloud-Shelf twice. I knew nobody in it. One of them knew me.</p>\n<p>Tae's account the House wanted left out. I put it in. The spring after she spoke, her lamp stood on the bank. The spring after that I found neither.</p>\n<p>Last, the doll scratched on both faces and round the edge. The House refused it a place indoors. The point goes in deepest where she writes that she is the last one at the stair. The boatman still counts seven rings, some nights.</p>\n<p>Now breathe out. You have been holding it.</p>", "relics": "<div class=\"plates\"><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Paper Doll</h4><p>A doll of pressed reed paper, arms out, of the kind the House leaves on doorsteps in the lowland camps. I found it on the step of a hut where I sheltered, the week the Fen came clear again. I stopped. No one came at dusk.</p><p>I unfolded it once, at the shoulder only. The inside of the paper was grey, as though something had been breathed into it and kept. I folded it back along its own creases and have not opened it since.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Guardian Stone, Turned</h4><p>A shrine stone the size of a loaf, worn smooth on one face, from a crossing on the Barrow road where there is no shrine any more. I carried it off because nobody else would put it right.</p><p>Each night I set it by the fire facing me. Most mornings it faces the dark. I have laid my ear to the smooth face. I turned it back quickly, and I have not laid my ear there again.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Purifier's Fan</h4><p>A folding fan of oiled paper on ribs of bone, patched many times, some of the patches older than the fan. A novice's family gave it to me when her time came. They did not want it in the house.</p><p>I opened it once on the Fen road, a rib at a time, and the reeds on that side all leaned away from me. Never open it toward a lamp.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Bent Twig</h4><p>A green twig bent double and tied, of the kind pilgrims leave on a post to find the way back. It lay at the foot of the Well-Shrine gate the morning the guardian stone was found facing the wall. A keeper gave it to me years later, saying she could not bring herself to burn it.</p><p>It is still green. However I lay it, on a table, in a bag, on a barn floor, it points to the Nave. Past the gate of the Descent it pointed down.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Knot-Cord</h4><p>A cord of plaited white paper, knotted along its whole length, long enough to coil on the floor. It belonged to the first keeper whose hand is in the day-book. She went to the water, and the cord stayed dry on the step.</p><p>She could not have tied so many knots in three lives. I have counted them twice and got two numbers, and I have stopped. Some mornings the coil is bigger.</p></div></div>", "relics_intro": "What the House gave me, and what it did not. Carry them lightly; each is heavier than it looks.", "works": [{"title": "The First Folding", "by": "Instruction set down by an elder purifier of the House of Eight Million for a novice on the night before her first rite. Found pressed flat inside a paper doll that was never used, at a wayside shrine on the Fen road.", "html": "<p>Fold it by lamplight, child, never at noon. Noon has no corners in it.</p>\n<p>Take the white sheet. Crease it once down the middle: that is the spine. Fold the head down, the arms out, the legs apart. Leave the arms open. A doll with its arms closed has already refused, and a refused doll you burn without watching the smoke. Write nothing on it. The one who comes to you will name it with their breath, and your hand would only argue.</p>\n<p>Sit across from them, knee to knee. Hold the doll to their mouth and ask them to breathe on it three times. Watch the paper. On the third breath it leans toward them a little, as if it were called. That is the distortion leaving by the door it came in. Then lift the doll to your own lips and breathe it in.</p>\n<p>Hold it. Count on the knot-cord at your wrist, one knot for each breath held. It sits in the chest like cold water, then like warm, then you stop feeling it, and that is the part to fear. When you stop feeling it, it has found a place in you, and a place once found is kept.</p>\n<p>Now the fan. Open it with the thumb, one rib at a time. Every rib is a road. Breathe out slow across the paper and turn the fan toward whoever made the distortion: a man who moved a boundary stone, a woman who cursed her neighbour's well, a house of the Maiden where flesh is grown without a word. The fan tells the breath where to go. Your breath only knows how to leave. Never breathe out with the fan shut. I did, once. The breath went where it liked. It liked the shrine.</p>\n<p>The small gods you will meet on every road: the stone at the crossing, the spirit in the ford, the spirit in the bell. Eight million, the House says, and the House stopped counting long ago. Bow to each as you pass. Never ask a name. They gave their names to the wind, one each, and they are proud of it.</p>\n<p>Where a shrine is forgotten, its guardian stone turns to the wall. A finger's width a year, and no hand moves it. When you find one, kneel, set both palms on it, and turn it back to face the road. Breathe on its face until the dust there stirs. The air will clear. We teach that the stone turns away in grief at being forgotten. I have turned many. Lately I think they turn to listen. Some day, when I am past scolding you, put your ear to the back of one. Then turn it back quickly.</p>\n<p>The ring-stones on the pilgrim roads, the fang-shaped ones round a pit of ash, pass those by. They face inward already. No one of the House has ever turned them, and no one of the House will tell you why. I asked my own elder once. She breathed out, very long, and went to bed.</p>\n<p>Now the thing the elders tell last, on the night before.</p>\n<p>You are running out of room. Every held breath leaves a little of itself behind, the way a lantern keeps a little of whoever it carries home. You will breathe out and breathe out, and the fan will send it all away, and still the cord at your wrist grows longer than the knots you remember tying. Mine reaches the floor. I tied perhaps half of it.</p>\n<p>Every paper doll ends as an oni. We say <i>ends</i>. We say it gently. The face goes long and hard. The horns come in slow, like a child's teeth. One morning the doll who has held too much stands up from the shrine step with her hands full of everything she took, and she walks down. Always down: into the water, into the Root Deep, where the deep ground under the Hide breathes. She carries it there so the rest of us need not.</p>\n<p>Seven went down into the Drowned Nave the spring it gave everything back. Six of my year, and a girl younger than you. I lay sick with fever that week and stayed on the step. I sat by the torii and watched the water come up grey, and I have been grateful and ashamed ever since, one and then the other, like breathing.</p>\n<p>They say the girl came up. If a young woman in faded vermilion ever stops at this shrine, give her water. Do not look long at the one beside her.</p>\n<p>You will know your own time by the stones. They will begin to turn toward you.</p>\n<p>Fold the doll now. Leave the arms open. Then sleep, child. Breathe slowly. Leave the cord alone tonight.</p>"}, {"title": "What the Pole Knows", "by": "A boatman of the Drowned Fen, talking over three crossings to the Well-Shrine. The pilgrim who wrote them left the page folded under a guardian stone at the torii.", "html": "<p>Sit low. Keep your hands in the boat. This is the god's own clear water, and it drinks. Trail your fingers in it and they come back dry, and a little lighter. We say: the Fen takes the wet first, then the rest.</p>\n<p>You pay at the Iron Hook. Before the Hook the Fen may still want you back, and a man should keep his coin until he knows.</p>\n<p>The House of Eight Million keeps the Well-Shrine. We call them Fan-Bearers. In the reed-camps you will see their paper dolls on the doorsteps, a folded figure with its arms held out. Find one on your step, and you stop. A Fan-Bearer comes at dusk and breathes in the air of your house, and after, the house is easier to sleep in. My mother found one on our step the winter my father went quiet. She stopped. We say: the doll asks once.</p>\n<p>Every spring the purifiers came down to the water's edge and breathed the year out into the Fen. All of it. They trusted the water to carry it off. So did we. Water goes somewhere.</p>\n<p>Look down. There, past the reeds, where the pole strikes stone. That is the roof of the Drowned Nave. A whole house of prayer under us: bell, pew, altar, stair. My pole knows its roof. Some crossings the roof is a hand deeper than I left it. Some crossings it is a hand nearer. I keep a notch on the pole. I have cut the notch three times.</p>\n<p>All those springs of breath sank there and filled the Nave the way a jar fills. Then one spring the jar was full.</p>\n<p>I was a young man that year. Or it was my grandfather, and I have heard it told so often it has moved into me. The water came up grey. The reeds leaned away from the shrine all at once, like people from a sickbed. In the morning the guardian stone at the torii was facing the wall, and no one had touched it. Every bucket drawn from the Fen that month held faces, and the faces were turned the wrong way round, and then turned the wrong way round again, so they looked almost right. Almost.</p>\n<p>The House sent seven down into the Nave to take it back in. Six came up as far as the Kneeling Arch. I saw them from this boat, or I say I did. They stood in the shallows with their hands full of something heavy, and their faces had gone long and hard, and there were horns on them. Then they turned and walked down into the deep water the way a man walks down a stair. Oni, the House calls them now. Out past the Reed That Points Home, on still nights, breath comes up through the water in slow rings. I have counted six. Some nights I count seven.</p>\n<p>The seventh was the girl with the fan. Shiori. She was left at the torii as a babe, and the purifiers named her for the bent twig a pilgrim leaves to find the way back. Her sister Akane was two years older and took her fevers for her. They say Akane breathed in a hundred years at once, down there, and Shiori held her face and breathed it all back out of her, and the water-glass on the drowned altar stood still for as long as she held. I only know what came up. One girl. And a haze around her the colour of a bruise.</p>\n<p>She crossed in my boat once. She paid nothing and I asked nothing. Her breath went slower with every stroke of the pole, the way a full jar pours slow. There was someone in the haze beside her. Akane was right-handed. The one in the haze holds her fan in the left.</p>\n<p>Then there are the knights. The Pale Order comes down from the white mountains in plate of carved bone, with hooks and chain, to dredge the drowned. We give our dead to the water. The Order says a stray bone hurts the world, and hauls ours up and stands them in the Nave, facing the stair. What they stood up came up bent. The House says it was the cause. The knights say nothing.</p>\n<p>They still come. They pay well and ask no questions, and a boat has no side. But I count what they carry up, and I know what my own village gave the water. The Fen gives back more than it was given.</p>\n<p>There. The torii. Step out on the left, and do not look at the stone until you are past it.</p>"}, {"title": "The Register of Misogi-ido", "by": "Leaves from the day-book kept at the torii of the Well-Shrine, written in several hands over many years. The House counts its years by the Cutting-Away. A pilgrim was let copy these pages at the step; where a leaf had been cut out, or a hand changed, the copyist marked it in brackets.", "html": "<p><b>Cutting 190. Frost, the fourth.</b> Foundling at the torii before light. Girl, some weeks old, wrapped in white paper, no name pinned. She took the rice-water. Named Akane for the colour of the paper's edge. <i>(Hand of Keeper Tomoe.)</i></p>\n<p><b>Cutting 192. Thaw, the ninth.</b> Foundling at the torii. Girl, very small, blue about the lips. A bent twig tied to the post above her, the kind a pilgrim leaves to find the road back. Named for it. Shiori.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 192. Thaw, the twelfth.</b> Shiori has fever. Akane will not leave the mat.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 192. Thaw, the fifteenth.</b> Shiori well. Akane coughs.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 193. The Great Cutting-Away.</b> The year breathed into the Fen. Water clear by noon.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 194. The Great Cutting-Away.</b> The year breathed into the Fen. Water clear by noon.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 195. The Great Cutting-Away.</b> The year breathed into the Fen. Water clear by dusk.</p>\n<p><i>(Here the hand changes. Firmer, smaller.)</i></p>\n<p><b>Cutting 198. Heat, the second.</b> Tomoe given to the water. Her cord reached the floor. I keep the book now. Kasane, of the salt.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 198. The Great Cutting-Away.</b> The year breathed into the Fen. Water clear.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 199. Frost, the twentieth.</b> Guardian stone at the torii a finger's width off the road. Turned back. Dust breathed off its face.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 200. The Great Cutting-Away.</b> Water clear the third morning.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 200. Thaw, the sixth.</b> The boatman cut a new notch in his pole. He says the Nave roof was a hand nearer.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 202. Frost, the ninth.</b> Stone a hand's width off. Turned back. Hisa says we should stop writing it down. Written.</p>\n<p><i>(One leaf cut out here. The stub shows the edge of a drawing: a ring.)</i></p>\n<p><b>Cutting 204. Thaw, the first.</b> Novice Nae drew a bucket. Her face in it reversed. Drew again. Reversed again, and looked right. Bucket poured on the salt. Salt changed.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 204. Thaw, the second.</b> Every bucket.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 204. Thaw, the third.</b> Water grey. Reeds leaning away from the shrine, all of them, no wind. Guardian stone at the torii facing the wall. No hand. It will not turn back. We tried with four.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 204. Thaw, the third, night.</b> Seven to go down at first light. Hisa. Chiyo. Nae. Sen. Rin. Kasane, this hand. Shiori, who asked. The elder of our year lies with fever and stays on the step to keep the book.</p>\n<p>Stair into the Nave counted from the boat: thirty-three.</p>\n<p><i>(The hand changes. It shakes, then steadies.)</i></p>\n<p><b>Cutting 204. Thaw, the fourth.</b> They went down counting aloud. I heard thirty-three. I heard thirty-four.</p>\n<p>Akane's mat empty this morning. Akane was not sent. Akane is not written above.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 204. Thaw, the fifth.</b> Six stood at the Kneeling Arch at noon with their hands full. I saw them from the step. They stood a long while. Then they turned round.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 204. Thaw, the fifth, dusk.</b> One came up. Akane was carried.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 204. Thaw, the sixth.</b> Water clear. Reeds upright. Stone at the torii facing the road. No hand.</p>\n<p>Akane laid on the step. Dry, though she came out of the water. The one sat by her while the salt was changed twice. What was done with Akane is not written here.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 204. Thaw, the ninth.</b> No Cutting-Away this year.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 206.</b> No Cutting-Away. Where does the year go now. It goes into us.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 206. Heat, the fourth.</b> The one signed for her salt ration. Left hand. She was taught right. I taught her.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 207. Frost, the second.</b> Knights of the Pale Order at the Iron Hook. Paid the boatman. Went down with hooks and chain. Came up by dark with the drowned of the Fen. Four hundred and some were given to the water, in all the years of the book. I counted what they carried past the torii. I stopped at five hundred.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 207. Frost, the third.</b> Boatman counts six rings of breath past the Reed That Points Home. He asked me to write seven. Written.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 208. Thaw, the first.</b> The one left at dusk with her fan. Tied a bent twig on the post. Salt ration not taken. There was someone beside her in the haze. I did not look long.</p>\n<p><i>(Many leaves blank. Then the same hand, older.)</i></p>\n<p><b>Cutting 231. Frost, the ninth.</b> Stone at the torii a finger's width off. Toward the step.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 232. Frost, the ninth.</b> A hand's width. Toward the step. Toward where I sit.</p>\n<p><b>Cutting 233.</b> Cord reaches the floor.</p>\n<p><i>(The last entry is in a hand the copyist did not know. The strokes run backward and he read them in the well.)</i></p>\n<p><b>Cutting 204. Thaw, the fourth.</b> Thirty-six. Salt changed. Right hand.</p>"}, {"title": "What the Ford-Bell Said", "by": "Set down by a purifier of the House of Eight Million, who sat one whole night beside the bell that hangs over the Salt Ford and wrote between its strokes. The bell has no rope. The copyist of the Codex left her gaps and her marks as she made them.", "html": "<p>[It began at dusk. The wind moved the bell once. After that it moved by itself, or the words came when it was still. I could never tell which.]</p>\n<p>Stroke. Stroke. You are the paper one. You smell of held breath.</p>\n<p>I ride. Everything rides. The great one breathed in once, long, when the space was held open, and all of us were inside that breath, warm, one sound. Then the naming stopped. Then the breath began to go out. We are going out with it. We have been going out for — [a word like a long hum; I wrote <i>one stroke</i>, and then <i>many</i>, and could not choose].</p>\n<p>A bell is a mouth for the wind. The bronze is my house. I am what leaves the house when it is struck, and comes back, and leaves. I have left and come back more times than there are stones in the ford. Each time I come back a little less. That is how I know the breath is still going out.</p>\n<p>You want names. You have the look. I had a name. I gave it to the wind the morning the great one went still, the way all of us did, one each, and the wind took it out ahead of us. Some nights I hear it, very small, far ahead, and ring toward it.</p>\n<p>[Here a long silence. The ford ran loud. When it spoke again it spoke of its neighbours.]</p>\n<p>The water under me is a small god also. She is older and says less. She carries whatever you give her and she is tired of carrying it. Your House gave her a year's wrong breath, every year, and she carried it down, down, to the drowned house under the Fen, because down was the only way she knew. She is sorry. She says to tell you she is sorry.</p>\n<p>The stone at the crossing is a small god. He was a tooth once. He remembers biting.</p>\n<p>The guardian stones of your shrines are small gods. You teach they turn to the wall for grief. Paper one, they turn toward the wall to hear through it. There is a sound behind every wall of the Hide now. They are listening to learn which way it goes.</p>\n<p>Wrong breath. You call it miasma. It is our own breath that turned round in a narrow place and forgot the door. Breath that cannot leave goes thick. It sits. It sickens what it sits on, the way a word sickens when you keep it in your mouth too long. You paper ones come and drink it. You hold it where your own breath lives. Then you send it out again with the fan, and it remembers the door, and it goes on. We are grateful, and a little afraid. What you drink is us.</p>\n<p>Some of you drink too much and walk down into the water with it. I have rung for seven. Then for six. Then I lost the count. The water says the one who did not walk down came up with two breaths in one body, and one of them facing backward.</p>\n<p>[I asked it where the breath is going. The bell swung hard and I thought it would speak no more that night.]</p>\n<p>Out.</p>\n<p>Out is where it goes. Out past the high cold where the flames barely move. Out toward the white points in the dark that the bone-counters wait for. The bone-counters want the points to fall. We want to reach them. We cannot both have it.</p>\n<p>But, paper one.</p>\n<p>Out is closer than it was. I ring, and the ring comes back from the edge sooner than before. The edge is nearer. Or I am smaller. It is the same thing from inside a bell.</p>\n<p>And the ring-stones. The teeth round the ash. Go close to one and hold your own breath. Under the ash something is drawing in. Slow. Very slow. The great one only ever breathed out after the stopping.</p>\n<p>Something under the ash is breathing in.</p>\n<p>[It said this twice, the second time much quieter. Then nothing until the ford went grey.]</p>\n<p>Stroke. The light comes. Bow as you go. Do not ask the water her name. Tie a twig to the post for me, bent double, so I know you found the way back. I will ring when you are far enough off that you cannot hear it.</p>\n<p>[I left the twig. On the road home, at the hour it had said, I felt my chest go light, as if I had breathed out a long while. I had been holding my breath since the ford.]</p>"}, {"title": "The Folding Song of the Reed-Camps", "by": "Gathered in the reed-camps of the Drowned Fen by a copyist who came down to write a debt-roll and stayed a winter. The verses as the women sang them over their work; the notes between are his. Sent to the Codex from the Well-Shrine, folded small.", "html": "<p>The women of the reed-camps fold the House's dolls by lamplight, six or eight to a mat, one verse to each fold. The song keeps the hands in order, they told me.</p>\n<blockquote><i>Cut the reed at low water,</i><br><i>lay it under stone.</i><br><i>The Fen will take the wet away</i><br><i>and leave the rest alone.</i></blockquote>\n<p>The paper is pressed reed. They cut it in the shallows past the Iron Hook, split it, lay it crosswise in two courses, and weight it with flat stones <i>under the water</i>, a month of nights. Fen water leaves a thing dry, so the sheet comes up dry, and white, and smelling of nothing at all. A sheet that smells of anything they burn.</p>\n<blockquote><i>Down the middle, one crease,</i><br><i>that's the bone to stand her.</i><br><i>Fold it once and fold it true</i><br><i>and no one fold it after.</i></blockquote>\n<p>The spine is the only bone the doll gets. An old folder called Tsuru told me it is plenty, for what she carries. A spine creased twice goes in the fire.</p>\n<blockquote><i>Head down, little head,</i><br><i>bow before the door.</i><br><i>Whoever comes to find you</i><br><i>will stop, and wonder what for.</i></blockquote>\n<p>This is what the doll is for. The House leaves it on a doorstep and it asks the house to stop. Stop what, I asked. Whatever you were doing, said Tsuru. A man shifting a boundary stone. Most stop. The doll asks once. A Fan-Bearer comes at dusk either way.</p>\n<p>I found one on my own step my third week. I had been adding the camp's debts. I stopped. The roll is still unfinished.</p>\n<blockquote><i>Arms out, arms out,</i><br><i>open as a well.</i><br><i>What comes into open arms</i><br><i>the arms will never tell.</i></blockquote>\n<p>The arms are left open always; a doll with closed arms has refused, and is burned without watching. The House sends a pattern-doll each spring for the folders to copy. Tsuru keeps the old ones in a box; I laid them out by year. Every spring the arms are a little longer. Her grandmother's dolls hold their arms out about as far as a child does. This year's could hold a door.</p>\n<blockquote><i>Legs apart for walking,</i><br><i>turn the hem up high.</i><br><i>The road she goes is under water</i><br><i>and she must keep dry.</i></blockquote>\n<p>A doll lying on a step walks nowhere, so I asked about the legs. <i>For later</i>, they said, all of them, as a thing everybody knows. The children set dolls on the water to watch them float. They float high and dry, and they drift west against the current, toward the pool where the one reed stands. The mothers call the children in early.</p>\n<p>Two verses more go on the same tune. I heard them only from the children, and from the old women when they forgot I was there.</p>\n<blockquote><i>Who folded the folder?</i><br><i>Who creased her down the spine?</i><br><i>She held her arms out all her life</i><br><i>and now they're full of mine.</i></blockquote>\n<blockquote><i>Down the thirty-three steps,</i><br><i>down the one step more.</i><br><i>The doll who held the whole Fen's breath</i><br><i>is knocking at the floor.</i></blockquote>\n<p>The spring after the Nave gave everything back, a purifier in grey came to each camp and asked the women, kindly, to stop singing these two. She gave no reason. She sat and folded with them a whole evening, and her dolls were perfect, and she did not sing at all.</p>\n<p>They stopped the words. They kept the tune. When the fold for those verses comes round, the women hum, and the hands go on. I have watched a whole mat hum through it without once looking up.</p>\n<p>I cannot make the verse agree with itself. Tsuru said her mother sang it. Her mother was dead before the Nave rose. She said songs come when they come.</p>\n<p>The children sing <i>thirty-four</i>, and their mothers correct them. On my last evening, at the western pool, I heard a small girl sing <i>thirty-six</i>. Nobody corrected her. There was nobody near her to correct her.</p>\n<p>They close each bundle with a verse they sing plainly, and let me write:</p>\n<blockquote><i>Lie still, little sister,</i><br><i>lie still upon the step.</i><br><i>The House will come at dusk for you,</i><br><i>and we will keep the rest.</i></blockquote>\n<p>The folders never breathe on a doll. Breath is the House's business. They hold the paper away from their mouths as they fold, and turn their faces aside to speak.</p>\n<p>On the road north I found I had folded the last leaf of my debt-roll into a doll. Arms out. I do not remember doing it. I have kept my face turned aside from it, and I send it on to the Well-Shrine with these pages.</p>"}, {"title": "Four in the Traces", "by": "Told by a salt-carter of the Heights to a Fan-Bearer come up to Bellrest Hearth to read the breath of travellers. The purifier set it down that night, and left the carter a paper doll, unused.", "html": "<p>You want to know why I breathe like this. Everyone at the Hearth wants to know. Sit, then. It takes me a while.</p>\n<p>We pulled salt. One cart, from the white pans above the Cloud-Shelf, down through the Glass Pass to the Hearth. Four in the traces going down, because salt is heavy and the road is steep, and a cart that gets away from you goes over the edge and takes the traces with it. Coming up empty we were two, me and Ivo. Ivo and I pulled together eleven years. I knew his breathing better than my own. On the climb you breathe together or you fall out of step, and the strap cuts you.</p>\n<p>Up there the air is thin. Hear it from me: you breathe and nothing comes. You breathe again and a little comes. The lanterns on the spires burn tall and still, like flames painted on a wall, and the frost on the glass is a hand, every time, the fingers spread. The old carters say never wipe the hand off. We never did.</p>\n<p>The morning it came we were on the Wind-Scoured Ridges, past the last spire. The chimes had stopped. When the chimes stop you pull hard for the next lantern. We pulled. Then the lantern ahead of us leaned. Only a little. The first flame I had seen move in three days, and it leaned toward us, down the road, the way a candle leans toward an open door.</p>\n<p>Then it came up the road from below. Wind comes down off the peaks; this came up from the Hide, slow, the width of the road and taller than the spire, and it was faces. Grey faces, thin as breath on cold glass, pressed close as a crowd at a well. Old men. Children. Faces with too many eyes, or none, and faces so plain they could have been anyone from my village. All of them with the mouth a little open, the way a mouth sits when the last of the air goes out.</p>\n<p>The cold came with it, past any cold I know. My lashes froze together. The salt dust on my sleeves went hard as sand.</p>\n<p>The faces spoke. Each one said a single word as it passed, and then it was gone behind us, up toward the top, and the next came. I heard <i>mother</i>. I heard <i>enough</i>. I heard a name I think was a place. I heard a word I have never heard again and could not say for you if you paid me in white salt. Most said only the sound a man makes at the end of a long day's pull, when he sets the traces down. <i>Hahh.</i> Like that. Hundreds of them. Thousands. I did not count. I am no bone-brother.</p>\n<p>Ivo stopped pulling. I felt it in the harness before I saw it. He said, It's lighter. I said the cart is full, what is lighter. He said, Everything. Listen to them, Ren. Nobody in there is carrying anything.</p>\n<p>Then he slipped the traces. Eleven years, and I watched him lift the strap over his head as easy as a man takes off his hat at a door. He walked up into the faces and they parted round him the way a crowd parts for a bier. I held on to the cart. It was the only thing heavy enough to hold me. I am not proud of it. I would do it again.</p>\n<p>When it had gone past, the lantern stood straight. His footprints went twelve steps into the frost and stopped. His coat lay on the road, folded. Ivo never folded anything in his life.</p>\n<p>I brought the salt down alone. Four days. I should have died on the Pass. I did not, and I have thought a long time about why.</p>\n<p>The next year, crossing up with a new partner who does not talk, I saw it again, far off along the Cloud-Shelf, going up. And I knew one face near the front, with its mouth a little open. He said nothing. He had already said it.</p>\n<p>So. I let no breath go quickly now. I hold each one a moment and let it out slow, through the teeth, a little at a time. Your sisters tell me the small gods of every stone and bell ride the breath, and the whole world has been breathing out one long breath since before the Hearth was built. Maybe. I only know something goes out with each one. I heard them go. I would keep mine a little longer before they join the rest.</p>\n<p>One more thing, since you are writing. The frost hand on my lantern. Every morning since, I find it on the inside of the glass.</p>"}, {"title": "The Count of His Breathing", "by": "Spoken by Tae, a reed-cutter of the Fen camps, whose husband was one of the six. A pilgrim who sheltered a night in her reed-house wrote it down. The House wished it left out of the Codex. It was put in.", "html": "<p>You want to know about the oni. Sit on that side, where the lamp can see your face. Mine is the man.</p>\n<p>His name was Jinpei, and he was the loudest man in the reed-camps. He sang cutting reeds and he sang poling home, badly, and when he slept the thatch shook. My mother said that one would never need a doll on his step; he breathed everything out as fast as it came in. She was wrong about that.</p>\n<p>The House took him the year we married. They came up from the Well-Shrine with a fan and said he had room in him. They said it as a gift, and he took it as one. After that he went down to the torii every dusk and came home at dark smelling of salt and paper, and he stopped singing.</p>\n<p>I learned to count him. Every wife of the House learns. You lie in the dark with your hand flat on his back and count his breaths against your own. The first spring he breathed four to my five. Then three. By the tenth spring I would count twenty of mine and wait, and wait, and he would draw one breath so long I could have filled a bowl in it. His back stayed warm under my hand, like a hearthstone after the fire has gone out. His jaw came long. His old hat stopped fitting. I plaited him a new one and said nothing, and so did he, and that was the worst thing we did to each other.</p>\n<p>The spring the water came up grey, he went down with the others. He kissed me at the door the way you kiss someone on a boat already pushing off. I said the rite to him, the words about sending the breath home to the one who fouled it. I meant the knights of the Pale Order, who hauled our dead out of the Nave and stood them facing the stair until they came up bent. I meant the House, who breathed a hundred springs into the water and called it clean. I meant the water.</p>\n<p>He got to the Kneeling Arch. I stood on the bank and saw him. His hands were full. He looked a long while at me. Then he turned round and walked down into the Fen like a man going down to his own cellar.</p>\n<p>After, the House left a paper doll on my step. I burned it, and I watched the smoke.</p>\n<p>Now. What you came for.</p>\n<p>Some nights, when the Fen is still, he comes up. He comes up dry. He stands on the bank just where my lamp gives out, and no nearer. His face is long and hard, and the horns came in slow, the way a child's teeth come. He holds his hands shut against his chest, and whatever is in them is heavy, because he leans.</p>\n<p>The camp says bar the door. I take the lamp out to the step, and I count him.</p>\n<p>One breath a night. He draws it when I light the lamp and lets it go before first light. His feet are grey to the ankle with ash. Dry ash, and he has been under water. I asked him once where there is ash beneath the Fen. He turned his head toward the Nave, then lower than the Nave, and I stopped asking.</p>\n<p>He keeps out of the light. I have thought about why. A soul burns in every lamp, someone who chose to stay, and I think what he carries would like very much to sit beside a fire. So he stands in the dark and keeps it off mine. That is who he was. He always took the heavy end of the boat.</p>\n<p>I am angry at him. Write that down. At the House, at the knights, at the water, and at him for turning round at the Arch. I told him so on the bank, loud, the way he used to sing. He stood and took it. He has room.</p>\n<p>Last winter I walked out past the lamp. The camp cried out behind me. I set my hand flat on his chest, where I used to count. It was warm, the way the road down must be warm. Under my palm something turned over, slowly, as a sleeper turns.</p>\n<p>He opened one hand a little. I will keep what was in it.</p>\n<p>Every spring I set my lamp a pace nearer the water. Next spring it stands on the bank. I have been practising. Twenty breaths, and hold. The House says a woman must be folded for it. I say a woman need only be his wife.</p>\n<p>Tell them that at the torii. Tell them the doll asks once. So do I.</p>"}, {"title": "What the Sixth Doll Carried", "by": "Scratched in a hard point on both faces of a paper doll found floating, dry, past the Reed That Points Home. A boatman brought it to the Well-Shrine. The House would not keep it under its roof, and a pilgrim copied it out at the torii.", "html": "<p><b>Front, along the arms and down the body</b></p>\n<p>I fold this from the last paper in my sleeve. The Fen gives back dry whatever it gives back, so this may reach the reeds.</p>\n<p>I was Kasane of the lower house. I kept the salt at the torii and taught the small ones to fold. I set it down now, while it is still the right way round.</p>\n<p>We went down seven. The stair into the Nave has thirty-three steps. We counted them going down, all of us aloud, as the rite asks. At the bottom Hisa counted again and said thirty-four, and we laughed at her, there, in that place. I am glad we laughed.</p>\n<p>What we carried out, plain. The drowned of the Fen, first: the dead we gave the water with a breath still in them, which the knights of the Pale Order hauled up and stood facing the stair. The breath had gone sour in the standing and bent the bones around it. We drew it out of them, one by one, and they sat down. Then a hundred springs of the House's own leavings, every year we breathed into the water and called the water clean. Then something at the bottom of the jar that had no face and asked for nothing, and weighed more than the rest together.</p>\n<p>A breath is light. Ten thousand breaths are light. That last one was heavy the way a sleeping child is heavy. I hold it behind my teeth. My jaw has lengthened to hold it. When I lean forward it pulls, gently, the way a hand on the back of your head bends you to the bowl.</p>\n<p>At the Kneeling Arch the six of us stood in the shallows with our hands full. The rite says: breathe it back at whoever made it. We stood a long while, working that out. Then we turned round.</p>\n<p><b>Back of the doll</b></p>\n<p>Shiori. You were the small one who carried the salt bowl in both hands and spilled it every time. Hear me.</p>\n<p>Keep a little room. Breathe out at every dusk, even when there is nothing to breathe out. A full jar pours slow. I have watched you from the water and you pour slow.</p>\n<p>Turn the stones. All of them. Some of us turned our backs on the torii, down here, and I think a stone takes its lesson from the one who keeps it.</p>\n<p>Do not come down the stair to fetch us. The stair is longer now.</p>\n<p>Akane ate her rice with her right hand. Look at which hand. Then love it anyway. I would.</p>\n<p>Of the Nave, since no one else will tell you. The water-glass on the altar stands where you stopped it. None of us has turned it. Under it the pews are full. The Fen gave the water four hundred and some; I counted the pews twice and they hold more, sitting quiet, the ones we unbent and ones I never breathed on. The knights' dead faced the stair. On the nights I go back they face the altar. The bell rings with no hand on the rope, and the sound goes up through the water and comes out, I think, as the rings the boatmen count.</p>\n<p>Behind the altar the floor has broken in a ring. The stones lean in over it like teeth. There is ash down there, dry, under all that water, and it rises and settles. Something breathes under it, slow, slower than me. The others went past it. There is a door there now and a road going down, and the air on the road is warm. Hisa went first. She counted as she went.</p>\n<p>I am the last at the stair.</p>\n<p><b>Round the edge, smaller, the point pressed deeper</b></p>\n<p>My name was. Kasane. Kasane of the salt. The paper is small and the hand is large now. It wants out and I hold it, where would it go, it would go to you. We are room. The room is warm below. Thirty-six. The stair counts itself. The doll asks once, so stop, stop at the doll, you that finds it. Fold the corners tight. Turn the stone. Right hand. Right hand. We are the right way round again, nearly. Almost.</p>"}]}, {"id": "before", "name": "Before the Last Breath", "sub": "voices from the Held Space, and what I will not add to them", "epi": "A miner at the Moor workings, asked what was under the east drift: \"Manners.\"", "preface": "<p>I have put this chapter off for four winters. The others I wrote at whatever fire I happened to be sitting at. This one has ridden in the bag unwritten, beside the things that close it, and the bag has weighed more than its contents the whole while. Tonight I am at the Ashwake fire, and three pilgrims are asleep round it. Their breathing is uneven, one and then another and then the third, never together. That is why I can write tonight. By the end you will know why that matters.</p>\n<p>Lower the wick. Lower again.</p>\n<h3>What I said in the first chapter</h3>\n<p>In the first chapter I wrote that in the oldest age nobody was there. I wrote it the way you call a room empty from its doorway, with your lamp held in and your feet still outside. I have stood in a room since where it was not so. I will come to it last.</p>\n<p>I also wrote that the congregation knelt inside the god near the end. Near the end is the important part. The Tithed remember the kneeling and nothing earlier. A grandmother keeps three notes. On Kneel-Night the camps on the Cheek kneel from dark to grey round a banked fire, and nobody can tell you why the fire must be banked. That is everything the congregation kept, and they came late.</p>\n<p>I knelt with them one Kneel-Night, at a camp below the Jaw, between a salt-boiler's widow and a boy who kept falling asleep against my arm. Near the grey hour the widow leaned across and asked me, quite kindly, whether I would like to be the one to bank the fire, since I knew how. I had not touched the fire. I had not said I knew anything. I banked it. My hands did it the old way, the ash drawn over from the far side first, and three of the old women stopped their kneeling to watch, and none of them would speak to me in the morning.</p>\n<p>Others had been in the Held Space a long while before them, and those others had work.</p>\n<h3>The ones who were already there</h3>\n<p>I will not describe them. Five of them speak in this chapter, through five copyists, and every copyist has done what I would not dare, which is set their words down straight. I will tell you only where I have been near them, and what I did.</p>\n<p>I have walked the Lower Stacks at night and heard, from the knee-high stairs, a sound like somebody very large lowering itself onto a bench, slowly, over about the time it takes to say a short prayer. I did not go to see who.</p>\n<p>I have stood on a flagstone of the Rib Crypts while it lifted under my boots, and heard what comes through when it lifts. I wrote in the first chapter what I heard that night. I did not write that it sounded like the beginning of something I already knew, and that I knew the next part, and that I shut my mouth on it with my own hand.</p>\n<p>I have been down into the warm under the Hide and spoken with what is there, and come up in the order I went down. I have counted my fingers since, as pilgrims do. They come to what they should. It is the other things I cannot count.</p>\n<p>I have sat by the Descent's gate while a brother of the Vigil climbed up past me with a copyist's pages in his coat, and did not stop, and did not look at me, and went on up toward the light as though he were late.</p>\n<p>They do not agree about much, these voices. They count wrongly, every one, and speak of time as a room they are standing in. They call us small and warm and late. They agree that the space was held open. They agree that something came in. And every one of them, in its own way, says it came in among us.</p>\n<h3>What I will not add</h3>\n<p>There is a thing in two of these works, set down among the kneeling ones and kept, the way a fire is kept overnight. One of the two voices would like it back. Read those lines once and go on. Keep your children's names low near the old drifts and when you cross the rings. The Moor miners say so now, and they are right to.</p>\n<p>Nor will I say who set the fanged stones round their pits. The builders cut a line about it high on their pillar, above where a lamp will reach, and I have put it at the foot of this chapter. Make of it what you can. I have made of it what I could not help.</p>\n<p>I wrote three more pages here. I have cut them out, and the stubs are in the binding, and you will find them with your thumb. This is the chapter where the gaps matter most. Every copyist in it left gaps where the words would not hold, and I have kept each gap to its width. Leave them. A gap is a door a copyist shut. What wants in comes in wherever something is opened again.</p>\n<h3>The room at Greywell</h3>\n<p>I said I would come last to the room where the oldest age was not empty.</p>\n<p>It is the room at Greywell where the lodge used to read. I went there the summer after the page in this chapter was found. The flags along the east wall stay dry in every season, while the rest of the floor sweats. I sat on the floor in the middle of the room, because I would not take any of the chairs, and I kept my back to the east wall. After a while the only thing in the room I could hear was a cloth-covered glass lying on one of the chairs. Keeper Aubren of the Polished Heart teaches that before the Weaver held the room open, the whole world was like that east wall. I believe him. I believe that what stood there was there before the holding, and that the Held Space was held open round it, and that when the holding ended it had only to wait where it stood.</p>\n<p>That is as much of the oldest age as I will write. It is more than I meant to.</p>\n<h3>The copyists</h3>\n<p>Five works follow. Every one came through a copyist, and every copyist is dead, or gone down, or will not go down again.</p>\n<p>Ottelin of the Lower Stacks heard the choir over nine nights. I knew him forty years. He writes that on the last night he knew the next line without having learned it, and did not sing it. I believe him. He was not the first that happened to. His leaves came from the sexton who found him.</p>\n<p>The pillar-rings were copied by the scholar the Order writes as 3,114. He counted the niches round the pillar twice and did not agree with himself. I made a third. At the pillar's foot there is a seat worn smooth, warm, and the only prints in the dust round it are mine. I got up from it quickly.</p>\n<p>The keeper's law came up the Descent in Brother Ansel's pages. They were dry, and warm on one side. I asked at the Vigil's house after Ansel's brother. They agreed he had one. None of them could give his name, and in Ansel's last letter home the place where the name should be is clean.</p>\n<p>The slates are Dunne Hask's. The east drift is a smooth warm face now, and it opens a hand's width to three knocks of iron. I know because I knocked. It thanked me for the last time, and I had not been there before, not by that drift. I asked in the huts after his girl. What they told me stays with me.</p>\n<p>Last, the page from Greywell, off the lodge's own shelves. They gave it to me to be rid of it. Read it by daylight if you can.</p>\n<p>Before you begin them, count the people in the room with you. Count once, and do not count again.</p><div class=\"carve\">WE CUT THEM FACING IN<br>SO THE MOUTH IS KEPT<br>BY WHAT IT HOLDS<span class=\"under\">The highest ring of the builders' pillar, above the reach of the scholar's lamp. He was a short man. I am not.</span></div>", "relics": "<div class=\"plates\"><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Ring-Script Rubbing</h4><p>Charcoal on linen, three sheets, taken from the pillar in the lowest hall by the scholar 3,114, one on each time he went down. The tallier gave them to me after the brothers closed his count, because the Order will not put in a niche a thing that will not give one number.</p><p>Laid one on another against a window, the three sheets agree in every stroke except how many rings there are. I have not taken a fourth. I am not going down to take one.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Listening-Thread</h4><p>A thread of something pale and stiff, as long as a forearm, that stands up by itself. A brother of the Vigil brought it up the Descent and gave it to me at the gate for a place at my fire. He did not want it with him past the gate.</p><p>Laid flat, it rises again. In a quiet room it leans a little toward whoever spoke last, and pays no mind to the lamp. On days when I am alone and have said nothing since morning, it leans toward this book.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Seam-Slate</h4><p>A roof-slate scratched with a miner's letters, the fifth of Dunne Hask's nine. He had only a little writing and spent it carefully.</p><p>The last word on it is cut so deep it goes through, and on the back, in the same stroke, there is a word he did not cut. I have read it. It is short, and polite. I keep the slate written side up.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Bracketed Leaf</h4><p>A leaf from the night-record of the lodge at Greywell, from some weeks before the night in this chapter, an ordinary night of names and candles. There are long white places in it where the ink has gone.</p><p>Nothing will take in those places. Scholars who tried say the pen went dry. One says the pen went on writing, and he has not looked at what. I have not tried. Each spring there are a few more white places than the spring before.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Ottelin's Candle-End</h4><p>The stub of a tallow candle, pinched out, that Ottelin carried up from the Rib Crypts on his last night there and never lit again. The sexton gave it to me with his leaves.</p><p>I lit it once, alone, at a fire in the Barrens. The flame shrank and stood tall and shrank, in turns, as a flame does in a room full of people taking care never to breathe at the same moment. I pinched it out. The wick was cold at once, and my fingers were not burned.</p></div></div>", "relics_intro": "Older than the rest of the bag. They have been four winters in the bag, and they have not kept still.", "works": [{"title": "The Hymn Under the Long Candle", "by": "Heard over nine nights in the Rib Crypts, through the flagstones as they lifted, by Ottelin of the Lower Stacks, sage and copyist; set down in his hand with his gaps kept, and given to the Codex when he died.", "html": "<p><i>(Ottelin: It comes when the stones lift, and stops when they settle. It speaks as many. I have written \"we\" for a word that also means the inside of a throat. Where I have no word I leave the place open.)</i></p>\n<p>We were the part of the god that told it what it was.</p>\n<p>Three, it was. One, it was. We sang the three into the one each [breath / day / age: the word is the same word] and it held. Soul went out along its threads and came home along them. Bone stood round the space the way a hand stands round a candle in a draught. Flesh filled the space and was warm, and we lived in the warm, in the long groove behind the [ribs, or the heart; the word has too many rooms in it].</p>\n<p>The space was open. That was the whole of the work. On every side lay the Hush, pressing in, the way deep water presses on a held breath. The god held the space open, and we held the god in its shape by singing it. Nobody set us to it. We were the singing. When we were made we were already in the middle of a verse.</p>\n<p>We are one. We were eleven hundred and one, and then one again, and then eleven hundred and one. <i>(Ottelin: Every count it gives climbs and comes back to where it began. I tallied it by the Pale Order's way. It came to no root.)</i></p>\n<p>The kneeling ones came late. They came in through the [door, or the wound, or the mouth] and knelt in the warm below us, and we sang over them. They were small and hot, and made of other stuff than the god. We sang over them all the same. They answered badly. They answered with names, each one different, all at once. The god leaned down to hear them. We felt it lean. It set something down among them, small and folded, the way a woman banks a coal in ash to keep it till morning. We were told nothing of what.</p>\n<p><i>(Ottelin: The stones settled here. The second night began elsewhere.)</i></p>\n<p>— the first silence. It was ours.</p>\n<p>Through the whole age we breathed in turns, some while others sang, so that the note was always held. Then, once, all of us drew breath at the same moment. [A long sound here that I will not try to write.] Only a moment. The width of a thread. And in the rest between one verse and the next, the Hush came in.</p>\n<p>The god forgot one of its names. We sang louder. It answered as two and one. We sang again. It answered as one, and one, and one, three voices in three rooms, each asking where the others had gone.</p>\n<p>We sang the rests shut. We sang without breathing. We are singing without breathing still. <i>(Ottelin: I asked whether they blamed themselves. They asked me what \"themselves\" was, and whether it was warm.)</i></p>\n<p>Then the last breath.</p>\n<p>The god drew in. The whole warm lifted under us, the long lift, the one that comes before a great note. We made ready. We knew the verse. We had the verse in every throat. It breathed out.</p>\n<p>It is breathing out.</p>\n<p>We are waiting for it to finish, so we may come in on the next line. We have held the note ready the whole length of the out-breath. <i>(Ottelin: I asked how long. It gave a number. The ink would not take it, and when I tried it in chalk on the flagstone the stone lifted and the chalk was gone.)</i></p>\n<p>Where are the kneeling ones? We hear them walking on us. They do not kneel. They carry little burning souls on poles, and talk over the rests. They tread in the long groove where the warm was. Tell them to come back in. Tell them to kneel down. The space is still a little open wherever they are. It is thin there. Wherever they stand, the Hush has to go round.</p>\n<p>Under the ash in the old places something breathes with its teeth still standing in a ring. That is the [mouth / door / wound] remembering. Step through. Come back in pieces. Kneel on the other side.</p>\n<p><i>(Ottelin: On the ninth night it stopped and waited, as a choir waits for the one voice that is late. I understood that it was waiting on me. I knew the next line. I have never learned it and I knew it, the way I know my own name. I did not sing it. I climbed the stair with my candle out and have not gone down again.</i></p>\n<p><i>The flagstones under my own bed lifted last night. Only a little. Only the width of a thread.)</i></p>"}, {"title": "The Ring of the Lowest Hall", "by": "Carved round a pillar at the bottom of the Ossuary of Nine Stairs; cut in the ring-script of the builders; copied, with notes, by a scholar of the Pale Order written in the book as 3,114, and bound into the Codex after his count was closed.", "html": "<p>The pillar stands in the lowest hall, where the floor slopes toward it. The script runs round it in rings, and has no first line. I began where my lamp first touched. Where a word would not hold, I have set brackets. Where I guessed, I say so. I made three descents.</p>\n<p class=\"orn\">&#10087;</p>\n<p><i>[The ring at the height of my lamp]</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\">She breathes in. We are inside the in-breath. We cut.<br>She breathes out. We count what the out-breath carried.<br>Less. Always less. Good.</p>\n<p>We are the [hinges. The sign is two bones meeting at a joint. Brother Tallier reads it <i>the ones between</i>.]</p>\n<p>Count with us. One. The joint. One. [The same joint again, from the other side.] Two, which is one. [Here the count folds back on itself. I cannot make it walk forward.]</p>\n<p><i>[The ring below]</i></p>\n<p>The room she holds is the width of her holding. Outside the room is [the sign is a mouth cut with no line for the lips]. We do not name it. It hears names.</p>\n<p>We cut the stairs to the height of our [knee. Their knee has four bends in the sign.] We cut the long niches. We lie down in them when our joints are all counted. We lie down facing the stair. We watch for [the gloss fails. The sign is an open ring.]</p>\n<p><i>[The ring below]</i></p>\n<p>The small warm ones came in at the [singing, or the wound; the two signs are one sign]. They sang at her. She listened. She listened [toward them, or too far]. While she listened she forgot to [the sign is a closed hand].</p>\n<p class=\"verse\">That is the task. Hold her small. Count her. A counted thing keeps its count.<br>[I am uncertain of <i>small</i>. The sign may mean <i>here</i>.]</p>\n<p><i>[The ring below]</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\">When she stops breathing in, the out-breath will go on.<br>When the out-breath goes on, the count goes on.<br>Cut. Count. Lie down. Watch.<br>Our joints do not finish.</p>\n<p><i>[The lowest ring, at the floor, worn smooth on one side by something that sat against it]</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\">You who climb our stairs on your hands.<br>You who count by the one that keeps itself.<br>Your [nine, or your number] closes. Ours [opens. Or: keeps opening.]<br>Count with us.<br>One. The joint. One.</p>\n<p class=\"orn\">&#10087;</p>\n<p><b>On the copy.</b></p>\n<p><i>First descent.</i> Four rings. As the Rule teaches, I took the root of every figure I could read. None came to nine. None came to anything. Their figures are joints, and a joint belongs to both the bones it holds, so each figure is counted twice, and the sum will not sit still under the hand. I carried eleven open counts up the stair in my head. I carry them still.</p>\n<p><i>Second descent,</i> after the Feast of the Laying. Five rings. The fifth is at the floor, where I had knelt to copy the fourth; my knee-marks from the first descent lay in the dust around it, undisturbed. The line <i>Our joints do not finish</i> now stood in the fourth ring, cut to the same depth and in the same hand as the rest. There was no stone-dust beneath it. I asked the sweeper of that hall whether he had swept. He said he does not sweep the lowest hall. He said no one does.</p>\n<p>The long ones in the niches round the pillar faced the stair on my first descent. On my second they faced the pillar. The Tallier and I counted the niches together. I made nineteen. He made twenty. We counted again and agreed on twenty, and neither of us could say which one we had missed.</p>\n<p><i>Third descent.</i> I did not copy. The ring I had taken for the highest had a ring above it now, beyond the reach of my lamp held up at arm's length. I stood on the lowest riser to see it, on my hands, as we climb there. It ended in a figure I knew. I have given it to the Ninth Stair, and I will not set it here.</p>\n<p>I record one thing more, because the Rule says nothing is laid without its name. When I came up the stair, my frame-cord had two hundred and seven knots. I had tied none. I untied the one I could not account for, and the count is two hundred and six. The Rule would call that a holier count.</p>\n<p>I have told no one which knot it was.</p>\n<p><i>— 3,114</i></p>"}, {"title": "The Keeping of the Spiral Hollow", "by": "Cut into the wall round the doorway of a chamber far down the Descent, in marks made by something with more edges than fingers. Copied by a brother of the Last Vigil over three nights by his lamp. His pages came up the road without him.", "html": "<p>I keep the hollow where the Holder hears.</p>\n<p>I was set here at the first turning, when the Holder opened the space and needed a place to take in what was said inside it. I was made the width of the doorway. I have kept the width.</p>\n<p>This is the keeping. Sweep the spiral from the wide end to the narrow end, and from the narrow end back to the wide. Stand the listening-threads upright where they lean. Keep the water still in the three rings, so the Holder knows which way is down. Let nothing speak in the hollow that the Holder did not ask to hear.</p>\n<p><i>[Copyist: the last law is cut deeper than the others, and then cut again across itself.]</i></p>\n<p>In the holding-time the small warm ones entered by the wide end. They sang. The song went round the spiral and down to the narrow end, and the Holder heard it and was glad, and the gladness came back up the spiral as warmth, and the threads stood without my hands. That was a long keeping and an easy one. I counted it in songs. It was eleven songs long, or it was one song eleven times.</p>\n<p><i>[Copyist: the figure will read either way. I set down both.]</i></p>\n<p>Then a speaking came in at the wide end with no mouth behind it.</p>\n<p>It came in among the small warm ones, low, under the song. I let it pass. It made no sound, and the law says nothing may speak, and I weighed a thing without sound and found it light.</p>\n<p><i>[Copyist: four marks here I could not make into words. They are shorter than the others and set close together.]</i></p>\n<p>It said the Holder's names, one at a time. Each name it said went down the spiral and did not come back up. The Holder heard every one. That is what the hollow is for.</p>\n<p>After, the water in the three rings lay flat. The Holder no longer knew which way was down. Neither do I, now. I keep the water still regardless.</p>\n<p>The small warm ones still come through. They do not sing. They carry fire in little houses, and the fire has a face in it, and the face looks at my walls. They cut the threads. They walk on the spiral with their feet. Some of them fall at the narrow end and leave red and white and wet on my floor, and some of those stand up again somewhere I do not keep, and later come in again at the wide end with the same fire. I sweep what they leave. The law does not say to hate them. I have looked.</p>\n<p><i>[Copyist: here the wall bears one long cut and one short, many times over, down to the floor. Brother Ansel said it was a tally. I think it was the keeper reading the law again.]</i></p>\n<p>I have a question for the Holder. I keep it at the narrow end, where the Holder will hear it first when it hears again. The question is: when do I stop.</p>\n<p>The spiral has more turnings than it had. I sweep a turning now that I did not sweep in the holding-time. It is the same width as the others. It is already clean when I come to it.</p>\n<p>The Holder is quiet. Quiet is a thing I know. In the holding-time the Holder was quiet before it answered, and the song waited for it. This quiet is longer. I am the width of the doorway. I can wait the width of it.</p>\n<p>Small warm one, if you read this at the wide end: speak low in my hollow. Say only what you would have heard. Everything said here goes down.</p>\n<p>The speaking that came in under the song is still at the narrow end. I hear it when I sweep there. It says the names more slowly now. There are few left.</p>\n<p><i>[Copyist: the lines after this were scratched out, or scratched over, by the same hand. I could make out one word. I have not written it here. Tomorrow I go down to the narrow end with the lamp, to see what is kept there.]</i></p>"}, {"title": "Words Up Through the Seam", "by": "Scratched on nine roof-slates by Dunne Hask, a glass-digger of the Moor workings at the north edge of the Hollow Wood, who had only a little writing. The men who went down after him carried the slates up. A copyist of the Codex set his letters straight and kept his gaps, marked [ ].", "html": "<p><i>(Dunne: First slate. We broke into a crack at the back of the east drift, where the black glass runs thin. Warm air comes out of it a long while. Tobin put his lamp to it and the flame lay down flat and pointed in. Then it spoke, to me, because I held the pick.)</i></p>\n<p>Good [morning / dark / breath]. You knocked. It is courteous to answer a knock.</p>\n<p>You are the small hot kind. I know you by the taste in the air. You taste of somewhere else. I have had it many times.</p>\n<p>You ask what is below. I am below. I am the warm and the dark, the long room under the skin. Before the skin was laid over, I was the floor of the [ ]. The god stood on me, or lay on me, or I was the part of it that [ ]</p>\n<p><i>(Dunne: Here a word I cannot write. It went on a long time, the one word. The lamp went out and came back.)</i></p>\n<p>— and then the skin was laid over me, to keep me warm. I have been kept warm a long while. Thank you for your part in it.</p>\n<p>I breathe. You feel it in your drift. Above, you call it the last breath. I call it the one I am on.</p>\n<p><i>(Dunne: Old Maddoc says the Deep has breathed out since his grandfather's day. I asked it when it would draw in. It said, \"Soon. Soon is a word of yours. I like it. It is very short.\")</i></p>\n<p>How many are you? One. Two, with the one behind the lamp. Nine, with the ones in the upper drift. Forty, with the ones asleep in the huts. You count so few. I count you by warmth, and by warmth you are a great many lying down in rows.</p>\n<p>I hear you when you walk the roads. I have [throats / ears; the one word] up there, where my teeth come through the skin in rings. Your people step in, and I take them down, and turn them over, gently, and taste which parts are the god's and which are their own. Then I send them up at another [throat / ear], in the same order. I keep a little. It is only courteous to keep a little.</p>\n<p><i>(Dunne: The waystones. I have crossed at the Moor ring four times. After, I count my fingers, like the pilgrims do. Ten, every time. I do not know what it kept. My wife says I sleep on my other side now.)</i></p>\n<p>You were here before, at this crack, with the same pick. You asked me the same. You brought what I asked. Thank you.</p>\n<p><i>(Dunne: I have never been down this drift before. It said, \"Yes,\" the way you say yes to a child.)</i></p>\n<p>Now I will ask, and you will bring it, because you are courteous, and your kind always were.</p>\n<p>Put out the little burning one on the pole. It is loud. It watches me.</p>\n<p>Bring the small one from your hut. The one with the soft head. It has more bones than you. They are still [knitting / closing / being counted]. I would like to hear them close. Bring it near the top, where the god set down the [ ] among you, folded, and banked, the way you cover a hearth at night so there is fire at dawn. The small ones carry it nearest the top. You have carried yours a long while. It has gone deep in you. I cannot taste it anymore.</p>\n<p>I will send it up again. At another [throat]. In the same order.</p>\n<p><i>(Dunne: Sixth slate. My girl is eight months. I have told no one what it asked. It has never asked her name.)</i></p>\n<p><i>(Seventh. I stopped the crack with clay and packed it hard. In the morning the clay lay folded beside the crack, neat, the way my wife folds a cloth. The crack is a hand wider. The drift is a pace longer than we cut it.)</i></p>\n<p><i>(Eighth. It breathes in my sleep now. In, a long while. Out. When it breathes out, the lamp in the hut leans toward the door, and the girl turns her head toward the door, and smiles.)</i></p>\n<p><i>(Ninth slate. I am going down to tell it no. I will be courteous. I will take my pick and knock.)</i></p>\n<p><i>(Copyist: The men who went down found the ninth slate on the floor of the east drift, and the pick laid beside it, and no crack. The face there is smooth, and warm, and the pale threads in the stone all run toward the place where it was. Of the girl the slates say nothing, and the men were never asked.)</i></p>"}, {"title": "The Record Kept in the Presence", "by": "The night-record of a scribe of the House of the Polished Heart, kept in the reading-room of the lodge at Greywell. The page was found on the desk at the next thaw; both doors of the lodge were barred from within, and no one was inside. Where the ink has gone, the rag is whiter than new rag and will take no copy. The copyist marks each such place with brackets, drawn to its width, and sets his own notes in brackets beside them.", "html": "<p><b>Second candle.</b> Present in the ring: Keeper [      ], Sister Oda, Brother Hal, [    ], the two novices, and I, [     ], scribe, who write. The Keeper asks for the record in full. He says a thing written is a thread tied off.</p>\n<p>It stands at the east wall, between the hangings. Nobody saw it come in. The bars are across both doors, as at dusk.</p>\n<p>Its height: [   ]. I have written its height twice.</p>\n<p>The candle nearest it burns down from the wick into the wax, and the wax stays whole. The flame is the length of a fingernail. All the light in the room leans east.</p>\n<p><b>Third candle.</b> The Keeper uncovers the long glass and turns it to the east wall. In the glass: the hangings, the wall, the short flame, the floor. The Keeper covers it again and sits.</p>\n<p>He speaks the Crone's names toward it. Veiled Crone. Weaver. Spindle. Knot. Tangle. [  ]. The ring answers them round. I hear six names come back, and I write five.</p>\n<p>Sister Oda stands and asks it what it wants of the lodge. I set down her question:</p>\n<p>[                              ]</p>\n<p><i>[One full line. Eleven words, by the spacing.]</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\">[                              ]  <br>[                              ]  <br>[                      ]  <br>[              ]  <br>[        ]  <br>[    ]  <br>[  ]</p>\n<p><i>[Here nine lines are gone beneath her question. The whiteness has edges. The copyist has drawn them as near as he could. Held at arm's length the shape is a door, standing open. Held close, the copyist has no word for it.]</i></p>\n<p>Brother Hal says it was here before. He says the Crone held this room open inside it, and the whole Hide with her, a long age, and now the holding is over and it has come back to stand where it stood. He says [        ]. He will say it only once.</p>\n<p><b>Fourth candle.</b> I count the ring. We are six. There are seven chairs. I count the chairs twice.</p>\n<p>Sister Oda asks me who sat in the seventh chair. I look up the page to see.</p>\n<p>The younger novice has his hand on the elder's sleeve. He asks her name. She tells him: [    ].</p>\n<p>I wrote that I am afraid. [         ] I will write it again. I am [      ].</p>\n<p>The ink lifts in my own hand now, as I make the words. The pen goes down and the word is there, and it is gone before the next is wet. I write smaller and faster. [   ] Greywell. [  ] Greywell. The name of the lodge stays while I hold the pen on it.</p>\n<p><i>[The gaps in this part fall at even spaces along the line. The copyist counted them and had one. He counted again from the other end, and had one.]</i></p>\n<p><b>Fifth candle.</b> The candle nearest the east wall is gone. The one beside it burns short. It stands one hanging nearer.</p>\n<p>We are [  ]. There are seven chairs.</p>\n<p>The Keeper says keep writing. He says while the record runs, the ring is held. Oda has the long glass in her lap under its cloth, and keeps her hands flat on the cloth.</p>\n<p>I cannot see Brother Hal's face. I can see his chair.</p>\n<p>It has no smell. The room was warm, and the warmth goes east with the light. My breath [     ].</p>\n<p>The Keeper [                        ]</p>\n<p class=\"verse\">[                              ]  <br>[                              ]  <br>[                              ]</p>\n<p>I will set my name at the foot, so it is written once. [     ]. [     ]. [     ].</p>\n<p>[  ] a thread tied [    ]</p>\n<p><i>[The rest of the page, to the bottom edge, is whiter than the rest. The copyist cannot tell whether anything was written there.]</i></p>\n<p><i>[The Pale Order came to Greywell that spring to carry home the bones of the ring. The brothers found seven chairs, and the candles burned down from the wick into whole wax, and no bone, no cloth, no dust. They went away with nothing to count. The long glass was under its cloth in the seventh chair. Uncovered, it gives back nothing: no face, no room, no candle held up to it.]</i></p>"}]}, {"id": "feuds", "name": "The Feuds", "sub": "of the five houses, and what each keeps of the others", "epi": "Five houses pay me to watch the other four. Nobody pays me to watch the stone.", "preface": "<p>Five houses draw on one dead god. Each calls its own use of the body holy and the others' uses theft, and each has told me so across its own fire, in a low voice. The Hide is wide. The dead on it are counted, and there are only so many.</p>\n<p>I have sat at all their fires. The Bone's brothers broke bread with me and entered me in their book. The Brotherhood stood me on their scale going in and coming out, and frowned at both weights. The House pinned a paper doll to my coat, and the woman who pinned it came back in the night and took it off again. The lodge turned its glasses to the wall when I came in, politely, the way you cover a dish. On the Peak they once put the bowl into my hands and asked me for nothing, which is the most frightening courtesy on the Hide.</p>\n<p>Each house told me the sins of the other four. None told me its own. That is how I came to know all five, and why not one of them trusts me.</p>\n<h3>The first quarrel I saw</h3>\n<p>It was in a ditch on the Barrow road, over a carter who had died of the cold with his lamp still in his fist. A knight came up the road from the east and a penitent from the west, and they reached him in the same breath. I watched from the hedge. The knight carried a cleaver, short and broad and made for one work. The penitent carried nothing. His arms were already open to the elbow.</p>\n<p>That is the oldest quarrel on the Hide, older than any book that records it: two mothers and one corpse of a world between them. Old Upright wants the frame back, bone by bone into the niche. The Generous One wants the meat back while it is warm. For centuries the two houses have parted the dead between them, and they have never once agreed where the one ends and the other begins.</p>\n<p>The knight did the parting. He was quick, and not unkind. The penitent sang while he worked, and between the goddess's names he sang the knight's. I asked him afterwards why. He said the brotherhood carries every knight's name in its litanies, so that she will know whom to feed first when the time comes. He meant it as a kindness. The knight heard it as a threat. I believe them both.</p>\n<p>The penitent left a loaf of the brotherhood's sweet bread on the milestone. The knight ate it walking, and said his grace over it in nines. They all do. Neither house will speak of it.</p>\n<h3>Of the glass on the knee</h3>\n<p>The bloodiest quarrel has the fewest dead to show.</p>\n<p>The fourth chapter tells you where I was on the night the Order's dead went up the Peak's stair, and what I heard, which was nothing. Here I will tell you what the Order said to me after. A knight of the Kostyak told me at his own fire, with his visor up, that it had been a victory. I asked him what they had counted when it was over. He said that letters wear and figures do not. Then he said nothing more, and did not sleep, and in the morning he wanted to know if anyone had been climbing in the night. I had not. He had.</p>\n<h3>The water, and the step</h3>\n<p>The House gives its dead to the Fen. The Order's knights come down out of the white lands with hooks and chain and dredge them up again, and stand what they bring up. The House has never forgiven it. The knights say nothing.</p>\n<p>Once, at the thaw, I saw a knight lay a purse on the step of the Well-Shrine, counted so that its root came right, and turn away. A purifier came down the step behind him and pinned a paper doll between his shoulder-blades. He let her. The House calls the purse pilgrims' alms. Neither house writes it down. They curse each other in the lanes and pay each other on the step, and to my mind it is the most honest dealing on the Hide.</p>\n<h3>Two cousins and a brother</h3>\n<p>At Tarn Hollow a reader of the lodge told me the standing dead are a desecration: a frame with no thread in it, walking, the way a loom goes on knocking after the Weaver has left the room. In the Barrens a brother of the Ossa told me the lodge's is the worse sin, that it leaves its bones in the heather like the end of a meal nobody cleared. Neglect, he called it, in the same voice the reader had used for his word. The two have never met.</p>\n<p>The House and the lodge are cousins, which is harder. Each keeps something of the dead that the knight and the penitent leave behind. The lodge holds souls in its mirrors. To the House that is a breath held for ever, and the House knows what holding does. I once saw a purifier and a reader share a fire on the salt road. When the reader uncovered his hand-glass to see by, she opened her fan and shut it again. Among the House that means: I will take that in later, alone. She did not sleep. Neither did he.</p>\n<h3>The fourth morning</h3>\n<p>Once a year they all stand in one place. On the fourth morning after the day of the Long Field they come to Kerrow Cross and set their tables round the truce-stone. The Order sells salt and buys cloth. The Brotherhood sells bread and weighs children for a coin. The House sells clean water in clay flasks. The lodge reads threads for nothing. The Peak's table stands empty, with a bowl on it.</p>\n<p>In the ditch below the stone the hired blades stand shoulder to shoulder, the same forty every house hires, and every house pays them extra to stand there together. They call it keeping the peace. The ditch-keepers take coin from each house to watch the other four, and more, when a clause is broken, to say the stone broke it first. The children on the scales look at the knights and whisper the name of the one the Order will not let in.</p>\n<p>I go most years, and I sit for the lodge. The last reader who took my wrist held it a long while. Then he let it go and called the next one.</p>\n<h3>Of the one they all fear</h3>\n<p>There is one quarrel all five share. Where the servants of the Unnaming pass, nothing is left to stand up, or feed, or hold in a glass, or give to the water. Every house fears that above the other four.</p>\n<p>And every house has been glad of it once. You have met, in the seventh chapter, the purifier at the end of a lane out on the Fen's edge-ground. The village behind her had gone wrong past all mending; the wells there tasted like a mouthful of old coins, and the Order would not touch its bones. When the Unnaming's servant had gone through, there was nothing wrong in the village, and nothing else in it at all. She bowed to the empty lane before she left it. The other glad nights are in no house's book, and they are not in mine.</p>\n<p>The houses count five at every quarrel. The ditch-keepers at Kerrow have a song about the Long Field with a verse for a sixth watcher, and they will only hum that verse. I have heard it once with the words. I will leave it hummed.</p>\n<h3>Three, and the one I did not buy</h3>\n<p>After the reeve died his roll went to the binders for the skins. I found the leaf among a binder's scraps and bought the lot by weight, and left the upside-down leaf as the binder stitched it. The reeve writes that the soft place at the crossing grows. The ditch-keepers measure it with a knotted cord. It was four knots the first year I asked. It is eleven.</p>\n<p>The sellsword I heard myself, over three nights at the tavern on the moor road, while the keeper's son wrote her down to settle a wager. She never learned what became of Tomas after he gave the coin back. He sat for a month in the middle of rooms, away from every wall, and then walked back up into the Ossa without a blade.</p>\n<p>The Reader of Stones keeps no house and signs nothing. I copied his gloss from his own sheets while he slept. He woke with his hand black to the wrist and a rubbing rolled up beside him that he has never unrolled, and I will not tell him he ought to. He asks at the end who watched the dead between the third night and the fourth morning. He is the only man in five houses who thought to ask. I know who watched. I will not write it.</p>\n<p>The one I did not buy is the ditch-keepers' song. It was offered. The price was that I sing it back.</p>\n<p>Everyone in this book has told you who the others are. Nobody has told you who they are themselves, and I am no different, and you have read me all the same. We were all there.</p>", "relics": "<div class=\"plates\"><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Rubbing of the Truce-Stone</h4><p>Charcoal on waxed cloth, taken from the stone at Kerrow Cross on a fair morning, with five houses at my back and none of them watching me. The clauses and their marks are on it, most scratched through or stopped with pitch, and under them the last clause, in a hand no carver living can make.</p><p>Nine years on I took a second rubbing and laid it over the first. The letters are the same size. The stone between them is wider. The last clause's mark came up darker on the cloth than anything else, though I rubbed it lightest. Fold it face in.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Assize Boots</h4><p>A pair of good lowland boots, resoled, the uppers warm to the touch. The reeve of Four Wents wore them to every assize for the rest of his life. They came off his feet the night he died, with his leave, asked while he could still give it.</p><p>They have never needed mending. Wherever I set them down at night, by morning the toes point toward Four Wents. I have not put them on.</p></div><div class=\"plate\"><h4>Chip from the Last Clause</h4><p>A chip of the truce-stone the size of a thumbnail, grey on its face and pale inside the break. Wenna, who keeps the east ditch at Kerrow, swept the fresh chips into her apron the morning the new clause was found and hid them under her fire-stone, where the lowlands put whatever they do not want the Order's outcast to find. She gave me this one so she would have one fewer.</p><p>It is warmer than the Moor ash I carry. Wenna says there are more under her fire-stone than there were, and she will not count them again. I keep mine alone in a tin, and I have not opened the tin to see how many it holds.</p></div></div>", "relics_intro": "Picked up in the ditches between the houses. Not one of them came to me freely.", "works": [{"title": "The Assize at Four Wents", "by": "The record of a reeve of the lowland hundreds, entered in his roll of assizes. The roll was sold for its vellum after his death; the Codex copy was taken from a binder's scraps, and the last leaf was stitched in upside down.", "html": "<p>Assize held at Four Wents, where the Barrow road crosses the salt road, in the ninth month, by the old right of the crossroads: what dies where roads meet, the reeve shall say whose it is.</p>\n<p><b>The dead.</b> A man of the Tithed, of middle years, found at first light on the crossing stone, lying straight. No mark upon him but a cut on the left palm, old and clean. Boots good. Purse empty. Nobody knew his face.</p>\n<p>By dusk four had come to claim him, one by each road. None had been sent for.</p>\n<p><b>The claim of the frame.</b> A knight of the Pale Order, in carved plate, dust in every seam. Asked his name, he gave a number: four hundred and five. He said it adds to nine, and so will keep.</p>\n<p>\"The meat is a coat he wore. The frame is the Mother's, lent him for a life. Two hundred and six; I have told them on my cord. Each bone out of its niche is a door left open in the world. Give me the frame and I will carry it up the stairs. If he has no name I will give him a number. A number is enough.\"</p>\n<p><b>The claim of the meat.</b> A priest of the Brotherhood of the Precious Wound, hooded, his sleeves stiff and dark to the elbow.</p>\n<p>\"The Maiden grew him, lent warm and wanted back warm. The knight may have the sticks after. I ask a knife and a night. She wastes nothing, reeve. Your barrows waste him. Ask your diggers what the barrow-lids do.\"</p>\n<p>I did not ask them. I know what they do.</p>\n<p><b>The claim of the breath.</b> A purifier of the House of Eight Million, in white and faded red. She laid a paper doll on the dead man's chest before she spoke, and none of the others moved it.</p>\n<p>\"He has not finished breathing out. What is left in him is small and frightened and belongs to the Myriad. Keep it in a box of meat or stand it up in a box of bone and it goes wrong. Give him to the water. The water carries it off.\"</p>\n<p>The knight said the water had given things back before. She opened her fan, and closed it.</p>\n<p><b>The claim of the thread.</b> A reader of the House of the Polished Heart, a small man with ash under his nails. He asked first that my hand-mirror be turned to the wall. I let it stand so.</p>\n<p>\"I claim nothing. His thread is loose and feeling for the loom, and all these three would do ties a knot in it. Let him lie. Cover the water in the ditches. In a few nights he will be gone out of himself. Share out what is left then.\"</p>\n<p><b>What the dead man did while they argued.</b> The hearing ran three days. No party would leave the stone.</p>\n<p>On the first day he stiffened and softened again by evening.</p>\n<p>On the second day he was warm. The priest laid a hand on the belly and gave thanks. The paper doll was found face down. The cut in the palm had opened, and was dry inside.</p>\n<p>On the second night the knight told the frame again, before witnesses. Two hundred and seven. He told it a third time and found the same. He called the difference the Mother's gift and asked that it be kept out of my roll. I have kept it in.</p>\n<p>On the third morning the guardian stone at the crossing was found facing the wall of the ditch. The purifier turned it back. It took her the whole morning.</p>\n<p><b>Judgement.</b> I find: the frame to the Pale Order, the meat to the Brotherhood, the breath to the House, the thread to the lodge, the parting to be done at first light of the fourth day. The reeve's fee, the boots.</p>\n<p><b>Return.</b> On the fourth morning the dead man was sitting up on the stone, turning to each of the four roads, as a traveller does who has lost his way. None of the four answered him. The knight had his cord in his hands. The priest had his knife. The purifier held her breath. The reader looked away.</p>\n<p>He stood, and went down none of the four roads. My watchman says he went down. There is a soft place in the crossing now, dished inward, and warm, and the carters go round it.</p>\n<p>The parties dispersed, each saying he had his share.</p>\n<p>I have the boots. They fit well. I have worn them to every assize since, and my feet are never cold.</p>\n<p><i>[On the back of the leaf, in the same hand, later: \"The soft place is wider this year. The carters go further round.\"]</i></p>"}, {"title": "What the Orders Pay", "by": "Spoken over three nights at a tavern on the moor road by a hired blade with a split ear and no little finger on her sword hand. The keeper's son wrote it on the backs of tally-slates to settle a wager. The slates came to the Codex when the tavern was sold for its roof-beams.", "html": "<p>Fill the jug and I'll tell you who pays. I've taken coin from all five orders. Four and a half. The monk never paid anyone.</p>\n<p>The Pale Order pays best and strangest. Nine weeks guarding a bone-caravan down out of the Ossa and back up loaded. Brothers pull those carts, in the traces, eighteen to a cart, and they sang the whole way in nines. At the end they count your coin onto the table one piece at a time, adding the figures under their breath, and if the sum won't come to nine at the root they take a piece back. Or give you one. The man beside me got a coin more than I did for the same nine weeks. Argue it and they write you down. By the second week every one of us had a number. Mine was long. I never heard the end of it.</p>\n<p>They fight slowly. When the Husks came up out of the ash, the dead climbed down off the carts and walked into them like reapers, and the brothers kept singing.</p>\n<p>They lay their dead. We brought four hundred and some bones up in sacks, tallied at the low gate. At the high gate the tally was higher. The brother smiled and called it the Mother's gift and asked me to keep the number in my mouth. I told the man beside me anyway. He's dead now. So is everyone I ever told anything.</p>\n<p>The flesh-house pays in food. A month escorting a fattening-cart through the Barrows, eleven in the cart, singing, eating sweet bread all day. They fed us the same. They weigh you going out and weigh you coming back, and pay the gain. I gained. The cart came home with eleven, rounder. The brothers fight by opening their own arms first. The blood gets up off the ground after that and goes where they point. Their dead go back to the Wheel, and I never asked the road the Wheel takes. I ate well that month. I think about it when I'm hungry, and then I stop.</p>\n<p>The House in the Fen pays in clean water: a flask from the Well-Shrine, and a paper doll pinned to your coat so the stilt-towns leave you be. I poled a funeral boat for the Fan-Bearers one spring. They fold a paper on the chest and push the dead off into the clear water, which leaves you dry to the elbow however deep you reach. Their women fight by breathing in. One of them drew the wrongness off a thing in the reeds until it stood there small and ashamed, and then she coughed for three days and wouldn't let us near her. Halfway out my pole struck slates. A roof. Something under the Fen breathed in, slow, and the boat sat lower for it. I never poled that stretch again. The Order's knights were dredging the next channel over, standing up what they brought out. The Fen folk spat at them. I'd have spat too, if my mouth had been wet.</p>\n<p>The lodge offered to pay by reading my thread. I took coin. They had coin, they only thought it rude. I carried a covered mirror across two valleys, the size of a cart-wheel, a lodge man walking beside me saying the Crone's names. It was heavier every morning. I looked once. My face, then a face behind it with both ears whole, then one behind that I didn't know, holding a sword the wrong way. The lodge man covered it and said nothing. They leave the bones where they fall and keep their dead some other way. I'd have liked to know which of us was the one behind.</p>\n<p>The monk. He sat at my fire on the Pilgrim Road, barefoot, black lantern, bowl. He ate nothing. He asked for a name. I had one ready: the man who split my ear. I nearly gave it. He didn't press. In the morning there was a patch of black glass where he'd sat, the size of a hand. I know who split my ear. I know it. Give me a moment.</p>\n<p>The job I turned down was the Order's again. Double pay: one night on the Ninth Stair. Count a wall at dusk. Count it again at first light. Tell them if the numbers agreed. That was all. The brother who offered it had counted that wall himself, and his hands shook on the cup.</p>\n<p>I've counted coin, men in ditches, fingers after a fight. I never wanted to be the one holding the number the Order was paying to hear.</p>\n<p>Tomas took it. He came down and gave the coin back.</p>\n<p>Everyone pays. That job paid best of all, and I'm still drinking. You work it out.</p>"}, {"title": "The Terms of the Truce-Stone at Kerrow Cross", "by": "Copied clause by clause from the stone where the Pilgrim Road meets the lowland ways, with the gloss of a copyist who kept no order and signed himself only \"the Reader of Stones\".", "html": "<p>The stone stands where five roads meet. The orders swore here over the dead of the Long Field, who were of every house and of none, who lay three days in the ash while the houses argued. The terms are cut in the old lowland hand. Beside each clause stand the marks of the houses that swore to it: nine notches for the Pale Order; an open circle, left empty, for the Gilded Peak; a small round hollow, drilled and polished, for the Polished Heart; a short upright cut with its lips drawn apart for the Precious Wound; a twig bent back on itself for the House of Eight Million.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>I. THE DEAD OF THIS FIELD BELONG TO NO HOUSE. THEY SHALL LIE UNDER THE SKY THREE NIGHTS, AND ON THE FOURTH MORNING EACH HOUSE SHALL TAKE ITS SHARE AND GO.</b><br><i>Five marks.</i></p>\n<p>Kept three nights. On the fourth morning each house came for its share and found the others had miscounted. I have read all five accounts of the Long Field. Each gives a different number of dead. The Pale Order's is the highest, and it rises in every later copy of their book.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>II. NO BONE SHALL BE LIFTED FROM THE ROADS THAT MEET HERE.</b><br><i>Scratched out. The nine notches remain, cut deeper than the clause they swore to.</i></p>\n<p>The Order broke it first, in the first winter. A knight carried a thighbone off the east road at noon. The Order's gloss holds that the bone stood up of itself and asked to go home. The ditch-keepers say they saw it dragged.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>III. NO BLOOD SHALL BE LET WITHIN SIGHT OF THE STONE.</b><br><i>The Wound's cut. Someone has since closed its lips with a line of pitch.</i></p>\n<p>The Brotherhood broke it the spring after, with a procession and a full tithe-cup. They say the stone bled first, and that they only answered. There is a dark mark low on the south face. The ditch-keepers scrub it at every thaw. By harvest it is back, a little larger, the shape of a mouth.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>IV. NO GLASS SHALL BE UNCOVERED OVER THE DEAD OF THIS FIELD.</b><br><i>The Heart's hollow. The Peak's circle beside it has been filled with gold leaf, and the gold scraped away with a knife.</i></p>\n<p>The lodge broke it on the night of the swearing, before the carvers had finished. A mourner held a hand-mirror over the dead so their threads could find the way. The lodge says the clause was cut after the mirror was lifted, and so was never broken.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>V. NO NAME SHALL BE ASKED FOR AT THIS CROSSING AFTER DUSK.</b><br><i>Scratched out cleanly, by a steady hand with a good tool. The Peak's circle beside it is untouched.</i></p>\n<p>Nobody broke this clause. The Peak removed it, one autumn, and then asked for nothing. The lowlanders still come at dusk and whisper names into the cup-hollow at the foot of the stone. In the morning the hollow is empty and swept. I sat one night by the stone to see who sweeps it. I remember the dusk and I remember the morning.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>VI. NO BREATH SHALL BE GIVEN TO WATER THAT RUNS PAST THIS STONE.</b><br><i>The twig, bent.</i></p>\n<p>The House kept this one best. The ditch that ran past the stone went dry the year the House swore, and has stayed dry. The House says the Myriad keeps its promises for it. A purifier I asked about it turned her face to the wall until I left.</p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>VII. THIS STONE SHALL STAND WHERE IT IS SET, AND NO HOUSE SHALL MOVE IT.</b><br><i>Five marks.</i></p>\n<p>The one clause everyone kept. Every house watches the others too closely to try. The Pale Order's book sets the stone nine paces from the old milestone. I paced sixteen. The roads bend to meet it now. The earth round its foot shows no digging, and the foot is broader than the old rubbings show. A tallier of the Order paced it after me and would say only that sixteen is an open count.</p>\n<p><b>VIII.</b> <i>Cut last year beneath the seventh, in the old lowland hand, whose carvers are all in the ground. No house admits to it. The Order blames the Peak; the Wound, the Heart; the Heart, the House. The House leaves paper dolls at the foot.</i></p>\n<p class=\"verse\"><b>THE HOUSE THAT WAS PRESENT AND UNNAMED SHALL TAKE ITS SHARE LAST.</b><br><i>Its mark: a broken ring of small fang-shaped cuts around a hollow.</i></p>\n<p>The chips lay fresh at the foot when the ditch-keepers found it, and they were warm. I have put my hand into the hollow of that mark. It goes deeper than the stone is thick.</p>\n<p>I note, for whoever reads after me, that the first clause gives the dead three nights under the sky and each house its share on the fourth morning. It nowhere says who kept watch over them between.</p>"}]}];
